/* Machine translations of meier in the manner of Kemp Smith: see translation-briefs.js. Not the translators' own work. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['meier/kemp'] = {"meier:1":["Logic, or the art of reason (*logica, philosophia instrumentalis, philosophia rationalis*), is a science which treats of the rules of learned knowledge and of learned exposition [*Vortrag*].",[]],"meier:2":["If logic is not to contain laws that are wholly arbitrary, artificial and unnatural, its rules must be derived (1) from experience of the operations of human reason, (2) from the nature of human reason, and (3) from the universal fundamental truths on which the whole of human knowledge rests.",[]],"meier:3":["The aim of logic is the perfection of learned knowledge and of learned exposition. This perfection is either of the kind that befits only those who are learned by profession, or of the kind that is also becoming and serviceable to other learned persons.",[]],"meier:4":["Logic is a means without which no one can attain learned knowledge and science, and through the proper use of which learned knowledge and science are attained, § 1.",[]],"meier:5":["Philosophy (*philosophia*) is a science of the more universal properties of things, in so far as they are known without faith [*Glauben*]. Now since learned knowledge and learned exposition comprise many species under them, their perfections and imperfections are more universal properties of things; and these are completely proved in logic, without their truth being derived from testimony. Logic is therefore a part of philosophy.",[]],"meier:6":["Logic treats either of learned knowledge that is completely certain, and of its exposition, or of probable learned knowledge and its exposition, § 1. The former is the logic of wholly certain learned knowledge (*analytica*); the latter is the logic of probable learned knowledge (*dialectica, logica probabilium*). It is the former logic that we shall treat.",[]],"meier:7":["In logic the rules of learned knowledge and of learned exposition are either applied to the particular species of these, or they are not. The former is practical logic (*logica practica, utens*); the latter is theoretical logic (*logica theoretica, docens*).",[]],"meier:8":["If logic is so constituted as by its nature it can and ought to be, it has, among others, a threefold use. (1) It furthers the learning and extension of all the sciences and of learning as a whole. Every student ought, therefore, in fairness to begin with the study of logic. (2) It improves the understanding and reason, and shows how these cognitive powers [*Erkenntnisskräfte*] must be employed if truth is to be known in a proper manner. (3) It furthers virtue as a whole, since it improves the free will, supplies that knowledge on which virtue rests, and exercises an indispensable influence upon the improvement of conscience.",[]],"meier:9":["Logic treats: I. Of learned knowledge. 1. Of learned knowledge in general. 2. Of the extent of learned knowledge. 3. Of the magnitude of learned knowledge. 4. Of the truth of learned knowledge. 5. Of the clearness of learned knowledge. 6. Of the certainty of learned knowledge. 7. Of practical learned knowledge. 8. Of learned concepts. 9. Of learned judgments. 10. Of learned syllogisms. II. Of the method [*Lehrart*] of learned knowledge. III. Of learned exposition. 1. Of the use of words. 2. Of the learned style of writing. 3. Of a learned speech. 4. Of learned writings. IV. Of the character of a learned man. Logic. Of learned knowledge. Of learned knowledge in general.",[]],"meier:10":["Experience teaches that we represent to ourselves an infinite number of things. A representation (*repraesentatio, perceptio*) is like a picture which the soul's skill as a painter draws within it.",[]],"meier:11":["Knowledge (*cognitio*) is either a sum of many representations, or the act by which a representation of a thing is produced. Representations and knowledge may also be taken as one and the same, without fear of any appreciable error.",[]],"meier:12":["Distinct from representation and knowledge is that which we represent to ourselves and that which we know. This latter is entitled the object of knowledge and of representation (*obiectum cognitionis et repraesentationis*).",[]],"meier:13":["We are conscious of our representations and of our knowledge (*conscium esse, adpercipere*) in so far as we distinguish them and their object from other representations and things. Consciousness is a twofold representation: a representation of the object, and a representation of its difference from others. Consciousness is like light in the corporeal world, which discloses to us the difference between bodies.",[]],"meier:14":["When we are conscious of a representation, we are conscious of it either merely as a whole, so that we distinguish nothing within it, or we are conscious also of the manifold in it. In the first case we have an indistinct or confused knowledge (*cognitio indistincta et confusa*); in the second, a distinct knowledge (*cognitio distincta*). For example, when we see a man at a distance, our knowledge of his face is indistinct so long as we do not discern its parts and features. But when he comes nearer and we begin to make out his eyes, his nose and the features of his face, we obtain a distinct knowledge of it.",[]],"meier:15":["That from which a thing, whether it be a knowledge or the object of a knowledge, can be known is its ground (*ratio*); and what can be known from the ground is its consequence (*rationatum*). Light is the ground of the visibility of bodies, and this visibility is a consequence of light. The connection of things (*nexus, consequentia*) consists in this, that the one is the ground of the other or contains it within itself. The ground of a thing is either such that we can derive from it our whole knowledge of the thing, so that nothing further is required in order to know all that is to be found in the thing, or it is not so. The former is the sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*), the latter the insufficient ground (*ratio insufficiens*).",[]],"meier:16":["Everything that is possible and actual has a ground; and everything has also a sufficient ground.",[]],"meier:17":["When we know something, we know it either distinctly from grounds or not. In the first case we have a rational knowledge (*cognitio rationalis*). Such knowledge requires three things: (1) a knowledge of a thing, (2) a knowledge of its ground, and (3) a distinct knowledge of the connection of the thing with its ground. For example, I know that all men can err because they have a limited understanding, and I think: whoever has a limited understanding can err; now all men have a limited understanding; therefore they can all err. I then have a rational knowledge of the truth that all men can err.",[]],"meier:18":["Every knowledge, in so far as it is not rational, is entitled common or historical knowledge (*cognitio vulgaris, historica*). All things can be known historically, and we may even know their grounds; but so long as we do not distinctly discern the connection of the consequences with their grounds, our knowledge is merely historical.",[]],"meier:19":["A more perfect historical knowledge is a beautiful knowledge (*cognitio pulcra, aesthetica*); and the fine sciences [*schönen Wissenschaften*] are concerned with the rules by the observance of which historical knowledge is made beautiful.",[]],"meier:20":["Historical knowledge differs greatly from rational knowledge, §§ 17, 18, so much so that even the most beautiful historical knowledge does not deserve to be called rational, § 19. Yet the former is indispensable to the latter, since no man can attain a rational knowledge of a thing unless he first possesses a historical knowledge of it.",[]],"meier:21":["Learned and philosophical knowledge (*cognitio erudita et philosophica*) is a rational knowledge which is perfect in a higher or more notable degree.",[]],"meier:22":["When the manifold in a knowledge agrees towards one end, or contains the sufficient ground of it, therein consists the perfection of the knowledge (*perfectio cognitionis*). The perfections of knowledge belong to it either in so far as it is distinct or in so far as it is indistinct, § 14. The former are entitled the logical perfections of knowledge (*perfectio cognitionis logica*), the latter its beauties (*pulcritudo et perfectio aesthetica cognitionis*). For example, mathematical certainty is a logical perfection, and pictorial vividness a beauty of knowledge.",[]],"meier:23":["In so far as a knowledge is not perfect, it is an imperfect knowledge (*imperfectio cognitionis*). The imperfections of knowledge belong to it either in so far as it is distinct or in so far as it is indistinct, § 14. The former are entitled the logical imperfections of knowledge (*imperfectio cognitionis logica*), the latter its uglinesses (*deformitas, imperfectio cognitionis aesthetica*). For example, the swinish and smutty element in everyday jests is an ugliness of knowledge; a false syllogism, on the other hand, is a logical imperfection of it.",[]],"meier:24":["Learned knowledge must be adorned with the perfections of knowledge, §§ 21, 22. Consequently it possesses either merely the logical perfections of knowledge, being either not beautiful at all or even ugly as well; or it possesses, besides the logical perfections, the beauties of knowledge, §§ 22, 23. The former is a merely learned knowledge (*cognitio mere erudita*); the latter a knowledge that is at once beautiful and learned (*cognitio aesthetico-logica*). The latter is more perfect than the former, and the former is not to be sought by itself alone.",[]],"meier:25":["The more we know, the more perfect is our knowledge, § 22. The first perfection of learned knowledge thus consists in its extent (*vastitas, ubertas cognitionis eruditae*), which is ascribed to a knowledge in so far as it represents to us many objects.",[]],"meier:26":["The greater and weightier our knowledge, the more perfect it is, since a great thing comprises much within it, § 22. The second perfection of learned knowledge accordingly consists in its magnitude and weight (*dignitas, magnitudo et maiestas cognitionis eruditae*), which belongs to a knowledge in so far as it is great and weighty. For example, the knowledge of God is weightier than the knowledge of the clothing of the Romans.",[]],"meier:27":["Since a false knowledge is no knowledge at all, the truth of knowledge (*veritas cognitionis eruditae*) is its third perfection. It may be entitled the fundamental perfection of knowledge, since without it knowledge is no knowledge at all, and so is capable of no perfection.",[]],"meier:28":["Since in a distinct representation we represent more to ourselves than in an indistinct one, § 14, the distinctness of learned knowledge is its fourth perfection, § 25.",[]],"meier:29":["The consciousness of the truth of a knowledge is its certainty (*certitudo subiective spectata*). Now since both the truth of knowledge and the consciousness of it are a perfection, §§ 13, 17, the certainty of learned knowledge is its fifth perfection.",[]],"meier:30":["A learned knowledge is practical in so far as it contributes its share to the ordering of our free actions (*cognitio erudita practica*); and therein consists its sixth perfection, § 22.",[]],"meier:31":["The more extensive, weighty, correct, distinct, certain and practical a learned knowledge is, the more perfect it is, §§ 25–30. Now since every rational man must at all times strive after the greatest perfection possible to him, he must, when he seeks to attain learned knowledge, endeavour (1) to achieve all its logical perfections, (2) each of them in the highest possible degree, and in addition (3) [to achieve] the beauties of knowledge, § 22.",[]],"meier:32":["Whoever, therefore, would attain the most perfect learned knowledge must not be content with a merely learned knowledge, §§ 24, 31. Though not all his learned representations can at the same time be beautiful, his learned knowledge, regarded as a whole, must also be a beautiful knowledge, if it is to be improved in as high a degree as possible.",[]],"meier:33":["A learned knowledge can be (1) logically perfect and imperfect at the same time; for example, a correct, distinct and certain knowledge may have the defect of not being practical; (2) logically perfect in a higher degree than it is imperfect, or more imperfect than perfect; (3) logically perfect and at the same time beautiful or ugly; (4) logically perfect and neither beautiful nor ugly; (5) logically imperfect and at the same time beautiful or ugly, §§ 22, 23.",[]],"meier:34":["Whoever would attain a learned knowledge that is at the same time beautiful, § 32, (1) must not make it logically perfect in such a manner and degree that all beauty in it is thereby prevented; (2) must not embellish it in such a manner and degree that the requisite logical perfection of it is thereby rendered impossible; (3) must seek chiefly to secure the logical perfections, and must introduce the beauties more sparingly, as an ornament.",[]],"meier:35":["If certain perfections in learned knowledge cannot be attained together, we must let go the lesser and less necessary perfections in order to secure the greater and more necessary. In a learned knowledge which is not meant to be merely learned, we must therefore often relax logical strictness in trifles, in order to attain the greater beauty of the knowledge.",[]],"meier:36":["The imperfections of learned knowledge are either defects or faults. A defect of learned knowledge (*defectus cognitionis eruditae*) arises when certain rules of its perfection are neither observed nor transgressed. For example, when a principal concept is not defined at all, the rules of definition are neither observed nor transgressed. A fault of learned knowledge (*vitium cognitionis eruditae*) arises when the rules of its perfection are transgressed; for example, when a concept is wrongly defined. Though all defects and faults must be avoided, we must guard more against the latter. For it may be said that every fault is bound up with a defect, and a fault is therefore a greater imperfection than a mere defect.",[]],"meier:37":["Common and historical knowledge can be much more perfect than merely learned knowledge, namely when it is very beautiful, §§ 22, 23. For example, an unlearned general or minister may possess a far more perfect knowledge than a learned and pedantic bookworm. But this advantage belongs to common knowledge only contingently, when learned knowledge is not as perfect as it could and should be.",[]],"meier:38":["Learned knowledge is always necessarily more perfect than common knowledge, if they are equal in other respects, §§ 18, 21.",[]],"meier:39":["Common knowledge is very useful, and in countless cases suffices to further our welfare; indeed many a common knowledge may contingently be more useful than many a learned one. Learned knowledge is nevertheless necessarily more useful than common knowledge, if they are equal in other respects. For (1) since it is more perfect, § 38, it also improves the cognitive power in a higher degree than common knowledge; (2) for the same reason it affords greater pleasure than common knowledge; (3) it is more in keeping with, and more becoming to, the character of humanity; (4) it does more than common knowledge to further the discovery of new truths; and (5) it can be applied and exercised far more skilfully and better than common knowledge.",[]],"meier:40":["A learned knowledge which is at the same time beautiful affords all the advantages of learned knowledge, § 39, and all the advantages of beautiful knowledge. And since it at the same time averts all the harm of merely learned and of merely beautiful knowledge, it is of all the kinds of human knowledge the most useful and serviceable. Of the extent of learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:41":["The imperfection of learned knowledge which is opposed to its extent, § 25, is the narrowness of learned knowledge (*angustia eruditae cognitionis*); and it always arises from ignorance (*ignorantia*), that is, from the total lack of knowledge of things and their grounds. In the measure in which the extent of a man's learned knowledge increases, his ignorance diminishes; and the greater a man's ignorance, the narrower is his learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:42":["To the extent of learned knowledge a twofold ignorance is opposed: (1) total ignorance (*ignorantia totalis*), when we have not even a historical knowledge of a thing; and (2) ignorance of the grounds of things (*ignorantia rationum*), which is compatible with an excellent historical knowledge of them.",[]],"meier:43":["A man's ignorance is (1) an absolutely necessary and unavoidable ignorance (*ignorantia absolute necessaria et invincibilis*), which owing to the limits of his cognitive power he cannot avoid; or (2) a voluntary ignorance (*ignorantia arbitraria et vincibilis*), the opposite knowledge of which he could attain if he would.",[]],"meier:44":["The sum of all those things which a man can know in a learned manner without prejudice to the rest of his perfection as a whole is the horizon, or field of vision, of his learned knowledge (*horizon seu sphaera cognitionis eruditae*). From it, therefore, are excluded all things in regard to which a man must, necessarily or voluntarily, remain ignorant, § 43.",[]],"meier:45":["A thing is raised above the horizon of human learned knowledge (*res supra horizontem eruditae cognitionis humanae posita*) when ignorance of it is absolutely necessary in a man, although it is not unworthy of learned knowledge. The human understanding is too weak to be capable of a learned knowledge of these weighty matters. We must not despise such things as lie beyond our horizon; nor must we, without sufficient ground, declare anything to be a thing raised above the horizon of our knowledge. None the less we must endeavour, as far as possible, to avoid the vain and harmful striving after a learned knowledge of such things as lie beyond our horizon.",[]],"meier:46":["A thing is lowered beneath the horizon of human learned knowledge (*res infra horizontem eruditae cognitionis humanae posita*) when, though it could be known by a man in a learned manner, it is not great enough to be worthy of such knowledge. Just as we must not, without adequate ground, declare a thing to be lowered beneath the horizon of human learned knowledge, so also we must not take pains to know in a learned manner such things as really are lowered beneath it. Otherwise we make ourselves ridiculous and contemptible, and neglect in the process the learned knowledge of weightier and more necessary matters.",[]],"meier:47":["A thing lies outside the horizon of human learned knowledge (*res extra horizontem cognitionis humanae eruditae posita*) when, though it could be known by a man in a learned manner and is not unworthy of human learned knowledge, the learned knowledge of it would hinder him in his other occupations. No one must, without adequate ground, declare a thing to be something found outside the horizon of his learned knowledge. But neither must anyone strive after a learned knowledge of such things as lie outside that horizon; for otherwise he would meddle in the affairs of others, and neglect his own work in the process.",[]],"meier:48":["All those things which are neither raised above the horizon of human learned knowledge, nor lowered beneath it, nor found outside it, lie within the compass of that horizon (*res intra horizontem cognitionis humanae eruditae posita*); and they constitute the learned horizon, § 44.",[]],"meier:49":["The most extensive learned knowledge of a man consists in the learned knowledge of all the things that lie within the compass of his horizon, §§ 48, 25. Now since every learned man must attain the most extensive learned knowledge possible to him, he must seek to measure the limits and compass of his horizon as exactly as human weakness permits, § 44.",[]],"meier:50":["Even if a man should have attained the most extensive learned knowledge, there still remains much necessary ignorance, which redounds neither to his honour nor to his shame, § 43. Voluntary ignorance, however, is either praiseworthy or blameworthy. Praiseworthy ignorance (*ignorantia laudabilis*) is ignorance of such things as are found beneath and outside the horizon of learned knowledge, §§ 46, 47.",[]],"meier:51":["Blameworthy ignorance (*ignorantia illaudabilis*) is ignorance of such things as are found within the horizon of learned knowledge, § 48. Whoever would attain the most extensive learned knowledge must therefore seek to avoid all blameworthy ignorance, and no other ignorance, § 49.",[]],"meier:52":["It is a ridiculous folly in many a poor head among the learned when they count their blameworthy ignorance a merit, and declare of themselves, with Socrates, who possessed a very extensive learning, that they know nothing except that they know nothing.",[]],"meier:53":["The more things anyone knows in a learned manner, the more extensive is his learned knowledge. A higher or notable, and consequently rarer, degree of extent of learned knowledge is entitled polymathy (*polyhistoria*). It is undeniably a great perfection of learned knowledge, provided only that greed for polyhistory does not lead us (1) to overstep the limits of our learned horizon, and (2) to neglect too noticeably the other perfections of learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:54":["Whoever would make his learned knowledge sufficiently extensive must (1) learn many principal branches of learning, for example philosophy, theology, history, philology, etc.; (2) [learn] many parts of each principal branch, for example, of philosophy, logic, metaphysics, physics, natural law, etc.; (3) again learn each of these parts extensively, for example logic; and (4) seek in turn to attain an extensive learned knowledge of each single truth, for example of the omniscience of God.",[]],"meier:55":["An extensive learned knowledge is complete and full (*completa cognitio erudita*) when it suffices for its ends, that is, when we know in a learned manner as much as the whole end of our learned knowledge requires. Extent without completeness is not perfect enough. We must therefore learn to distinguish, in every object of learned knowledge, the principal truths from the subordinate matters, in order that through the learned knowledge of the former we may attain a complete learning.",[]],"meier:56":["Consider one who studies merely in the manner of a gentleman: he runs hastily through a broad outline of all the parts of learning; he goes through a few parts somewhat more extensively, but still quite briefly; and here and there he perhaps investigates some particular matters because for some reason or other they especially please him. The learned knowledge of such a man is a poor skeleton of learning, and deserves very little esteem, § 55.",[]],"meier:57":["Since it is impossible for a man to attain a completely extensive learned knowledge all at once, we must constantly seek to extend our learned knowledge, lest by failing to go further forward we go backward in it.",[]],"meier:58":["Since through the extension of learned knowledge the cognitive powers are at the same time made more capable, there is no need to fear that the constant extension of learned knowledge will overload our heads, provided only that in this occupation we do not overstep the limits of the learned horizon.",[]],"meier:59":["Though art is long and human life short, this reflection should rather incite us to learn, with due haste, as much as possible, than instil in us a timidity and despondency that hinders us from extending our learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:60":["If we are not to regard the extension of learned knowledge as unnecessary and useless, we must not set before ourselves too small, mean and base an end as that which we seek to attain by our learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:61":["Since it is naturally necessary that we men forget much, we must for that very reason greatly extend our learned knowledge, so that we may forget much and nevertheless retain much. The truths we forget have in any case not been wholly useless, since they have increased the degree of our cognitive powers.",[]],"meier:62":["The extent of learned knowledge arises from a twofold source, and both must be present together: (1) from the extent and rich content of the object, when it contains within it much that can be known by a man in a learned manner (*vastitas obiectiva*); and (2) from the range of the cognitive powers (*vastitas subiectiva*), by virtue of which we are able to know much about a thing in a learned manner.",[]],"meier:63":["Since it is impossible for a man to learn all the parts of learning in an equal degree of perfection, each must choose one of them, with which he chiefly occupies himself and of which he seeks to attain the most perfect learned knowledge. This is his principal science (*scientia eruditi principalis*). Each must choose for his principal science that part of learning (1) which can be known by men in a very high degree of perfection, and (2) for which he possesses the most skill, rational inclination, and other means of advancement.",[]],"meier:64":["Each must (1) learn his principal science most extensively and completely, and (2) investigate all other parts of learning in relation to his principal science. The more closely another part of learning is connected with the principal science, the more perfectly and extensively must we seek to know it in a learned manner, § 63.",[]],"meier:65":["The narrowness of learned knowledge causes, among others, a threefold harm: (1) learned pedantry and charlatanry (*pedantismus et charlataneria eruditorum*), by which a man prizes far too highly the little he understands and wholly despises everything else; (2) a ridiculous conceit and vain arrogance; and (3) a little learning can make a man unhappy all his life, since he may fall into just such circumstances as require of him not what he has learned, but what he has not learned. Of the magnitude of learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:66":["The magnitude of learned knowledge requires not only a great object; the knowledge must also be great enough for the object, § 26. The greater the object, and the more proportioned the knowledge, the greater is the learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:67":["The object of learned knowledge is great considered in itself (*magnitudo eruditae cognitionis obiectiva absoluta*) when it contains within it much manifold that can be known in a learned manner; for example, God, philosophy, history, etc.",[]],"meier:68":["The object of learned knowledge is great in respect of its consequences (*magnitudo eruditae cognitionis obiectiva respectiva*), (1) when it is weighty (*res digna, gravis*), that is, when it has great consequences, as when the happiness of men, the welfare of the fatherland, etc., rest upon it; (2) when it is fruitful (*res foecunda*), that is, when many consequences flow from it; for example, godliness, for it is profitable unto all things.",[]],"meier:69":["Whoever would make his learned knowledge truly great must seek to know none but things that are absolutely great, weighty and fruitful. The more the object contains within it, the greater its consequences, and the more consequences it has, the greater, weightier and more fruitful it is; and the greater, weightier and more fruitful is the learned knowledge of it, in so far, that is, as that knowledge is regarded in respect of its object.",[]],"meier:70":["The magnitude which belongs to learned knowledge itself (*magnitudo cognitionis eruditae subiectiva*) consists in its being proportioned to the magnitude of its object, § 66. The greater the object, the more extensive, correct, distinct, certain and practical must the learned knowledge of it be, and the more time and diligence must be spent in attaining it. The less great the object, the less perfect need the learned knowledge be, and the less time and diligence must be spent in attaining it.",[]],"meier:71":["The imperfection of learned knowledge which is opposed to its magnitude is entitled its pettiness (*parvitas, vilitas cognitionis eruditae*); and it arises either from the pettiness of the object, or from the learned knowledge not being proportioned to the object, § 66.",[]],"meier:72":["The object of learned knowledge is, considered in itself, petty (*parvitas cognitionis eruditae obiectiva absoluta*) when it contains within it little that can be known in a learned manner; for example, the hairpins of Roman women.",[]],"meier:73":["The object of learned knowledge is petty in respect of its consequences (*parvitas cognitionis eruditae obiectiva respectiva*), (1) when it is not weighty (*res leviores*), that is, when it has no great consequences, for example the doctrine of the composition of bodies out of monads; (2) when it is unfruitful (*res infoecunda, sterilis*), that is, when it has few consequences, for example the doctrine of the incorporeality of the soul.",[]],"meier:74":["When a thing is petty in every respect, §§ 72, 73, it is not worthy of learned knowledge, for it is lowered beneath its horizon, § 46. The less a thing contains within it, and the fewer and smaller its consequences, the pettier it is. Among these trifles we must also reckon vulgar and base things (*res plebeiae, abiectae*), the learned investigation of which would even be contrary to decent morals; for example, the outbreaks of vice among the rabble.",[]],"meier:75":["The pettiness which belongs to learned knowledge itself (*parvitas cognitionis eruditae subiectiva*) consists in its not being proportioned to its objects, § 71: consequently (1) when we seek a more perfect learned knowledge, with more labour and more time spent upon it, than the object deserves; and (2) when we do not know an object as perfectly, do not investigate it with as much labour, and do not spend as much time upon it, as it deserves. For example, whoever assails errors more learnedly and diligently than vices has a learned knowledge that is not sufficiently proportioned.",[]],"meier:76":["Whoever would make his learned knowledge truly perfect must (1) choose, if possible, the greatest part of learning, §§ 67, 68, for his principal science, § 63; (2) the greater a part of learning, the more labour and diligence must he spend upon it, and the more perfect must his learned knowledge of it be; (3) the greater the truths, the more labour and time must he spend upon them, and the more perfect must his learned knowledge of them be.",[]],"meier:77":["Whoever lacks a mature and manly judgment cannot possibly judge of the true worth of things, and it is therefore impossible for him to achieve magnitude of learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:78":["Since all objects of learned knowledge stand in a universal connection, they are all infinitely great, weighty and fruitful, §§ 67, 68. An object is therefore entitled a trifle only in relation to us, because it is impossible for us to know its magnitude, weight and fruitfulness in a learned manner.",[]],"meier:79":["A learned knowledge, together with its object, is not to be esteemed great because this or that petty mind makes much ado about it; nor, likewise, is it to be held petty because the same mind declares it a thing of small account.",[]],"meier:80":["A learned knowledge is not a contemptible and unfruitful trifle because this or that dry and barren head is unable to derive many and weighty consequences from it. The barrenness of the soil is not to be imputed to the plant.",[]],"meier:81":["The application of a great learned knowledge to petty, ridiculous, vulgar and contemptible cases may indeed contingently render it ridiculous, contemptible and vulgar; but it is not on that account to be reckoned among trifles of small account.",[]],"meier:82":["When the great objects of learned knowledge are represented and expounded by this or that person in a contemptible and ridiculous manner, they are not on that account to be reckoned among contemptible trifles.",[]],"meier:83":["A learned man must not, through his own fault, through wretched application, and through ridiculous representations and expressions, render learned knowledge ridiculous and contemptible, §§ 81, 82.",[]],"meier:84":["If a learned mode of knowledge should not be important and fruitful in our present circumstances, because we do not yet discern its consequences, it may yet become important and fruitful in the future; and it is not, therefore, on that account a trifle. We must plant trees for our descendants also, though we ourselves shall not enjoy their fruits.",[]],"meier:85":["We disgrace ourselves if we refuse to regard abstract knowledge, subtleties and profound distinctions as great and important matters, merely because they require much laborious reflection.",[]],"meier:86":["The intermediate truths in an extensive system [Lehrgebäude] are in themselves, it is true, of no great significance. But since without them we cannot know the principal truths in a properly learned manner, they are on that account great and worthy objects of our learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:87":["A learned inquiry is not important merely because it demands much labour, diligence and time, and because it cannot be undertaken without great erudition. For there are also buffooneries [Possen] that are very difficult.",[]],"meier:88":["A part of learning is not to be reckoned among trifles merely because it is filled with many trifles.",[]],"meier:89":["A learned mode of knowledge which is not great in one respect may be great in another, and it must not therefore be regarded as a trifle (§ 67, 68). For example, a mode of knowledge may not be important and yet be fruitful.",[]],"meier:90":["Since different scholars may have chosen different principal sciences [Hauptwissenschaften] (§ 63), a learned inquiry may be great in respect of the one and small in respect of the other.",[]],"meier:91":["A great mind possesses the facility [Fertigkeit] of having only great learned knowledge. Its inclination towards such knowledge, and its aversion from all trifles, constrain it always first to examine the greatness of a matter before it endeavours to know it in a learned manner. It does so in order that it may know whether the matter is worthy of learned knowledge, and of what degree of the perfection of such knowledge it is worthy. Of the truth of learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:92":["A false or incorrect mode of knowledge (*cognitio falsa*) is a mode of knowledge which is not knowledge, and yet seems to be knowledge. A false learned mode of knowledge (*cognitio erudita falsa*) only seems to be learned knowledge: it is either no knowledge at all, or at least not learned knowledge. For example, those who assume a vegetative soul in plants, and derive the growth of plants from it, have a false learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:93":["A true or correct mode of knowledge (*cognitio vera*) not only seems to be knowledge, but is so in fact. A true learned mode of knowledge (*cognitio erudita vera*) not only seems to be learned knowledge, but in fact deserves that name. For example, whoever convincingly demonstrates the actuality of God from the contingency of this world has a true learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:94":["The criteria of the correctness and incorrectness of knowledge (*criteria veritatis et falsitatis cognitionis*) are the grounds from which it can be known that a mode of knowledge is true or that it is false. They are either present in the knowledge itself, or they are not. The former are the internal, the latter the external criteria of correctness and incorrectness (*criteria interna et externa veritatis et falsitatis cognitionis*).",[]],"meier:95":["The first internal criterion of the truth of a mode of knowledge consists in its internal possibility (*possibilitas cognitionis interna*), in so far as it represents something possible and contains nothing self-contradictory, even when it is considered quite by itself. The internal impossibility of a mode of knowledge (*impossibilitas cognitionis interna*), when it represents nothing and the manifold in it is in conflict with itself, is therefore the first internal criterion of its falsity (§ 94, 93, 92).",[]],"meier:96":["The second internal criterion of the truth of a mode of knowledge consists in its being possible in a connection [Zusammenhang] (*possibilitas cognitionis hypothetica*); consequently, (1) in its being a consequence of correct grounds, and (2) a ground of correct consequences (§ 94, 93). A mode of knowledge is therefore true if it is not impossible and is in conformity with the principle of sufficient reason (§ 16).",[]],"meier:97":["A mode of knowledge is false if it is impossible in connection (*impossibilitas in nexu*); consequently, if it has no grounds or false grounds, and no consequences or false consequences (§ 96). And this is the second internal criterion of its incorrectness (§ 94).",[]],"meier:98":["We must not assume: (1) that a mode of knowledge is true because we perceive no internal impossibility in it; (2) that it is false because we perceive no internal possibility in it; (3) that a mode of knowledge is true when we perceive neither its groundlessness nor its false grounds and consequences; (4) that a mode of knowledge is false when we know no correct grounds and consequences of it. For we men are not omniscient.",[]],"meier:99":["If we represent a thing otherwise than it is, we believe that we know it, and yet do not know it. Our knowledge is therefore false (§ 92). Moreover, all possible things have an internal possibility, grounds and consequences (§ 15, 16). The logical truth of knowledge (*veritas cognitionis logica*) therefore consists in its agreement with its object, and its logical incorrectness (*falsitas cognitionis logica*) in its not agreeing with its object (§ 95, 96, 97).",[]],"meier:100":["If a mode of knowledge contains nothing true, it is wholly false (*falsitas totalis*); and if it contains nothing false, it is wholly true (*veritas totalis*). But it may be true and false at the same time, though in different respects (*veritas et falsitas partialis*). We must not, therefore, accept an extensive body of knowledge as a whole because perhaps the greater and more important part of it is true; nor reject it as a whole because perhaps the greater and more important part of it is false. For example, the systems of the various religions.",[]],"meier:101":["The more numerous and the more varied the parts which a mode of knowledge contains, and which are possible together; the greater these parts are; and the more numerous and greater the correct grounds and consequences it has: the more correct is the knowledge (§ 95, 96). Whoever, therefore, wishes to improve his learned knowledge as far as possible must seek to attain in it the highest possible degree of truth.",[]],"meier:102":["A mode of knowledge which is correct in a higher degree is entitled exact knowledge (*cognitio exacta, exasciata*); one which is true in a lesser degree is entitled crude [grob] knowledge (*cognitio crassa*). All crude knowledge must be avoided, and the more so the cruder it is, that is, the more, and the more important, falsehood it contains. On the other hand, perfect learned knowledge must be as exact as possible (§ 101); and the greater its objects are, the more exactly must we seek to know them (§ 70). But the smaller they are, the less correct need our knowledge of them be (§ 70).",[]],"meier:103":["Learned knowledge can be false in a threefold manner: (1) when the knowledge of the things is false, although the knowledge of the grounds is correct; (2) when the knowledge of the grounds is false, although the knowledge of the things is correct; (3) when the representation of the connection between true grounds and consequences is incorrect (§ 100). A true learned mode of knowledge must therefore be at one and the same time a correct knowledge of the things, of the grounds, and of their connection (§ 21).",[]],"meier:104":["By truths (*veritates*) we also understand true knowledge itself; and in that sense all truths are either dogmatic (*veritates dogmaticae*) or historical (*veritates historicae*). The former can and must be known from the internal criteria of truth, the latter only from the external. For example, that there is a God is a dogmatic truth; but that David was the second king of the Israelites is a historical truth. A system (*systema*) is a multitude of dogmatic truths so combined with one another that, taken together, they constitute a single body of knowledge which can be regarded as a whole.",[]],"meier:105":["The more truths a system contains, and the greater and more correct they are, the more perfect is the system (§ 104). For the most exact truth of a system it is required: (1) that all its parts be correct in the most exact manner; (2) that none contradict another; and (3) that they be all connected, each being either a ground of the rest, or a consequence, or both at once (§ 95, 96). Since there is no truth outside systems (§ 96, 104), learned knowledge must be systematic if it is to be perfectly correct.",[]],"meier:106":["All truths are either merely aesthetic truths (*veritates mere aestheticae*), which must be known only in a beautiful manner, and with which learned knowledge must never concern itself; or merely learned truths (*veritates mere eruditae*), which can be known only in a learned manner, and with which alone learned knowledge must not concern itself, since it would otherwise be merely learned (§ 32); or they are both at once (*veritates aesthetico-eruditae*), and these are the chief object of a truly perfect learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:107":["Since truth is not the only perfection of learned knowledge, a scholar can never be excused if he occupies himself with a learned inquiry merely because it is true. It is not good to think and to say all truths.",[]],"meier:108":["Everyone must seek to know his principal science with the greatest exactness (§ 102), and in the highest possible degree systematically (§ 105) (§ 63).",[]],"meier:109":["Error (*cognitio erronea, error*) consists in our holding false knowledge to be true and true knowledge to be false. Consequently: (1) every erroneous mode of knowledge is false (§ 99); (2) not every false mode of knowledge is erroneous, namely when we know that it is false (§ 99); (3) error arises from false knowledge. If we had no false knowledge at all, we could have no errors either. Error is worse than merely false knowledge, for it is a hidden poison. Learned knowledge can therefore be erroneous in a threefold manner (§ 103).",[]],"meier:110":["If we transgress the rules of § 98, error arises (§ 109). The first source of all errors is therefore ignorance (§ 41), when it is combined with precipitancy [Übereilung], in virtue of which we deny that of which we have no knowledge.",[]],"meier:111":["Error is either avoidable (*error vincibilis*) or unavoidable (*error invincibilis*). The former arises from an avoidable, the latter from an unavoidable ignorance (§ 43). The former is a blameworthy blemish upon learned knowledge; the latter, however, cannot and need not be avoided.",[]],"meier:112":["The more extensive, important and fruitful an error is, and the more easily it could have been prevented, the greater it is. The greater the error, the more it disgraces learned knowledge, and the more carefully must it be guarded against. Consequently, everyone must seek especially to guard against errors in his principal science (§ 108).",[]],"meier:113":["A mode of knowledge is manifestly false (*cognitio aperte falsa*) when its incorrectness is discovered merely by considering it; for example, a quadrangular triangle. But if, in order to discover its incorrectness, we must undertake a more extensive inquiry, it is covertly false (*cognitio cuius falsitas latet*); for example, [the proposition that] matter can think. An error through which a manifestly false mode of knowledge is accepted as true is a preposterous, absurd and foolish error (*cognitio absurda, absona*). Not all errors are absurdities.",[]],"meier:114":["All truths are either absolutely necessary truths (*veritates absolute necessariae*) or contingent truths (*veritates contingentes*). In the former it is wholly impossible that they should be false; for example, there is a God. The latter, however, might also be false; for example, this world is actual. Since error is more easily avoided in respect of the former, it is greater than error in contingent truths (§ 112). Of the clearness of learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:115":["A mark, or characteristic, of knowledge and of things (*nota, character cognitionis et rei*) is that in the knowledge or in the things which, when it is known, is the ground of our being conscious of them; in other words, marks are the distinguishing features of knowledge and of its objects. Wherever there is consciousness, therefore, marks are known (§ 13). For example, reason is a mark of man, and of the knowledge we have of him.",[]],"meier:116":["Marks have in turn their own marks (§ 115). Consequently, all marks of a thing are either immediate marks (*notae immediatae, proximae*) or mediate marks (*notae mediatae, remotae*). The latter are marks of marks; the former are only marks of the thing, though they are not marks of its marks. For example, reason is an immediate mark of man; but since reason is a faculty of distinctly discerning the connection of things, faculty is a mediate mark of man. Consciousness rests on the immediate marks (§ 13).",[]],"meier:117":["Marks are either negative (*notae negativae*) or affirmative (*notae affirmativae, positivae*). Through the former we represent something as absent in the thing, and thereby know only what it is not; for example, the irrationality of irrational animals. Through the latter we represent something as present in the thing, and know through them what the thing is; for example, the reason of men. Although both kinds can give rise to consciousness, affirmative marks are better than negative.",[]],"meier:118":["Marks are either more important (*notae graviores*) or less important (*notae leviores*). The former disclose a greater, the latter a lesser, difference in the thing. They are also either fruitful (*notae foecundae*) or unfruitful (*notae infoecundae*). The former disclose a manifold difference from many things; the latter do not. Reason is an important and fruitful mark of a man; that he is a thing is a less important and less fruitful one. The more important and fruitful the marks are, therefore, the more do they promote consciousness.",[]],"meier:119":["Since marks are grounds (§ 115), they are either sufficient or insufficient grounds of consciousness (§ 15). The former are sufficient marks (*notae sufficientes*), the latter insufficient marks (*notae insufficientes*). The former are better marks than the latter. The faculty of thinking is an insufficient mark of a spirit; that it has understanding is a sufficient one.",[]],"meier:120":["Marks are either absolutely necessary and invariable (*notae absolute necessariae et invariabiles*) or contingent and variable (*notae contingentes et variabiles*). The former are such that without them the thing cannot be represented; for example, the reason of man. The latter are such that the thing can still be represented without them; for example, the actual thinking of man. The former are better marks than the latter.",[]],"meier:121":["Marks in a thing can either be represented without considering it in connection with other things outside it, or they cannot. The latter are external marks or relations (*notae externae, relationes*); for example, the dominion [Herrschaft] of a man. The former are internal marks (*notae internae*). These internal marks are either necessary or contingent (§ 120). The contingent are entitled modes (*modi*); for example, the learning of a man. The necessary are either the grounds of all the other determinations, or they are not. Those which are not are the attributes (*attributa*); for example, the faculty of thinking in a man. Those which are are entitled the essentials (*essentialia*); for example, the reason of man. The sum of all the essentials is the essence (*essentia*).",[]],"meier:122":["The more marks of a thing we know, the greater these marks are, and the more perfectly we know them, the greater and better is our consciousness (§ 13). Consequently, affirmative, important, fruitful, internal, necessary and sufficient marks give rise to a greater and better consciousness than those opposed to them (§ 117–121).",[]],"meier:123":["A representation, in so far as we are conscious of it, is entitled a thought (*cogitatio*). Consequently, not every representation and mode of knowledge is a thought. And what has been proved of consciousness holds also of thinking (§ 115, 116, 122).",[]],"meier:124":["A mode of knowledge either contains as many marks as are required for consciousness, or it does not. The former is clear knowledge (*cognitio clara*), which is the same as thought, and as knowledge of which we are conscious (§ 123). The latter is obscure knowledge (*cognitio obscura*), which is therefore neither a thought nor bound up with consciousness (§ 123). The former is more perfect than the latter. When we cannot recall a word, and yet it seems to be, as it were, on the tip of our tongue, we then have an obscure representation of it.",[]],"meier:125":["Obscure knowledge is either absolutely obscure (*cognitio absolute obscura*) or relatively obscure (*cognitio relative obscura*). The former would have to be so obscure that it would be absolutely impossible to make it clear. No true knowledge is absolutely obscure, and we must therefore regard nothing as absolutely obscure. All true obscure knowledge is only relatively obscure; that is to say, the powers of this or that thinking being are not sufficient to make it clear. And in that case either the object is chiefly to blame for this obscurity, or the man to whom the knowledge is obscure. The former is obscurity in the things (*cognitio obiective obscura*), the latter obscurity in the head of him to whom the knowledge is thus obscure (*cognitio subiective obscura*). For example, things which are too far distant from us, or too small, are the cause of our sensing them obscurely. But whoever finds logic obscure is himself to blame. Finally, obscure knowledge is either wholly obscure (*cognitio totaliter obscura*), when we are not conscious of it at all, or only partly so (*cognitio partialiter obscura*), when we are at the same time conscious and not conscious of it. All our clear knowledge is always partly obscure to us, since we are not able to think any single thing through completely.",[]],"meier:126":["One man can know what another does not know; one may therefore know marks which are unknown to another (§ 115). Consequently, one may know clearly what another knows only obscurely (§ 124). We must not, therefore, infer: (1) what is clear to me is clear to others also; (2) what is obscure to me is obscure to others also; (3) what is now clear to me will be clear to me in the future also; (4) what is now obscure to me will remain obscure to me in the future also.",[]],"meier:127":["The more numerous the marks required for consciousness which are unknown to us, the greater the marks which are unknown to us, and the more power is required to make a mode of knowledge clear, the greater is its obscurity. Before a mode of knowledge can become clear, therefore, we must often spend much time and labour in diminishing its obscurity.",[]],"meier:128":["Since we have much clear knowledge whose marks we do indeed know (§ 115), but not clearly, there actually are obscure representations in our soul. They constitute the materials out of which the soul gradually composes its clear knowledge.",[]],"meier:129":["The obscurity of knowledge arises from a threefold source. (1) It arises when a man does not possess sufficient powers to make the knowledge clear. This lack of powers is either necessary or not; and in the latter case the man either ought to have acquired the powers or not. (2) It arises from lack of attention. This lack arises either from a necessary lack of powers, or because we may not attend to a certain knowledge, or because we do not attend to it although we ought. (3) It arises from ignorance of such things as are required if a certain knowledge is to become clear, whether this ignorance be necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy (§ 43, 50, 51). All obscurity of knowledge is therefore either necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy. Consequently, in improving knowledge we must seek to avoid only contingent and blameworthy obscurity; and obscurity which is necessary and praiseworthy in one man may be contingent and blameworthy in another.",[]],"meier:130":["Obscure knowledge (1) can be true, but it can also be false (§ 92, 93); not every obscure mode of knowledge is therefore false. (2) It is not learned knowledge, in so far as it is obscure. In the learned knowledge of men it is an unavoidable evil.",[]],"meier:131":["The act whereby the obscurity of knowledge is diminished, and its clearness produced and increased, is entitled the unfolding or development of knowledge (*evolutio, explanatio cognitionis*); just as the opposite act is entitled its enfolding (*cognitionis involutio*). For the former, (a) three things are required by way of preparation. (1) We must examine as far as possible whether the obscurity of the knowledge which we wish to develop is necessary or contingent, unculpable or culpable. Only in the latter case may we venture upon the development. (2) We must examine whether its development belongs to the horizon of our clear knowledge or not. Only in the former case is this labour permitted us. (3) We must examine whether this development does not presuppose some other clear knowledge without which it cannot take place. If this is in fact so, we must not undertake the labour until we have acquired that other clear knowledge (§ 129). (b) For the development itself three things are required: (1) we must direct our attention to the thing which we wish to know clearly; (2) we must compare it with other things different from it, in order to know its marks (§ 115); (3) we must abstract from all other things, or obscure them, while attending to the object. The more often, the more intently and the longer we attend to a thing, the more things we compare it with, and the more strongly we abstract from other things, the better and the sooner does the knowledge of it develop, even though at the beginning we should several times perform this labour in vain.",[]],"meier:132":["Through a clear mode of knowledge we are either able to distinguish the object constantly, and in all circumstances, from all other possible things, or we are not. In the first case our knowledge is completely clear (*cognitio complete clara*); for example, the representation of the colour red. In the other case it is incompletely clear (*cognitio incomplete clara*); for example, when we can indeed taste that a wine is Rhenish, but cannot taste of what sort it is. The former is more perfect than the latter, since the latter contains more obscurity than the former (§ 124); and we attain it through knowledge of the necessary, invariable and sufficient marks (§ 119, 120, 121).",[]],"meier:133":["When we have a clear mode of knowledge, we are either conscious of everything that is to be met with in the object, or we are not. In the first case the knowledge is wholly clear (*cognitio totaliter clara*); in the latter it is only partly clear (*cognitio partialiter clara*). No human clear knowledge is wholly clear (§ 125); and a mode of knowledge can be completely clear although it is not wholly clear (§ 132). Meanwhile, a mode of knowledge is the more perfect the more nearly its clearness approaches total clearness (§ 124).",[]],"meier:134":["The more marks we know, and consequently the more numerous and the more closely resembling the things from which we can distinguish a thing; the greater the marks, and the better we know all the marks; the more easily we can be conscious of a mode of knowledge and of its object: the clearer is our knowledge (§ 124), and therefore also the more perfect. In improving a mode of knowledge, therefore, we must seek to attain the highest possible degree of its clearness.",[]],"meier:135":["A mode of knowledge which is clearer through the multitude of its marks is entitled vivid knowledge (*cognitio extensive clarior, vivida*); for example, 'the nightingale's melodious song rings resounding through the valleys.' But one which is clearer through the greatness of its marks and their clearer representation is intensively clearer (*cognitio intensive clarior*). Logic concerns itself only with the latter degree of clearness (§ 1, 17, 21).",[]],"meier:136":["If we forget marks of clear knowledge, a clearer mode of knowledge can be changed into a less clear one, and finally into a wholly obscure one (§ 124). Whoever, therefore, wishes to prevent the enfolding of clear knowledge (§ 131) must guard against forgetfulness.",[]],"meier:137":["A mode of knowledge considered in respect of the intensity of its clearness is either distinct or confused (§ 14, 135). In the former the marks also are clear; in the latter they are obscure (§ 14, 115, 124). Since distinctness is thus a manifold clearness, distinct knowledge is more perfect than confused (§ 124).",[]],"meier:138":["Since all learned knowledge is distinct (§ 21, 17), confused knowledge, in so far as it is confused, is not learned (§ 137). Just as the fine arts [schöne Wissenschaften] concern themselves with improving the confused half of human knowledge, so must a scholar, by means of logic, improve its distinct half. Obscurity and confusion are a necessary evil in learned knowledge, which we either also seek to improve incidentally or do not. In the latter case there arises a merely learned knowledge; in the former a learned knowledge which is at the same time beautiful (§ 24).",[]],"meier:139":["The act whereby a certain degree of distinctness is produced in our knowledge is entitled the analysis [Zergliederung] of knowledge (*resolutio, analysis, anatomia cognitionis*). A mode of knowledge can be analysed if it can be made distinct by some thinking being or other (*cognitio resolubilis*). In so far as it cannot become distinct, it is a mode of knowledge which cannot be analysed (*cognitio irresolubilis*). And in that case either it is altogether impossible that it should become distinct, or it merely cannot become distinct through the powers of this or that thinking being. In the first case it is absolutely unanalysable (*cognitio absolute irresolubilis*); in the latter it is only relatively unanalysable (*cognitio respective irresolubilis*). If a true mode of knowledge were absolutely unanalysable, its marks would have to be absolutely obscure (§ 137). Since this is impossible (§ 120), all true knowledge which cannot be analysed is so only relatively.",[]],"meier:140":["In so far as we have a distinct knowledge of a thing, we conceive it (*concipere*). What can be distinctly known is conceivable (*conceptibile*). What cannot be distinctly known is inconceivable (*inconceptibile*): either absolutely (*absolute inconceptibile*), if it is absolutely unanalysable; or only relatively (*relative inconceptibile*), if it is relatively unanalysable. All possible things are only relatively inconceivable (§ 139); and what we know in a learned manner, we always to that extent conceive (§ 21, 17).",[]],"meier:141":["What we do not conceive is not on that account an absurdity (§ 140). And since a mode of knowledge may be distinct to one man which to another is confused or even obscure (§ 126), we must not infer: what we do not conceive, others also do not conceive; what we conceive, others also conceive; what I now conceive I shall conceive in the future also; what I do not now conceive I shall not conceive in the future either. These inferences hold also when the question is of mere possibility or impossibility.",[]],"meier:142":["If we wish to analyse a mode of knowledge, (1) we must develop it (§ 131), should it not already be clear in us. (2) We must think the clear knowledge through, that is, develop its marks one after another in accordance with § 131. The more marks, and the better marks (§ 116–121), we develop, the better. (3) We must reflect upon [überlegen] the developed marks, that is, represent them together as one mode of knowledge; otherwise we should think the object only piecemeal, and that is not yet distinct knowledge of the whole object. (4) We must represent the developed marks in the very same connection and order in which they are found in the object. Otherwise the distinct knowledge does not agree with the object; and all disorder throws us into confusion. (5) We must obscure, or abstract from, all other things and representations not taken into consideration, in order that the knowledge may not at once become confused again.",[]],"meier:143":["The more numerous and greater the clear marks of a thing that we know, and the better we know the marks, and consequently the more extensive, proportionate, correct, clear, certain and practical [they are]: the more distinct is the knowledge (§ 137, 25–30).",[]],"meier:144":["A distinct mode of knowledge is either wholly distinct (*cognitio totaliter distincta*) or only partly so (*cognitio partialiter distincta*). The former contains no obscurity or confusion whatsoever; the latter contains some. No human distinct knowledge is wholly distinct (§ 133). But it is the more distinct the more nearly its distinctness approaches total distinctness (§ 142).",[]],"meier:145":["The confusion of knowledge arises (1) from the sources of obscurity, since it consists in the obscurity of the marks (§ 137, 129); (2) from a necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy, lack of thinking things through [Nachdenken]; likewise (3) of reflection [Überlegung]; (4) from lack of order in thinking; and (5) from a necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy, lack of abstraction (§ 142).",[]],"meier:146":["There is, therefore, not only a confusion in all our knowledge which we cannot possibly prevent; there is also a confusion which we could prevent, but may not, because it is found below or beyond our horizon (§ 146). Before we venture upon the analysis of a mode of knowledge, therefore, we must first examine as far as possible whether we can, or may, perform it.",[]],"meier:147":["The marks of a distinct mode of knowledge are clear (§ 137). They are therefore either distinct or confused (§ 137). In the first case we have adequate knowledge (*cognitio adaequata*); in the other, inadequate (*cognitio inadaequata*). For example: vice is an acquired facility of sinning. An acquired facility is an ease of acting which we have obtained through practice; and we sin when we perform evil free actions. This is an adequate knowledge of vice; but it would be inadequate if I had no distinct knowledge of facility and of sin. Adequacy is a manifold distinctness, and therefore a perfection of knowledge (§ 137). Inadequacy consists in the confusion of the knowledge of the marks, and therefore arises from the sources of confusion in general (§ 145). The more distinct marks a mode of knowledge contains, and the more distinct they are, the more adequate is the knowledge. And the most perfect learned knowledge must be as adequate as possible (§ 21).",[]],"meier:148":["Adequacy arises from the analysis of the marks (§ 147, 142). The more remote the marks which we in turn analyse, the more adequate does the knowledge become (§ 116, 147). A higher degree of adequacy is profound knowledge (*cognitio profunda, purior*). When we analyse a mode of knowledge, we bring the task to completion if we discover the marks which we can analyse no further. But we halt in the analysis if we do not further analyse marks which we nevertheless could analyse. Now since it would be a tedious labour always to complete the analysis of all our distinct knowledge, we must always have a rational end in view for analysing a mode of knowledge; and as soon as we have attained it, we must for the time being break off the labour. It is in any case impossible for a man to avoid all inadequacy in his knowledge (§ 144).",[]],"meier:149":["Distinct knowledge is either completely distinct (*cognitio complete distincta*) or incompletely so (*cognitio incomplete distincta*) (§ 132). The former is more perfect than the latter (§ 132). For example, if I represent a spirit as a being which can think, I have a distinct but incomplete knowledge of it. But if I represent it as a thing which has understanding, my distinct knowledge of a spirit is at the same time complete.",[]],"meier:150":["If we wish to render a distinct knowledge of a thing complete, we must either (1) seek to discover those marks which are met with in no other thing besides it, or (2) discover so many marks that, taken together, they belong to no other thing. For example: virtue is a facility [*Fertigkeit*] in free actions that accord with right. Each of these marks is also met with in things that are not virtues. Taken together, however, they are found in nothing else. Accordingly the sum of these four marks makes the distinct representation of virtue a completely distinct representation.",[]],"meier:151":["A distinct knowledge is either determinate (*cognitio determinata*) or indeterminate (*cognitio indeterminata*). The former is completely distinct, and contains not a single mark more than is absolutely necessary for completeness. For example: a spirit is a being which has understanding. The latter either contains too few marks, and is then incompletely distinct knowledge (§ 149); or it contains more clear marks than are needed for completeness, and is then an over-prolix knowledge (*cognitio nimis prolixa*). For example: a spirit is a thinking being which possesses understanding and free will. If, therefore, we separate from a completely distinct knowledge those marks which either are not needed for completeness or follow from other marks of the same knowledge, it becomes determinate. Determination is a perfection only of merely learned knowledge (§ 24), [and is called for] when we wish to make it easier for the weak [*schwach*] though profound [*tiefsinnig*] understanding of man to comprehend a thing.",[]],"meier:152":["The most adequate knowledge may gradually become less adequate, then inadequate, confused, and finally quite obscure, when clear knowledge becomes obscure through forgetfulness (§ 136). Whoever, therefore, would guard against this loss of perfections must seek to prevent forgetfulness. We men are never wholly secure in the possession of our perfections. We must therefore everywhere be diligently on our guard.",[]],"meier:153":["Whoever would make learned knowledge truly perfect in respect of its clearness must proportion the degree of clearness to the magnitude of the objects (§ 70). Consequently, the more extensive and the greater the object, the more numerous, the better and the greater must be the kinds and degrees of clearness by which the learned knowledge of it is illuminated. In the opposite case we must, in this matter, proceed in the opposite way.",[]],"meier:154":["Every man of learning must, if it is otherwise possible for him, choose as his principal science that part of learning which is capable of the most manifold and the greatest clearness. And everyone must seek to attain the clearest knowledge in his principal science (§§ 63, 64). He must therefore be a clear head, that is, he must love the clearest truths above all others [*vor züglich*], and must at all times think as clearly as possible. An obscure head scarcely deserves the name of a man of learning. Of the Certainty of Learned Knowledge.",[]],"meier:155":["Certainty (*certitudo subiective spectata*) is consciousness of truth, or clear knowledge of truth (§ 29). If, then, we would possess a certain learned knowledge, it must be true according to the rules of the fourth section, and clear in the proper manner according to the rules of the fifth section.",[]],"meier:156":["When we know clearly neither that something is true nor that it is false, our knowledge of it is uncertain (*incertitudo*). Uncertainty, therefore, is to be found only as an imperfection in our knowledge. A knowledge that is not uncertain for us is either certainly true (*certo vera cognitio*), when we are conscious of its truth, or certainly false (*certo falsa cognitio*), when we are conscious of its incorrectness (§ 155).",[]],"meier:157":["All certainty is either a distinct or a confused knowledge of truth (§§ 155, 137). The latter is sensitive certainty (*certitudo sensitiva*), and, when it is perfect in a higher degree, aesthetic certainty (*certitudo aesthetica*). The former is rational certainty (*certitudo rationalis*), and, when it is perfect in a higher degree, it is entitled logical or learned certainty (*certitudo iogica* [*logica*], *erudita*). No certain human knowledge can be merely rational and learned (§ 144).",[]],"meier:158":["Rational certainty is either adequate (*certitudo adaequata*), when we are in turn rationally certain of the criteria of truth, or inadequate (*certitudo inadaequata*), when we are only sensitively certain of the criteria of truth (§§ 157, 147). For example: what thinks is actual; I think; therefore I am actual. Since I have a distinct certainty of the first proposition, I am to that extent adequately certain of the last. But since the second proposition is only sensitively certain to me, the last proposition is to that extent only inadequately certain to me.",[]],"meier:159":["Certainty is either complete (*completa certitudo*) or incomplete (*incompleta certitudo*) (§§ 132, 149). Determinate certainty (*certitudo determinata*) is a complete certainty which contains no more criteria of truth than are indispensable to complete certainty (§ 151).",[]],"meier:160":["Through complete certainty the mind is always so set at rest that it is raised above all rational fear of the opposite. Incomplete certainty, on the other hand, cannot entirely dispel this rational fear (§ 159). A man may, however, often be freed from all fear of the opposite and nevertheless not be certain at all.",[]],"meier:161":["Determinate certainty, when it is moreover as adequate as possible, is mathematical certainty (*certitudo mathematica*).",[]],"meier:162":["We have total certainty (*certitudo totalis*) of a thing when everything that we know of it is certain to us. If not all of this is certain to us, the thing is only partly certain to us (*certitudo partialis*).",[]],"meier:163":["A certain knowledge is entitled (1) convincing (*cognitio convincens*), in so far as it is completely certain; and the production of such certain knowledge is entitled conviction (*convictio*); (2) undeniable (*cognitio evidens, indubitata*), in so far as we know clearly that that of which we are convinced cannot possibly be false; (3) thorough (*cognitio solida*), in so far as the certainty is adequate, or again when it is as great and of such a kind as is required.",[]],"meier:164":["The more things we know with certainty, and the more we know with certainty of each object; the greater the things we know with certainty, and the more clearly we know the truth: the greater is the certainty (§ 155). Whoever, therefore, would truly improve learned knowledge must make it as certain as possible. This he does by producing certainty of all kinds, and in each kind the greatest degree of certainty, so far, that is, as the other rules for the perfection of learned knowledge permit.",[]],"meier:165":["We must not infer: (1) What is certain to me is also certain to other people, and conversely (§ 126). (2) What is uncertain to me is, considered in itself, uncertain, or even false (§ 156). (3) What is uncertain to me is also uncertain to other people, and conversely (§ 126). (4) What is now certain to me will always remain certain to me in the future (§ 126). (5) What is now uncertain to me will always remain uncertain to me in the future also (§ 126). (6) If not everything in an object is certain, the object has no certainty at all (§ 162).",[]],"meier:166":["We attain some kinds of certainty, especially logical certainty, only gradually. On the one hand, therefore, we must not grow impatient when certainty does not come as quickly as we sometimes wish. On the other hand, when in some case we have become certain all too quickly, we must be suspicious whether we have not perhaps overlooked something. In attaining certainty we must make haste slowly.",[]],"meier:167":["When a knowledge is not certain in the kind and in the degree in which it could and should be, it is entitled a superficial knowledge (*cognitio superficiaria*).",[]],"meier:168":["We give our assent to a knowledge, or accept it (*assentiri, ponere aliquid*), when we hold it to be true; we reject it (*tollere aliquid*) when we hold it to be false; and we withhold our assent (*suspendere iudicium*) when we do neither. When we accept or reject an uncertain knowledge, we do so either because we know some criteria of its correctness or incorrectness, or without knowing any of these criteria at all. In the latter case we are precipitate (*praecipitantia*); and the uncertain knowledge which we accept or reject through precipitancy is a begged knowledge, a prejudice, a preconceived opinion (*praecaria cognitio, praeiudicium, praeconcepta opinio*). Begged knowledge is not learned knowledge at all (§§ 21, 17). All prejudices can be avoided in learned knowledge, and they are therefore an inexcusable blemish upon it.",[]],"meier:169":["Since the correctness and incorrectness of things does not depend upon our insights, (1) what we accept through a prejudice may be false, and what we reject through a prejudice may be true. (2) What we accept through a prejudice may in fact be true, and what we reject through a prejudice may actually be false. (3) In every prejudice there is always something erroneous and false, since we believe ourselves to be certain when we are not certain (§ 168).",[]],"meier:170":["A logical prejudice (*praeiudicium logicum*) is a prejudice by which the perfection of learned knowledge, and especially its thoroughness, is hindered. For example: (a) the prejudice of excessive confidence (*praeiudicium nimiae confidentiae*), when we precipitately hold something to be logically perfect. (1) The prejudice of excessive authority (*praeiudicium autoritatis*), when we accept or reject something because it is accepted or rejected by a man whom we honour so highly that we hold him worthy of imitation. (2) Logical egoism (*egoismus logicus*), when someone holds something to be logically perfect because he is himself its author. (3) The prejudice of antiquity (*praeiudicium antiquitatis*), when we hold something to be true because it is an old opinion. (4) The prejudice of novelty (*praeiudicium novitatis*), when we hold something to be true because it is a new opinion. (5) The prejudice of the adopted system (*praeiudicium systematis*), when we accept something merely because it accords with our system, and reject it because it is contrary to that system. (6) The prejudice of lazy confidence (*praeiudicium pigritiae*), when we believe that we can get as far in learned knowledge by lesser diligence as by greater. (7) The prejudice of superficiality (*praeiudicium corticis*), when we believe that we can get as far with a superficial knowledge as with a thorough one. (b) The prejudice of excessive mistrust (*praeiudicium nimiae diffidentiae*), when we precipitately hold something to be logically imperfect. (1) The prejudice of antiquity (*praeiudicium antiquitatis*), when we reject something because it is old. (2) The prejudice of novelty (*praeiudicium novitatis*), when we reject something because it is new. (3) The prejudice of nationality (*nazarethismus*), when we reject something because it derives from a certain people. (4) The prejudice of the mistrust which we place in ourselves (*praeiudicium nimiae diffidentiae in se ipsum positae*), when we trust ourselves too little, and so on.",[]],"meier:171":["When we accept or reject an uncertain knowledge on account of some criteria of correctness and incorrectness (§ 168), there are three possibilities. Either we know more and stronger grounds for accepting it than for rejecting it, and then our knowledge is probable (*cognitio probabilis, verosimilis*); or we know more and stronger grounds for rejecting it than for accepting it, and so we have an improbable knowledge (*cognitio improbabilis*); or the grounds on both sides are equal, and then it is a doubtful knowledge (*cognitio dubia*). For example, it is probable that the planets are inhabited, but it is improbable that the sun is. Whenever we can come to no decision, we have a doubtful knowledge.",[]],"meier:172":["Since men differ so infinitely from one another in their knowledge, one man may accept or reject through a prejudice a thing which a second holds to be quite certainly true, a third quite certainly false, a fourth probable, a fifth improbable, and a sixth doubtful. The first always acts wrongly. The two following cannot possibly both be right or both be wrong. The last three, however, may all together be right (§§ 159, 168, 171).",[]],"meier:173":["Probability, improbability and doubtfulness are uncertainties (§ 171), and are therefore not to be found in the objects of our knowledge (§ 156). The most probable things may be false, and the most improbable true.",[]],"meier:174":["Prejudices must be held neither true nor false (§ 168), and the same holds of doubtful knowledge (§ 171). Improbable knowledge must be held false, and probable knowledge true, but both under a constant fear of the opposite (§ 171). To completely certain knowledge we must give our assent with the greatest confidence, without fearing the opposite (§ 159).",[]],"meier:175":["The more numerous and the greater the grounds we know for accepting something, the fewer and the smaller the grounds we know for rejecting it, and the better we know the former and the worse the latter: the greater is the probability, and the greater the improbability of the opposite (§ 171). A degree of probability so great that in our regular conduct it is as good as a complete certainty is entitled moral certainty (*certitudo moralis*).",[]],"meier:176":["When we hold something to be true or false, every knowledge of a ground for the opposite is entitled a doubt (*dubium*). Doubts are known either in a merely obscure and indistinct manner, or in a rational and learned manner. The former are entitled scruples (*scrupulus*), the latter objections (*obiectio*).",[]],"meier:177":["All doubts are either true doubts (*dubium verum*) or false doubts (*dubium falsum*) (§§ 176, 92, 93). False doubts, when they seem to be true, have a great semblance [of truth] [*Schein*]. When we know clearly what is incorrect in a false doubt, the doubt is resolved or answered (*dubium resolvitur, seu ad dubium respondetur*). All doubts are either answerable or unanswerable, and each of these either absolutely or relatively (*dubium absolute et respective resolubile aut irresolubile*). Consequently (1) doubts can be raised against all truths, and nothing is false merely because doubts are raised against it; (2) doubts can be raised against a truth which are unanswerable for this or that man; (3) against no truth can absolutely unanswerable doubts be raised. Established truths (*veritates indubitatae, extra omnem dubitationis aleam positae*) are either truths that are completely certain, or truths against which no doubts can be raised other than such as are answerable and have already been answered. Truths that are not established are unestablished truths (*veritates non indubitatae*).",[]],"meier:178":["Complete conviction of a truth does not require (1) that the unknown doubts against it be answered; nor (2) that the scruples be answered, for they deserve no answer at all; nor (3) that all known doubts be answered. There may even be some among them which are unanswerable for a man who can nevertheless be completely convinced. (4) It does require that all doubts which make the mind waver be answered. (5) The answering of doubts always serves to increase certainty and to strengthen conviction (§ 176).",[]],"meier:179":["Uncertainty of knowledge arises (1) from the limitation of our cognitive power [*Erkenntnisskraft*], for which we either are or are not to blame; (2) from want of due attention, reflection, diligence and patience (§§ 129, 145), a want which may be necessary or contingent, blameworthy or praiseworthy; (3) from a necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy ignorance of those truths without knowledge of which another truth cannot become certain to us (§§ 43, 50, 51); (4) from an all too intense attention to doubts (§ 176).",[]],"meier:180":["There is in human knowledge an uncertainty (1) which is quite unavoidable, and which redounds neither to our shame nor to our honour; (2) which we need not avoid even if we could, because its object lies beyond our horizon or beneath it; and this redounds to a man's honour; (3) which a man can and ought to avoid, because the opposed certainty belongs within his horizon (§ 178). This last always redounds to our shame. Whoever would improve his learned knowledge as far as possible need seek to avoid only uncertainty of the third kind.",[]],"meier:181":["An opinion (*opinio*) is any uncertain knowledge, in so far as we accept it and at the same time know that it is not certain. An opinion is either accepted as a ground from which we explain the appearances in the world, or it is not. The latter is a common opinion (*opinio vulgaris*). The former is a philosophical or learned opinion (*hypothesis philosophica, erudita*), as, for example, when we assume the efflux of a magnetic matter in order to explain thereby how the magnet attracts iron.",[]],"meier:182":["All opinions (1) share the nature of all uncertain knowledge; (2) common opinions are, in learned knowledge at least, to be wholly despised; (3) what is an opinion for some is not an opinion for all; (4) we must not make too much of learned opinions, for they belong to the more imperfect half of learned knowledge; (5) they must not be wholly despised, since they are the transition from historical knowledge to completely certain learned knowledge (§ 181).",[]],"meier:183":["In regard to learned opinions the following rules must be observed. (1) No learned opinion must be held to be certainly true, nor must as much zeal be shown on its behalf as for certain truths. (2) Such opinions must be adopted only in case of necessity, when we cannot yet have any better learned knowledge. (3) No manifestly false opinion must be adopted, that is, one which is either internally impossible, or contradicts some other undeniable truth, or contradicts the appearances. An appearance does not contradict an opinion merely because we cannot derive the appearance from it. (4) We must be ready to abandon the most elegant and most learned opinion as soon as we discover its incorrectness. (5) A learned opinion must not be adopted until it has been brought to some degree of probability. We must therefore, among other things, be able to explain most of the appearances from it. (6) We must constantly endeavour to transform an opinion into a certain knowledge. (7) We must not have too great an inclination for opinions, nor invent too many of them. (8) An opinion must not be held true because it is new, elegant, wonderful, harmless or edifying, or because much labour, learning, wit and acumen were required before it was invented.",[]],"meier:184":["Certainty and conviction are either genuine or merely apparent. The error through which we imagine ourselves convinced when we are not convinced is entitled persuasion in the bad sense (*persuasio malo significatu*). Since the certainty of learned knowledge is hindered by it, we must seek to prevent it as far as possible. It arises (1) from ignorance of the rules of logic [*Vernunftlehre*]; (2) from the want of certain knowledge, for we then do not yet know how we feel in a genuine conviction; (3) from prejudices (§§ 169, 170, 171); (4) from excessive negligence and haste. Now in so far as it lies in a man's power to remove these causes of persuasion, he is to that extent also able to avoid this fault itself.",[]],"meier:185":["If, then, we guard against persuasion and attain to a thorough conviction, we acquire a science (*scientia subiective spectata*), that is, a learned knowledge in so far as it is completely certain.",[]],"meier:186":["The greater, the more important and the more fruitful the object of our learned knowledge, the more certain our knowledge [of it] must be (§ 70). That is to say, we must seek to make the knowledge of it thorough in the greatest number and the best kinds of certainty, and in the highest degrees of these. With smaller objects the case is exactly the reverse.",[]],"meier:187":["Everyone must, so far as is otherwise possible for him, choose for his principal science that part of learning which is capable of the greatest certainty. And he must not only do this, but must also seek to raise the principal science he has chosen to the highest possible degree of certainty (§§ 63, 64).",[]],"meier:188":["Complete certainty consists in the consciousness of the truth (§ 155), and consequently [in the consciousness] that it is possible and grounded (§§ 94, 96), in such a way that no fear of the opposite remains (§ 160). We are then, consequently, conscious that the opposite is false, impossible and ungrounded (§§ 95, 97). Complete certainty therefore arises from consciousness of the necessity of the truth (§ 114).",[]],"meier:189":["Complete certainty arises either from our knowing clearly that the truth is absolutely necessary, or from our knowing that it is necessary only in this world (§ 113). If we know the former with mathematical certainty, therein consists mathematical certainty of the first order (*certitudo mathematica primi ordinis*). In the other case we have mathematical certainty of the second order (*mathematica certitudo secundi ordinis*), as, for example, in our experiences.",[]],"meier:190":["Consciousness of the contingency of a truth occasions uncertainty, or at most only an incomplete certainty (§§ 189, 113). Arbitrary truths (*hypothesis*) are those whose truth depends on the choice [*Willkür*] of men; and their arbitrariness does not prevent complete certainty regarding them.",[]],"meier:191":["Proof (*probatio*) is that which is added to a truth in order that it may become certain. The ground of proof [*Beweisthum*] (*probatio materialiter sumta, ratio probans*) is the ground from which the truth can be clearly known; and such grounds are the criteria of truth (§ 94). The consequence of the proof (*probatio formaliter sumta, consequentia*) is the connection of the truth with the ground of proof (§ 15). Every proof thus consists of the ground of proof and the consequence, and it may produce either a complete or an incomplete certainty (§ 159). If it produces the former, it is entitled a sufficient proof or a demonstration (*probatio sufficiens, demonstratio*); if the latter, it is an insufficient proof (*probatio insufficiens*). The latter is treated at length in the logic of probable knowledge.",[]],"meier:192":["The knowledge of a truth is either demonstrable (*cognitio demonstrabilis*) or indemonstrable (*cognitio indemonstrabilis*). The latter becomes certain to us as soon as we know it distinctly; the former does not. The latter is completely certain without proof; the former is not. We must take care to hold neither begged knowledge nor demonstrable knowledge to be indemonstrable (§ 168).",[]],"meier:193":["If we would attain complete certainty of a truth through and from a proof, or if a proof is to be a demonstration (§ 191), then (1) every ground of proof contained in the proof must be completely certain. All demonstrable grounds of proof must therefore be proved in turn until we arrive at nothing but indemonstrable grounds of proof. (2) The consequence must be completely certain (§ 191). Besides these two requirements nothing further is required of a proof if it is to be a demonstration (§ 15).",[]],"meier:194":["If (1) even a single ground of proof in a proof is false, or (2) uncertain; [if] (3) a single consequence in the proof is false, or (4) uncertain; or (5) if several of these faults are met with together in a proof, it cannot be a demonstration (§ 193).",[]],"meier:195":["Just as someone may take an insufficient proof for a demonstration, so someone may also take a demonstration for an insufficient proof, or even for a false proof (§ 165). Consequently we must by no means reject a demonstration because it does not have its proper effect upon our mind.",[]],"meier:196":["Through a demonstration we seek to become certain either that something is true or that something is false (§§ 191, 156). In the first case a truth can be demonstrated in two ways: (1) immediately (*demonstratio directa, ostensiva*), when we derive the truth from its criteria; (2) mediately (*demonstratio indirecta, apogogica, deductio ad absurdum*), when we demonstrate the incorrectness of its opposite and infer its truth from that. Similarly we can demonstrate that something is false, or refute it (*refutatio*): (a) mediately (*refutatio mediata*), when we demonstrate the truth of its opposite; (b) immediately (*refutatio immediata*), when we derive its incorrectness from its criteria. We can prove that something is false, however, (α) by proving that it is impossible and ungrounded, (β) that it is contrary to other undeniable truths, and (γ) that something false follows from it.",[]],"meier:197":["A demonstration produces either a distinctly or an indistinctly complete certainty (§§ 191, 188). Demonstrations of the latter kind are aesthetic demonstrations (*demonstratio aesthetica*); those of the former kind are logical, philosophical and learned demonstrations (*demonstratio logica, philosophica, erudita*). A logical demonstration which produces mathematical certainty is a mathematical demonstration (*demonstratio mathematica*) (§ 161).",[]],"meier:198":["We can always infer: (1) if the whole proof is correct and certain, the thing proved is also correct and certain; (2) if the thing proved is false and uncertain, there is a fault in the proof (§ 194). But we cannot infer: (1) if the proof is false, the thing proved is also false; (2) if the proof is uncertain, the thing proved is likewise only uncertain; (3) what we cannot prove is false; (4) if the thing proved is true and certain, the proof is also true and certain.",[]],"meier:199":["The demonstrative spirit (*spiritus demonstrationis*) consists in a man's sufficient skill in demonstrating, together with a natural impulse towards such certain knowledge as is attained by demonstrating. Without this spirit no one can demonstrate, and no one who lacks it ought to venture upon the business.",[]],"meier:200":["Excessive love of demonstrating is the itch for demonstration (*pruritus demonstrandi*). This learned malady shows itself in the following ways: (1) when we seek to demonstrate what we cannot and need not demonstrate; (2) when we even demonstrate in a spurious manner, merely in order to awaken among the simple-minded [*Blödsinnige*] the semblance of demonstration; (3) when we stop short at mere demonstrating, and do not duly use and apply the demonstrated truths; (4) when we are pedants in demonstrating, or utterly despise whatever is not demonstrated, whether it really is not demonstrated or we merely do not hold it to be so; (5) when, like a charlatan, we exalt demonstrating too highly and boast too much of it, even when we are demonstrating useless trifles.",[]],"meier:201":["A sensation (*sensatio*) is a representation of a present thing; and in so far as we sense something clearly, we experience it. Experience (*experientia*) therefore consists in that knowledge which is clear through sensing. Clear sensations are immediate experience (*experientia immediata*). The rest of clear knowledge, which is derived from immediate experience by a shorter proof, is entitled mediate experience (*experientia mediata*). For example, that I think is a sensation and an immediate experience; but that I have a faculty of thinking is a mediate experience.",[]],"meier:202":["When something is certain to us, it is certain either from experience or from other grounds; and in the first case either from our own experience or from the experience of other people. Consequently we have a threefold source of all proofs (§ 191), namely our own experience, the experience of other people, and other grounds which are not experiences.",[]],"meier:203":["(1) When we conduct a proof from our own immediate experience, we need only represent clearly to ourselves a single case in which we have sensed something (§ 201). (2) In proofs from mediate experience, not only must at least one immediate experience be adduced, but the remaining grounds of proof, together with the consequence, must also be certain (§§ 201, 193). (3) That of which we are certain even through immediate experience is not an indemonstrable knowledge (§§ 192, 201). (4) Experience gives us a complete certainty, since it contains all the criteria of the truth of the object (§§ 201, 94, 96). (5) Experience gives us only a complete certainty of the second order (§ 189), and (6) it represents the truth to us only from external criteria (§ 94).",[]],"meier:204":["When we prove a truth from other grounds of proof which are not experience, we conduct a proof from reason (*probatio ex ratione*). In such a proof, if it is to be a demonstration, (1) no ground of proof must occur which is an experience, and (2) all its grounds of proof must be completely certain without experience (§ 193).",[]],"meier:205":["All experience, and what we prove from it, is knowledge from behind (*cognitio a posteriore*); the rest of rational knowledge, on the other hand, is entitled knowledge from before (*cognitio a priore*). When we are certain of a truth both from our own experience and from reason, this is entitled the marriage of reason and experience (*connubium rationis et experientiae*).",[]],"meier:206":["We become certain from the experience of other people by means of belief [*Glaube*]. Whoever gives out an actual thing as true in order that another may also hold it true is entitled a witness (*testis*), and his act a testimony (*testimonium, testari*). To believe (*credere*) is to accept something on account of a testimony. Belief (*fides, fides historica*) is the assent which we give to a thing on account of a testimony. The object of belief consists in past, present and future things, but not in other truths.",[]],"meier:207":["The authority of a witness (*autoritas testis*) consists in that degree of his honour by virtue of which he is held worthy of imitation in his knowledge. We can believe no witness who has no authority with us. This authority consists (1) in the competence of the witness (*dexteritas testis*), when he possesses sufficient powers not only to obtain a correct experience but also to express [*bezeichnen*] it in a correct manner; (2) in the sincerity of the witness (*sinceritas testis*), that is, in the inclination of his will to express his experiences as he holds them to be true. Neither of these can, without the other, procure for a witness the authority due to him.",[]],"meier:208":["An eye-witness (*testis oculatus*) is a witness who has himself experienced the thing to which he testifies. An ear-witness (*testis auritus*) is not an eye-witness; he has only experienced the testimony of others concerning the thing.",[]],"meier:209":["The competence of an eye-witness requires: (1) that he must have been present at the thing to which he testifies; (2) that he must be in a position to obtain a correct experience; (3) that he must have a good and faithful memory, or else write down his experiences at once; (4) that he must possess the gift of expressing his own knowledge in a correct and sufficient manner.",[]],"meier:210":["An ear-witness is, in respect of the testimony to which he testifies, an eye-witness, and must accordingly possess all the competence required for that (§ 209). But in so far as he is an ear-witness, the following are required for his competence. (1) He must repeat only the testimonies of competent eye-witnesses, and must therefore always be able to cite a competent eye-witness who is his authority [*Gewährsmann*]. A common report (*fama*) is a testimony of many ear-witnesses concerning a thing whose eye-witness is unknown. These ear-witnesses either live at one time, and then their report is a public rumour (*rumor sine capite*); or they live at different times, and then it is an oral tradition (*oralis traditio*). Common report lacks the necessary authority. (2) He must possess understanding enough to grasp correctly what he hears or reads. (3) He must possess a faithful memory, in order to pass the reports on to others just as he received them.",[]],"meier:211":["In the sincerity of a witness everything depends simply on his good will not to lie §.207. A witness who expects from a true testimony either nothing but good, or more good than evil, and from a lie either nothing but evil, or more evil than good, speaks the truth. In the opposite case he lies. This good or evil may concern his soul, or his body, or his outward condition. He may hope for it or fear it from God, from those men to whom he gives the testimony, or from those of whom he testifies, etc., whether or not he deceives himself in the matter.",[]],"meier:212":["The certainty of a testimony rests 1) on the internal marks of its truth §.95. 96; 2) on the external marks of its truth, namely, on the sufficient authority [Ansehen] of the witness, which must be at least very probable §.207, 94. The testimonies of a competent and sincere witness cannot be false.",[]],"meier:213":["What we believe, we do not [obtain by] begging [the question] §.212. 168. Incredulity (*incredulitas*) is the habit of not believing a certain or probable testimony. Credulity (*credulitas*) is the habit of believing an improbable testimony. Both faults are irrational §.174.",[]],"meier:214":["A witness is credible (*testis fide dignus*) when it can be known in a learned manner, at least with probability, that he has sufficient authority. The testimony of such a witness is a credible testimony (*testimonium fide dignum*). Rational or seeing belief (*fides oculata, rationalis*) is the habit of believing only credible witnesses.",[]],"meier:215":["There is 1) a union of experience and belief (*connubium experientiae et fidei*), when we know something both from our own experience and from credible testimonies; 2) a union of belief and reason (*connubium fidei et rationis*), when we are certain of a thing both from reason and through belief; and 3) a union of reason, belief and experience (*connubium rationis experientiae et fidei*), when we know a truth convincingly by all three ways. The union of belief with the other sources of knowledge is also entitled mixed belief (*fides mixta*); but when we accept something solely on the strength of belief, this is entitled pure belief (*fides pura*). Since we can demonstrate neither the competence nor the sincerity of human witnesses, belief yields only probability, and at most only a moral certainty §.175. Of practical learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:216":["A [piece of] knowledge is practical (*cognitio practica*) in so far as it can move us in a noticeable manner to do or to omit an action. All more perfect knowledge which is not practical is entitled speculative knowledge (*cognitio speculativa, speculatio*). All learned knowledge is therefore either practical or speculative.",[]],"meier:217":["Knowledge in which we represent that something ought to be done or omitted is also entitled practical, in so far as it is opposed to theoretical knowledge (*cognitio theoretica, theoria*), that is, to knowledge which does not represent to us that something ought to be done or omitted. All learned knowledge is either practical or theoretical, and both kinds belong either to practical or to speculative knowledge [in the sense of] §.216.",[]],"meier:218":["Practical knowledge is better than speculative knowledge §.216, because a greater harmony of the manifold is to be met with in it §.22. Whoever, therefore, would improve his learned knowledge as far as possible must guard against speculation, and seek nothing but practical knowledge.",[]],"meier:219":["No true learned knowledge is speculative by its nature. It is so only through a defect of insight in the learned man, who cannot, or will not, perceive its connection with human conduct. In the latter case the learned man disgraces himself.",[]],"meier:220":["It is irrational to wish to regard as speculation: 1) all profound, determinate, exact and difficult learned knowledge; 2) all learning whatsoever; 3) all theory; 4) that learned knowledge which is not practical in this or that man; 5) all knowledge which has no immediate influence on the moral conduct of a man.",[]],"meier:221":["Knowledge is practical either on account of its object (*cognitio obiective practica*), or on account of its constitution (*cognitio subiective practica*), or both at once. In the first case it must have an object that can be known in the manner required for practical knowledge. In the second case it is so constituted that it can act upon the faculty of desire [Begehrungskraft].",[]],"meier:222":["Learned knowledge is practical on account of its object: 1) when the truths known are good and useful. And in this respect all true learned knowledge is practical §.39. 221.",[]],"meier:223":["Learned knowledge is practical on account of its object: 2) in so far as it lies at the basis of the rational actions by which human perfection is preserved §.221, and [in so far as] its absence is at once noticed in a man's actions.",[]],"meier:224":["Learned knowledge is practical on account of its object: 3) when its object consists in the rules which we must observe if we would preserve our entire welfare in the best way §.221.",[]],"meier:225":["Learned knowledge is practical on account of its object: 4) when its object has, in a remote and mediate way, an influence on our entire good conduct §.221; for example, the knowledge of those truths without which the other practical truths §.222-224 could either not be known by us men at all, or at least not in a learned manner.",[]],"meier:226":["Since no man is in a position to know all practical truths, that learned knowledge is entitled pre-eminently and simply practical (*cognitio obiective et comparative s. eminenter practica*) which is practical in a higher degree, for example, which is noticeably more useful to us than some other, and which has an immediate or very near influence on our conduct.",[]],"meier:227":["All learned knowledge is entitled comparatively speculative (*cognitio comparative speculativa*) when it is practical in a very small degree; for example, 1) when it has a very small and imperceptible influence on our regular conduct; 2) when it is of very little use; 3) when it hinders us from more necessary and more useful knowledge.",[]],"meier:228":["Owing to differences in manner of life and in principal science, one man may rightly regard a piece of learned knowledge as practical, and another as speculative §.226. 227. Indeed, someone may, owing to the weakness of his cognitive powers, and from negligence, fail to perceive the connection of a piece of learned knowledge with our conduct; he will therefore regard it as speculative knowledge.",[]],"meier:229":["In so far as learned knowledge is practical, in so far is it also useful. Accordingly, 1) no truth and no learned knowledge is to be regarded as wholly useless §.222. 2) Learned knowledge is not useless because it is of no use to this or that man, and may even be harmful to him. 3) Learned knowledge which is not useful in one respect may be useful in another. 4) We must not infer: what is not useful to us now will not be useful to us in the future either.",[]],"meier:230":["The more benefits a piece of learned knowledge affords, the greater these are, and the greater and more manifold its influence on our entire conduct, the more practical it is §.216.",[]],"meier:231":["In order that every learned man may improve his learned knowledge as far as possible, he must 1) know most perfectly those truths which are practical in the highest degree. The degree of diligence and of perfection in learned knowledge must be proportioned to the degree of the practical in its object §.70. 2) Everyone must, if possible, choose as his principal science that part of learning which is practical in the highest degree §.63.",[]],"meier:232":["Knowledge which is practical by its constitution is entitled living and moving knowledge (*cognitio viva, movens*). Living rational knowledge (*cognitio rationalis viva*) is rational knowledge so constituted that rational desires and aversions are aroused by it. In other words, it contains motives [Bewegungsgründe] (*motiva*), that is, such rational representations of good and evil as give rise to desires and aversions. All rational knowledge which is not so constituted is entitled dead rational knowledge (*cognitio rationalis mortua*).",[]],"meier:233":["That constitution of knowledge in virtue of which it produces desires and aversions is entitled the life of knowledge (*cognitionis vita*). When this life consists in motives §.232, it is entitled the rational life of knowledge (*vita rationalis cognitionis*); the knowledge then touches and moves [us] in a rational manner.",[]],"meier:234":["Learned knowledge may 1) be exceedingly practical in respect of its object, and yet be dead; 2) it may be false and exceedingly living, so that the life of knowledge is no mark of its truth; 3) learned knowledge may be very extensive, great, correct, certain and distinct, and yet be dead §.232. Life is therefore a further perfection of learned knowledge, distinct from all its other perfections.",[]],"meier:235":["The practical element in learned knowledge becomes actually practical only through life. Living rational knowledge is therefore more perfect than dead, even should both be otherwise adorned with all the other logical perfections in equal degree.",[]],"meier:236":["If rational and learned knowledge is to move [us] rationally, it must 1) be intuitive knowledge. Knowledge is intuitive (*cognitio intuitiva*) when we represent the object more strongly than its signs; if, however, we represent the signs more strongly than the object, the knowledge is symbolic (*cognitio symbolica*). All learned knowledge is either intuitive or symbolic. If attention, reflection and deliberation are occupied chiefly with the object of learned knowledge, it becomes distinct intuitive knowledge.",[]],"meier:237":["Pleasure (*voluptas*) is the intuitive knowledge of perfection, and the intuitive knowledge of imperfection is displeasure (*taedium*). Knowledge causes in us indifference (*indifferentia*) when it causes us neither pleasure nor displeasure. If, then, rational and learned knowledge is to move [us] rationally, it must 2) arouse pleasure and displeasure: a) concerning itself it must arouse in us nothing but pleasure. For in accordance with all the rules of logic it must be exceedingly perfect, and the feeling of its perfection must impel us to desire and to attain it. He to whom it is a matter of indifference whether he knows a thing in a learned manner or not, has learned knowledge that is not living.",[]],"meier:238":["If rational and learned knowledge is to move [us] rationally, it must b) arouse concerning its object either pleasure, or vexation, or both at once, by representing that object to us in an intuitive manner either as good, or as evil, or as both at once. Learned knowledge then contains motives §.232, not only for desiring itself §.237, but also for either desiring or abhorring its object.",[]],"meier:239":["If rational and learned knowledge is to move us rationally, it must 3) represent to us in a learned manner that all the good and all the evil on account of which it gives us pleasure or displeasure will actually exist in our future states.",[]],"meier:240":["If rational and learned knowledge is to move us rationally, it must 4) represent to us in a learned manner that it is, or may be, in our power to make actual the good on account of which it gives us pleasure, and to prevent the evil on account of which it causes us displeasure.",[]],"meier:241":["If learned knowledge is to be truly living, it must 1) cause no equilibrium, or at least soon remove it. We are in a state of equilibrium (*aequilibrium*) when the motives on both sides are equal and opposed to one another. 2) It must outweigh the sensuous life of indistinct knowledge, or else this [life] must not contradict it at all.",[]],"meier:242":["When rational and learned knowledge is truly living, it produces a strong and lasting resolution, which breaks forth in deed, inasmuch as we actually act in conformity with the learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:243":["The genuine life of learned knowledge (*vita vera cognitionis eruditae*) consists in its true use (*usus verus cognitionis eruditae*), when we bring about all its true benefits, or actually make ourselves more perfect by means of it. If, however, we thereby make ourselves less perfect, or only apparently more perfect, in this consists its misuse (*abusus cognitionis eruditae*), and this is its spurious life (*vita cognitionis eruditae spuria*). Perfect learned knowledge must be living in a genuine manner.",[]],"meier:244":["The more and the greater the motives that learned knowledge contains, the better these are known, and the more and the greater the desires and aversions aroused by it, the more living is the learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:245":["We must not infer: 1) learned knowledge which does not move me does not move others either; 2) that which moves me moves others also.",[]],"meier:246":["The want of life in learned knowledge arises from ignorance §.236-240. 41. According as this [ignorance] is necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy §.43.50. 51, so also is the want of life. It cannot be demanded that all learned knowledge should move [us] constantly and in equal degree.",[]],"meier:247":["The greater and more practical the objects are, the more strongly must the learned knowledge of them move [us]; the smaller and less practical they are, the less must the learned knowledge of them move [us] §.70. All our learned knowledge must move us in a certain respect §.237.",[]],"meier:248":["Everyone must choose as his principal science that part of learning which by its nature is capable of the most living learned knowledge; and everyone must seek to know his principal science in the most living manner §.63. 64. Of learned concepts.",[]],"meier:249":["A concept (*conceptus*) is a representation of a thing in a being which possesses the faculty of thinking. All our representations are therefore concepts.",[]],"meier:250":["There are as many different kinds of concepts as there are different kinds of knowledge §.249. Concepts are, accordingly, either learned concepts (*conceptus eruditus, logicus*), or not §.21. The former is a distinct concept which is logically perfect in a noticeable degree. An unlearned concept (*conceptus ineruditus*) is a concept which possesses no noticeable degree of logical perfection; and it is then either a beautiful concept, or not §.19. The former is an aesthetic concept (*conceptus pulcher, aestheticus*), the latter a common concept (*conceptus vulgaris*). A learned concept is either at the same time beautiful, or not. The latter is a merely learned concept (*conceptus mere eruditus*); the former a concept which is not merely learned (*conceptus non mere eruditus*). And such are the best concepts §.32.",[]],"meier:251":["A learned concept must conform as fully as possible to the rules of the extent, the magnitude, the truth, the clearness, the certainty, and the practical character of learned knowledge §.41-248.",[]],"meier:252":["The object of learned concepts is not determined by their nature. A learned concept is possible of every possible thing §.250.",[]],"meier:253":["Everyone need only improve in accordance with logic chiefly those concepts which belong to his principal science §.64, and in every system of doctrine chiefly the principal concepts. It is neither possible nor necessary to improve all our concepts in accordance with logic, and just as little to improve all our learned concepts in equal degree. The greater a concept is, the more must it be improved §.70.",[]],"meier:254":["We have only three ways of arriving at concepts: experience, abstraction, and arbitrary combination.",[]],"meier:255":["All our sensations are concepts §.249. 201. An empirical concept (*conceptus per experientiam formatus*) is a concept which we acquire through experience; for example, the concepts of the changes of our soul, of our body, and of other things outside us. We acquire an empirical concept either through immediate or through mediate experience §.201.",[]],"meier:256":["Through immediate experience we can acquire concepts only of actual things in so far as they are present to us; and these concepts contain only affirmative and changeable marks. Mediate empirical concepts can represent to us other objects also, and other [features] in them §.201.",[]],"meier:257":["Since empirical concepts can be learned only in so far as they are distinct §.250, they must be carefully analysed §.142. This work is often furthered 1) by the use of the instruments [Waffen] of the senses, e.g. magnifying glasses; 2) by the anatomy of corporeal things, through which their parts and the manner of their composition are known; and in this way we obtain the concept of the essence of a corporeal thing; 3) by attention to that from which a thing arises and how it arises, when we are present at its arising; and from this too we obtain concepts of the essence; 4) by seeking to experience the object often in different circumstances; for we then more easily discover the best marks, whereby the empirical concept becomes complete [ausführlich].",[]],"meier:258":["All empirical concepts are true and certain §.202, and they represent objects to us as these are constituted; otherwise we should be inhabitants of another world. If, however, we take for an empirical concept a concept which is not one, or take for an object of the empirical concept something which is not such, it does indeed seem as if the empirical concept were false; but the error lies in another concept.",[]],"meier:259":["We form a concept through logical abstraction (*conceptus per abstractionem logicam formatus*) when we compare agreeing concepts of different things, and represent to ourselves distinctly only the marks which they have in common. To this end 1) take several concepts which are different and at the same time similar, e.g. a rational animal and an irrational animal; 2) analyse each of them §.142; 3) abstract [abstrahire] the marks in which they differ, or obscure them; 4) combine the remaining marks in one concept, e.g. an animal.",[]],"meier:260":["All concepts formed by logical abstraction are abstracted or abstract concepts (*conceptus abstractus, notio*). Concepts which are not abstracted are entitled singular concepts (*conceptus singularis, idea*), e.g. Leibniz. All immediate empirical concepts are singular concepts §.255.201. What is represented as a mark of another is contained in it and belongs to it (*in altero contineri, ipsi convenire*). The abstracted concept is therefore contained in all those concepts from which it can be abstracted §.259. The abstracted concept contains under itself those from which it has been abstracted, and these are contained under it (*conceptus alios sub se continet, et conceptus sub alio continentur seu ad eum referuntur*).",[]],"meier:261":["An abstracted concept is entitled a higher concept (*conceptus superior*) in so far as it contains others under itself; but in so far as a concept is contained under another, it is entitled a lower concept (*conceptus inferior*). The lower concept always contains marks which are not contained in the higher §.259, and these are entitled the difference of the lower concepts (*differentia conceptus inferioris*). An abstracted concept which comprehends under itself only singular concepts is entitled a species (*species*); one which contains abstracted concepts also under itself is entitled a genus (*genus*).",[]],"meier:262":["The sum of all the concepts contained under an abstracted concept is its sphere (*sphaera notionis*). The more abstract and higher a concept is, therefore, that is, the more often logical abstraction has been repeated in it, the greater is its sphere. An abstracted concept belongs either to more concepts than those contained under another, or to fewer, or neither. In the first case it is a wider concept than the other (*conceptus latior*); in the second, a narrower (*conceptus angustior*); and in the third they are reciprocal concepts (*conceptus reciproci*), neither of which is wider than the other. In so far as an abstracted concept belongs to all that are contained under another, it is entitled a universal concept (*conceptus universalis*); in so far as it does not belong to all of them, a particular concept (*conceptus particularis*). An abstracted concept may be entitled a higher and a lower, a wider and a narrower, a universal and a particular concept, in different respects. Every abstracted concept is in a certain respect universal. Abstract learned knowledge is therefore entitled universal learned knowledge (*cognitio erudita universalis*).",[]],"meier:263":["In universal knowledge we can 1) infer: what belongs to, or contradicts, the higher concepts also belongs to, or contradicts, all the lower concepts contained under them §.260. 2) We cannot infer: what belongs to, or contradicts, a lower concept also belongs to, or contradicts, other lower concepts which together with it belong to a higher concept §.261. 3) We can infer: what belongs to, or contradicts, each and every one of the lower concepts also belongs to, or contradicts, their higher concept §.259. 261.",[]],"meier:264":["Abstract knowledge promotes 1) the distinctness and completeness [Vollständigkeit] of knowledge §.143. For the more abstract it is, the less it contains §.259, and the more easily, therefore, can it be thought through without much confusion. 2) The extent of knowledge §.263. 25. 3) The thoroughness of knowledge §.263. 163. 4) The utility and use of learned knowledge §.39.",[]],"meier:265":["An abstracted concept contains nothing that is not contained in the lower concepts §.259. If these, then, are true, it cannot possibly be false. If, therefore, one would prove an abstracted concept, one need only show that it has been abstracted from true concepts. If from a false concept one abstracts the marks which contradict the rest, one obtains a true concept §.95.",[]],"meier:266":["A concept is formed by learned arbitrary combination (*combinatio conceptuum arbitraria, logica, erudita, philosophica, rationalis*) when we represent to ourselves as one two concepts which we have known in a learned manner not to be incompatible with each other. To this end take 1) an abstracted concept, whether it was abstracted long ago or is only now being abstracted; 2) a difference of a lower concept, of which we are either already convinced, or only afterwards become convinced, that it does not contradict the abstracted concept. This difference has either been left out from the abstract concept through abstraction, or not. In the first case we obtain a lower concept from which we abstracted the abstract concept; in the second, a new lower concept. 3) Represent the abstract concept, taken together with this difference, as one concept.",[]],"meier:267":["A learned concept which has been formed by arbitrary combination must be proved or refuted. Both are done 1) from experience, by showing that its objects either are or are not actual; 2) from reason, either mediately or immediately §.196; for example, by showing either that and how its objects can become actual, or that they cannot become actual.",[]],"meier:268":["A definition, or logical definition (*definitio, definitio logica*), is a determinate concept of a thing. The object of the definition is the thing defined, or the concept defined (*definitum*) §.151.",[]],"meier:269":["A description (*descriptio*) is an indeterminate concept, and it is either a complete or a[n in]complete description §.151 (*descriptio completa, vel incompleta*). No description is a definition, and still less must it be taken for one §.268. Descriptions may, nevertheless, be very perfect, useful and necessary learned concepts.",[]],"meier:270":["Definitions must conform to six rules. 1) A definition must conform to the rules of the extent of learned knowledge §.41-65. It must, therefore, be a complete concept §.268.151. 55. Consequently, a) it must not contain fewer marks than are required for a complete concept; nor b) more than are necessary §.269. The definition must therefore be, of all possible complete concepts of a thing, the shortest, that is, it must contain as few marks as completeness permits. c) It must not be wider than the thing defined §.262; its marks taken together must therefore belong to no other thing than to the thing defined alone. d) It must not be narrower than the thing defined §.262. The concept defined and the definition are therefore reciprocal concepts §.262.",[]],"meier:271":["If in a system of doctrine the higher concepts are defined before the lower, then, when we have to define a concept which is not the highest, we can compose its definition from the next higher concept and from the difference §.261. The definitions thereby become short and complete; they hang together more systematically; they are more in keeping with the nature of the lower concepts; and they open an easier way to demonstrations.",[]],"meier:272":["Since the concept defined and the definition are reciprocal concepts §.270, we can infer: 1) to whatever the concept defined belongs, the definition also belongs; 2) to whatever the definition belongs, the concept defined also belongs; 3) to whatever the concept defined does not belong, the definition also does not belong; 4) to whatever the definition does not belong, the concept defined also does not belong §.262.",[]],"meier:273":["2) A definition must conform to the rules of the magnitude of learned knowledge §.66-91. It must therefore consist of the greatest, most important and most fruitful marks, namely of the essence, the essential parts [wesentliche Stücke] and the attributes [Eigenschaften], and, as often as possible, of affirmative marks §.115-121. It is therefore a fault a) if a definition consists of contingent qualities; it would then not even be a complete concept §.121; b) if it consists of relations; it would then likewise be no complete concept §.121; c) if it consists, without need, of negative marks. When, in a system of doctrine, opposed concepts have to be defined, the opposition must be evident from the definitions; and one of the two must therefore be defined negatively.",[]],"meier:274":["3) A definition must conform to the rules of the truth of learned knowledge §.92-114. The truth of definitions requires, further, that they conform to all the rules which must be observed in definitions. A legitimate definition (*definitio legitima*) conforms to all the rules of definitions; one which violates one or more of these rules is an illegitimate definition (*definitio illegitima*).",[]],"meier:275":["4) A definition must conform to all the rules of the distinctness of learned knowledge §.115-155. It may, however, be an incomplete [unvollständig] concept §.147. Now since all its marks must be clear §.137, we may either presuppose them as clear, partly from common life and partly from other systems of doctrine; or else we must first define them before putting them as marks into the definition §.268.",[]],"meier:276":["If a definition is absolutely obscure, or has absolutely obscure marks, it is no definition at all §.275. 125. If, however, it is only relatively obscure, or contains marks which are only relatively obscure, it may indeed be a good definition, but not for him to whom it is obscure §.125.",[]],"meier:277":["The concept defined is no clearer than it itself is. In order, therefore, that the definition may not become obscure and indistinct §.275, we must take care that the concept defined is not put, either as an immediate or as a mediate mark, into its own definition §.116. When the latter happens, the fault is entitled circularity in defining (*circulus in definiendo*).",[]],"meier:278":["5) A definition must conform to the rules of the certainty of learned knowledge §.155-215. Consequently, in the case of every definition we must prove a) that it is either a correct empirical concept §.258, or a correct abstracted concept §.265, or a correct arbitrary concept §.267; b) that it is legitimate §.274.",[]],"meier:279":["6) A definition must conform to the rules of practical learned knowledge §.216-248. Consequently, we must choose for a definition the most important and most fruitful marks, those from which can most easily be proved what is most useful and serviceable among the things we must investigate regarding the thing defined, in order to know it in a practical manner.",[]],"meier:280":["A definition either represents the essence of the thing defined, or it does not §.273. The former is a real definition (*definitio realis, genetica*), the latter a nominal definition (*definitio nominalis*). A nominal definition contains either the essential parts of the thing defined, or its attributes §.273. The former is an essential nominal definition (*definitio essentialis*), the latter an accidental nominal definition (*definitio accidentalis*).",[]],"meier:281":["If we would frame a real definition, we must 1) seek to know the essence of the thing to be defined, either through mediate experience, e.g. §.257, or through abstraction, or through a proof from reason, or through arbitrary combination; 2) form a determinate concept of the essence §.280.",[]],"meier:282":["If we would frame a nominal definition, we must 1) seek to know the essential parts or attributes of the thing to be defined, either from mediate experience §.256, or through a proof from reason, or from the essence, if this is already known to us. 2) We must select those essential parts and attributes, and as many of them, as are necessary for a perfect and legitimate definition §.270-279.",[]],"meier:283":["Since the things to be defined may have many essential parts and attributes, we may select at pleasure those which are to make up a nominal definition §.282, provided only that we otherwise observe the rules of definitions. And in so far nominal definitions are arbitrary.",[]],"meier:284":["Just as we cannot define all concepts §.139.150. 268, so too we need not define all concepts, but only those which belong within the horizon of our learned knowledge, and without the definition of which our learned knowledge could not attain the required degree of its perfection. Extravagance in defining is entitled the itch for definition (*pruritus definiendi*).",[]],"meier:285":["The logical division of concepts (*divisio logica*) consists in a distinct representation of all the lower concepts which are opposed to one another and are contained under one and the same higher concept. This higher concept is entitled the divided concept (*divisum*), and the lower concepts the members of the division (*membra dividentia*). If, then, we would make a logical division, 1) we must take an abstracted concept; 2) discover all the mutually opposed differences of its lower concepts; 3) combine these differences one after another, by way of opposition, with the higher concept. For example: all concepts are either obscure or clear concepts.",[]],"meier:286":["A division of a member of a division is entitled a subdivision (*subdivisio*); and different divisions of one concept, made in different respects, are entitled codivisions (*codivisiones*). It is self-evident what the divided concept of the subdivision (*subdivisum*) and of the codivisions (*codivisum*) is; and likewise what the members of the subdivision (*membra subdividentia*) and of the codivisions (*membra codividentia*) are.",[]],"meier:287":["If a logical division is to be correct, 1) the divided concept must not be wider than the members of the division taken together by way of opposition (*disiunctive*); in other words, no member of the division may be omitted, since otherwise not all the mutually opposed lower concepts would have been adduced §.285. 262. 2) The divided concept must not be narrower than the members of the division taken together by way of opposition §.262; otherwise a concept which is not a lower concept of the divided concept would be mixed in among the members of the division §.285. Consequently the divided concept and the members of the division, taken together by way of opposition, are reciprocal concepts §.262, and the division must exhaust the divided concept.",[]],"meier:288":["3) The members of the division must be so opposed to one another that none belongs to any other, §.285. 260. 4) The divided concept must contradict no member of the division, for it is contained in each of them, §.285. 260. 5) The members of a subdivision must not be placed among the members of the division. Otherwise not all the members would be opposed to one another, nos. 3, 4, §.286. 6) The number of the members of the division must be determined solely by the nature of the divided concept and by the purpose for which it is divided.",[]],"meier:289":["Whatever the divided concept belongs to, one member of the division also belongs to. Whatever one member of the division belongs to, the divided concept also belongs to. Whatever the divided concept does not belong to, none of the members of the division belongs to. Whatever none of the members of the division belongs to, the divided concept does not belong to either, §.287.",[]],"meier:290":["Logical divisions serve three purposes. 1) They further the discovery of learned concepts by arbitrary combination, §.285. 266. 2) They enable us to set our abstracted concepts in proper order and connection, and so to retain them the more easily. 3) They convince us of the universality of our abstract knowledge.",[]],"meier:291":["In making divisions we must take care not to multiply them, together with their subdivisions, excessively, since otherwise great confusion arises. We must also avoid everything forced in them. Of learned judgments.",[]],"meier:292":["Concepts which belong to one another, §.260, agree with one another. Those which do not belong to one another are opposed to one another, or conflict with one another (*repugnare*). The agreement and the conflict of several concepts are the logical relations of concepts (*logica conceptuum relatio*). A judgment (*iudicium*) is a representation of a logical relation of certain concepts. In so far as everything is abstracted from it without which its truth can [still] be known in a learned manner, it is entitled a logical judgment (*iudicium logicum*). A logical judgment is a learned judgment (*iudicium eruditum*) when it conforms, as far as possible, to all the rules of learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:293":["The concept to which, as we represent in a judgment, another concept does or does not belong, is the subject (*subiectum*). The other concept, which we represent as belonging or not belonging to the subject, is the predicate (*praedicatum*). The representation of the agreement of several concepts is the copula (*copula*). Negation (*negatio*) is the representation of the absence of a thing, and the representation of the absence of the copula is the negation of the copula (*negatio copulae*). It is therefore the representation of the conflict of the predicate with the subject, §.292.",[]],"meier:294":["In a logical judgment we represent either that the predicate belongs to the subject or that it does not belong to it, §.292. 293. The former is an affirmative judgment (*iudicium affirmans, affirmativum*), the latter a negative judgment (*iudicium negans, negativum*). For example: the soul can think; matter cannot think. In a negative judgment the copula is negated, §.293. If there is a negation in the subject or in the predicate, or in both at once, but the copula itself is not negated, the judgment is affirmative; it is entitled an infinite judgment (*iudicium infinitum*). All negative judgments can therefore be transformed into affirmative judgments by removing the negation from the copula and attaching it to the predicate. For example: the soul is not mortal; the soul is immortal. The quality of judgments (*qualitas iudicii*) consists in their affirmation and negation.",[]],"meier:295":["An affirmative judgment is true if the predicate belongs to the subject, and belongs to it in just the way in which it is affirmed of it. It is false if the predicate does not belong to the subject, or at least does not belong to it in the way in which it is affirmed of it. A negative judgment is true if the predicate does not belong to the subject, and that in the way in which it is denied of it. It is false if the predicate belongs to the subject, or at least is not opposed to it in the way in which it is denied of it, §.294. 99. If a true judgment is held to be false, or a false one to be true, the judgment is erroneous, §.109.",[]],"meier:296":["The truth and falsity of a judgment lie in the copula and in its negation, §.295. 293. Consequently, 1) the subject and predicate of a false judgment may be true; 2) the subject and predicate of a true affirmative judgment may be false; 3) in a true negative judgment the subject may be true and the predicate false, or conversely.",[]],"meier:297":["All true judgments have a ground, and a sufficient ground, of their truth, §.16. This ground is entitled the condition of judgments (*hypothesis, conditio iudicii*). Consequently the truth and falsity of judgments can be known from it. It is therefore the criterion and the argument [*Beweisthum*] of truth, §.94. 191.",[]],"meier:298":["The conditions of judgments are 1) either sufficient or insufficient conditions, §.297. 119. 191; 2) either internal or external conditions, §.297. 94; 3) either absolutely necessary or contingent conditions. The former are the essence, the essential parts, the attributes, or the definition of the subject; the latter are its contingent qualities and relations, §.297. 121. 273. When the contingent condition of a judgment is combined with its subject, it is entitled the determination or limitation of the judgment (*determinatio et limitatio iudicii*).",[]],"meier:299":["1) If the condition of a judgment is internal, absolutely necessary and sufficient, it is inseparable from the subject. Whether we think it or not, it is still present; consequently the predicate belongs or does not belong to the subject, according as it is affirmed or denied of it. Truth does not then require that this condition be combined with the judgment, §.295. 2) If, however, the condition is contingent, it is sometimes present and sometimes not, and the judgment would be sometimes true and sometimes not true. Truth therefore requires that such conditions be combined with the judgment. A determinate judgment (*iudicium determinatum, limitatum*) is a judgment which has a determination; a judgment which is not determined is an indeterminate judgment (*iudicium indeterminatum, illimitatum*).",[]],"meier:300":["The analysis of a judgment (*analysis, resolutio iudicii*) consists in attending successively to all its parts. All judgments can be analysed, §.139. By this labour we not only find the proofs of judgments, §.297, but also learn to grasp them correctly and to present them to others.",[]],"meier:301":["The subject of a judgment is either a singular or an abstract concept, §.293. 260. In the former case the judgment is singular (*iudicium singulare*), in the latter common (*iudicium commune*). A common judgment affirms or denies the predicate either of all that is contained under the subject or of some of it. The former is a universal judgment (*iudicium universale*), the latter a particular judgment (*iudicium particulare*). A particular judgment either is at the same time universally true, and is then a judgment not merely particular (*iudicium non tantum particulare*), or it is not, and is then a merely particular judgment (*iudicium tantum particulare*). All these judgments either affirm or deny, §.294. Universal affirmative judgments are entitled A; universal negative, E; particular affirmative, I; particular negative, O. The predicate of every universal judgment is, in respect of the subject, a universal concept, since negative judgments can be transformed into affirmative ones, §.294. 262. And since in singular as well as in universal judgments it is judged that the predicate does or does not belong to the whole subject, singular judgments may be counted among the universal. In so far as a judgment is either singular or common, a quantity (*quantitas iudicii*) is ascribed to it.",[]],"meier:302":["A universal affirmative judgment is true if the predicate belongs to everything contained under the subject, §.301. 295. It is therefore false if the predicate belongs to nothing at all contained under the subject, or does not belong to some, or even to a single one, of the things so contained. A universal negative judgment is true if the predicate is opposed to everything contained under the subject, §.301. 295. It is therefore false if the predicate belongs to everything contained under the subject, or to some of it, or even to a single one of the things so contained.",[]],"meier:303":["1) If the sufficient condition of a common judgment is absolutely necessary in the subject, it is inseparable from the subject, and is found wherever the subject is found, and so in everything contained under it, §.263. The judgment is then universally true; for where the condition is, there also is the predicate, §.299. Hence, if a predicate is affirmed or denied of an abstract concept on account of its essence, or an essential part, or an attribute, or its definition, the judgment is universally true, §.298. 299. 2) If this condition is a determination, it becomes absolutely necessary as soon as it is combined with the subject, since all things to which this determination does not belong are thereby excluded from the subject, §.299. The determinate judgment is therefore universally true.",[]],"meier:304":["A judgment has either only one subject and one predicate, or several. The former is a simple judgment (*iudicium simplex*), the latter a composite judgment (*iudicium compositum*). When subject and predicate are composed of several concepts, either some of these are thought for the sake of the rest, or none is thought for the sake of another. In the first case the concepts for the sake of which the rest are thought are the principal subjects and principal predicates (*subiectum et praedicatum principale*), and the rest are the subordinate subjects and subordinate predicates (*subiectum et praedicatum minus principale*). In the second case the composite judgment is a copulative judgment (*iudicium copulativum*).",[]],"meier:305":["A judgment which affirms that a [further] judgment follows from the condition, without either the condition or that judgment being given out as true or false, is a conditional judgment (*iudicium hypotheticum, conditionale*). The condition of conditional judgments is entitled the first or antecedent (*prius, antecedens*); the judgment which follows from it, the last or consequent (*posterius, consequens*). Not all judgments which have a condition are therefore conditional, §.297.",[]],"meier:306":["The truth of conditional judgments does not require that the antecedent and the consequent be true, but that the consequence be valid, that is, that the antecedent be the sufficient ground of the truth of the consequent. In the opposite case the conditional judgment is false, §.305. 295.",[]],"meier:307":["A disjunctive judgment (*iudicium disiunctivum*) is a judgment which affirms that among several judgments one is true and the rest false, yet in such a way that it is not determined which is true and which false. The several judgments of which it is composed are entitled the members of the disjunction or opposition (*membra disiunctionis, disiunctiva*). For example: the soul is either simple or composite.",[]],"meier:308":["If a disjunctive judgment is to be true, the members of the disjunction must stand to one another in just the relation in which they are represented in it, §.295. Consequently, 1) not more than one member may be true. If all the members, or even only two, are true at the same time, the judgment is false. 2) One member must necessarily be true. If all the members are false, or one is true only contingently, the judgment is false. 3) No member may be omitted. For even if the omitted member were false, it would still not be evident from the disjunction that one of those enumerated is necessarily true, §.307.",[]],"meier:309":["The representation of the manner in which the predicate does or does not belong to the subject is the determination of the copula and of its negation (*modus formalis*). A judgment either has such a determination or has not. The former is an impure judgment (*iudicium modale, modificatum, complexum qua copulam*), the latter a pure judgment (*iudicium purum*). For example: this world exists necessarily; it does not exist necessarily. In judging the truth of impure judgments we must pay particular attention to the determination of the copula and of its negation.",[]],"meier:310":["A judgment which is composed, in a very concealed manner, of an affirmative and a negative judgment is entitled an exponible judgment (*iudicium exponibile*). If it is to be true, both judgments must be correct; to assure ourselves of this we must therefore analyse it. For example: God alone is absolutely immortal.",[]],"meier:311":["Judgments which at the same time represent the state of mind in respect of a certain judgment are judgments that are not logical (*iudicia non logica*). For example: Oh, how greatly the sinner deceives himself! Such judgments are highly practical; and in order that learned knowledge may not be merely learned, we must take care that not all learned judgments be simple or merely logical.",[]],"meier:312":["All learned judgments are either theoretical judgments [*Erwägungsurtheile*] (*iudicia theoretica*) or practical judgments [*Übungsurtheile*] (*iudicia practica*), §.217. The latter judge that something ought to be done or left undone, for example: we must observe the laws. The former do not, for example: virtue makes us happy.",[]],"meier:313":["All learned judgments are either demonstrable (*iudicia demonstrativa*) or indemonstrable (*iudicia indemonstrabilia*). The truth of the latter is evident from themselves as soon as we know them distinctly; the former cannot be certain without proof, §.192.",[]],"meier:314":["The predicate of an affirmative judgment is either the same as the subject or different from it. The former is an identical judgment [*leeres Urtheil*] (*iudicium identicum*), which is either wholly identical (*iudicium ex toto identicum*) or partly so (*iudicium ex parte identicum*). The predicate of the former is in no way different from the subject; the predicate of the latter is only a part of the subject. Since no concept is opposed to itself, we know the truth of all identical judgments as soon as we understand them, §.295; they are therefore indemonstrable, §.313. If a judgment is not identical, we must fear that the difference between subject and predicate may be so great that they do not belong to one another. Such judgments are consequently not indemonstrable; and apart from identical judgments there are therefore no indemonstrable judgments, §.313.",[]],"meier:315":["Indemonstrable judgments are either theoretical or practical. The former are axioms [*Grundurtheile*] (*axioma*), the latter postulates [*Heischeurtheile*] (*postulatum*). We must not take any demonstrable judgment for an indemonstrable one, §.313.",[]],"meier:316":["Whenever we affirm a concept of itself, or a part of it of the concept, we have an axiom, §.315. 314. Axioms can therefore be found from definitions, by affirming of the defined concept: 1) the whole definition, and in so far logical definitions are indemonstrable; 2) some of the marks of the definition; 3) its several marks singly, §.268.",[]],"meier:317":["Whenever I represent to myself a thing that can be regarded as an effect, and affirm of anyone who wishes to produce it that he must produce it or a part of it, I have a postulate, §.315. 314. If, therefore, the defined concept can be regarded as an effect, and I affirm of anyone who wishes to produce it that he must produce 1) the whole definition, or 2) some marks, or 3) single marks [of it], I find postulates from definitions, §.268.",[]],"meier:318":["In a demonstration from reason all the arguments must be completely certain, §.193. 204. They are therefore either demonstrable or not, §.313. In the first case they must in turn be proved. Consequently a proof does not become a demonstration until I arrive at arguments that are all indemonstrable. Identical judgments, axioms and postulates are therefore the first beginnings of all demonstrations from reason, §.314. 315. The understanding is completely satisfied only when the proof has been carried back to such judgments.",[]],"meier:319":["Demonstrable judgments are either certain merely through experience or not. The former are intuitive judgments (*iudicium intuitivum*), the latter discursive judgments [*Nachurtheile*] (*iudicium discursivum*). An intuitive judgment consists entirely of empirical concepts, and is an immediate experience, §.201, and a singular judgment, §.301. No intuitive judgment is indemonstrable, §.313. 314; for I must always recall a single case, and so recognise how, and whether, I have arrived at such a judgment without deception, §.202. All intuitive judgments are the first beginnings of all demonstrations from experience, §.202.",[]],"meier:320":["To find an intuitive judgment: 1) take the thing that is sensed as subject; 2) analyse the sensation, according to §.142. 257; 3) affirm the marks so discovered of the subject, §.319.",[]],"meier:321":["The predicates of intuitive judgments may be contingent qualities, alterations, relations, effects, causes, actions and passions; but never the essence, the essential parts, the attributes, or negative marks, §.256. No negative judgment is an intuitive judgment, although it may be derived from one, since the marks opposed to those we sense in the subject can truly be denied of it, §.295.",[]],"meier:322":["Universal judgments are derived from intuitive judgments [in two ways]. 1) We pass an intuitive judgment, according to §.320, on all things of a species, and then conclude that the predicate can be universally affirmed of the whole species, §.263. 2) If we wish to derive a universal judgment from a single intuitive judgment, then a) we seek the higher concept under which the subject falls, according to §.259; b) we seek the condition of the intuitive judgment; c) we examine whether this condition is absolutely necessary or contingent in the higher concept. In the latter case we combine it with the subject. In either case the predicate can then be universally affirmed of the higher concept, §.299.",[]],"meier:323":["Discursive judgments are demonstrated either from experience or from reason, §.319. The former are mediate experiences, §.203. 204. A judgment of experience is any judgment which is certain through experience, whether it be an intuitive judgment, §.319, or a discursive judgment.",[]],"meier:324":["Discursive judgments require either a shorter or a longer proof, §.319. The former are entitled corollaries [*Zusätze*] (*consectarium, corollarium*), and may be either theoretical or practical judgments, §.312. They are for the most part stated without proof, and are easily found by comparing a few definitions, indemonstrable judgments and other truths with one another.",[]],"meier:325":["Discursive judgments which require a longer proof (whether from experience or from reason) are either theoretical or practical judgments, §.312. The former are entitled theorems (*theorema*), the latter problems (*problema*).",[]],"meier:326":["To discover a theorem, 1) we must discover the judgment, unless it is already known; 2) we must discover a longer proof. Consequently, a) we must seek out all the arguments, whether they are already known to us, or are to be learned from others, or have first to be newly discovered; b) we must set them in distinct connection; and c) we must understand the art of composing a proof out of many arguments without causing confusion, §.325.",[]],"meier:327":["Whoever wishes to practise himself in the discovery of theorems must begin with the easier ones, and with such as can be tested by experience, so that he may the more easily recognise his missteps.",[]],"meier:328":["For the sake of distinctness a problem is analysed into three parts. 1) The question (*quaestio problematis*) is the representation of the action which is to be done or left undone. Whenever we represent a thing that can be regarded as an effect, a question can be raised. 2) The solution (*solutio problematis*) analyses the manner in which what the question asks for comes about. It must therefore state either all the actions of which [the thing asked for] consists, or all the causes, or both together; and to find it we must give a logical definition of the object of the question. 3) The proof of the problem (*demonstratio problematis*) demonstrates that by following the solution what the question asks for becomes actual. It therefore presupposes the solution as a condition, and the whole problem can be regarded as a conditional theorem, §.305. The test of the problem (*proba, examen problematis*) is that whereby we are convinced that we have followed the solution.",[]],"meier:329":["If no impossibility can be discovered in the question, we may venture upon the solution. It can be discovered either through experience, or from reason and by abstraction, or in an arbitrary manner. To find it through experience, 1) we must try to be present when the thing comes about, and form a distinct concept of the manner of its origin through experience, §.257; 2) we must closely observe all the causes, and everything that takes place in its coming about, so far as it falls within our senses; 3) the remaining causes, and the remaining parts of its origin which do not fall within our senses, we must try to divine by means of that part of learning within whose sphere the object falls; 4) we compare the effect whose manner of origin we cannot experience with another which we can analyse through experience, and conclude that the former comes about in a similar manner and through similar causes.",[]],"meier:330":["When we adopt an effect merely arbitrarily, 1) we must be well versed in the parts of learning to which it belongs, form a distinct concept of it, and call to mind all the powers and causes known to us, to see whether we come upon anything that belongs to the solution of the question. 2) By arbitrarily connecting many actions and causes with one another, and attending to what results, we also find many a solution to many a problem.",[]],"meier:331":["In the midst of a system we come unawares, in following out demonstrations from reason, upon many solutions. And if we have already found the solutions of the lower questions, we need only abstract from their differences in order to have the solution of the higher question, whether this concerns an abstract action or some other abstract effect.",[]],"meier:332":["For the sake of the distinctness of a problem we must find, from the distinct concepts of the effect and of the causes contained in the solution, what each cause contributes to the effect.",[]],"meier:333":["To preserve brevity in the problems of a system, 1) we must solve, in the earlier ones, the actions and effects of which the actions and effects of the later problems are composed; 2) we must solve, in the earlier ones, those actions and effects under which the questions of the later problems are contained.",[]],"meier:334":["A problem is 1) true (*veritas problematis*) if it contains nothing impossible, and consequently if the solution is not opposed to the question; 2) complete (*completum problema*) if, by following the solution, that which the question concerns results; 3) exact (*problema accuratum, adaequatum*) if it contains neither too little nor too much. These are the three perfections of problems.",[]],"meier:335":["A problem is false 1) if the solution is absolutely impossible; 2) if the solution is impossible in a certain respect, for example if it is not possible by human powers, or in certain circumstances; 3) if the solution can indeed become actual but contributes nothing to the question; 4) if it actually contradicts the question, §.334.",[]],"meier:336":["A problem is incomplete 1) if the end is not attained even by the most exact following of the solution; 2) if it is not known how the solution can be made actual; 3) if the solution cannot be carried out in all the cases where it is needed, §.334.",[]],"meier:337":["A problem is not exact if, though true and complete, it proceeds by roundabout ways and contains too much, §.334.",[]],"meier:338":["Occult qualities (*qualitates occultae*) are qualities of which we have no clear and distinct knowledge, and which we assume without sufficient ground. Since they conflict with the nature of learned knowledge, §.21. 168, whoever assumes them is either stupid or a deceiver. They must consequently be especially avoided when we investigate the grounds of things, of their nature and alterations, and so also in solving problems.",[]],"meier:339":["A lemma [*Lehnurtheil*] (*lemma*) is a demonstrable judgment which is assumed in a system without proof, because it belongs, together with its proof, to another system.",[]],"meier:340":["Scholia [*Anmerkungen*] (*scholia*) are judgments which in a system are neither arguments nor proved, but which have been mingled with the rest for the sake of further usefulness.",[]],"meier:341":["Equipollent judgments (*iudicia aequipollentia*) are different judgments in which the logical relation of one and the same subject and predicate is the same. For example: not all men are virtuous, some men are not virtuous; not no man is learned, some men are learned; everything has a ground, nothing is without a ground. Since the truth and falsity of a judgment are found in the logical relation, §.295. 296, 1) all the other equipollent judgments are true if one is true, and 2) all the others are false if one is false. The equipollence of judgments depends on such modifications of the judgment as do not alter its truth or falsity; otherwise they are not equipollent judgments.",[]],"meier:342":["Judgments which have the same subject, predicate and quality, and of which only one is universal, contain one another (*iudicia subalternata*). The universal judgment contains the others (*iudicium subalternans*), and the rest are contained in it (*iudicium subalternatum*). For example: all men can err, some men can err, one man can err; no man is sinless, some men are not sinless, one man is not sinless. 1) If the universal judgment is true, those contained in it are also true, but not conversely. 2) If the judgments contained in the universal are false, the universal is also false, but not conversely, §.302.",[]],"meier:343":["Judgments contradict one another (*iudicia contradictoria*) when one precisely denies what the other affirms. For example, the judgments A and O, and likewise E and I, contradict one another when they have one and the same subjects and predicates, §.301. 341. Now every concept either belongs or does not belong to every other concept, for it cannot be thought that both, or neither, could be the case. Of any pair of contradictory judgments, therefore, either the affirmative is true and the negative false, or the negative true and the affirmative false, §.295. Consequently we can infer 1) from the truth of one of two contradictory judgments the falsity of the other, and 2) from the falsity of the one the truth of the other.",[]],"meier:344":["When one judgment particularly affirms what the other particularly denies, they are opposed to one another in a particular manner (*iudicia subcontraria*): consequently the judgments I and O, when they have the same subjects and predicates. 1) These judgments can both be true. For since their predicate can, in respect of the subject, be a particular concept, §.262, it can truly be both particularly affirmed and particularly denied of it, §.295. 2) These judgments can never both be false. For if I and O are false, A and E are also false, §.342. Consequently A and O, and E and I, having the same predicates and subjects, could be false at the same time, which is impossible, §.343.",[]],"meier:345":["When one judgment universally affirms what the other universally denies, that is, when A and E have the same subject and predicate, they are opposed to one another in a universal manner (*iudicia contraria*). 1) These judgments can both be false. For their predicate can, in respect of the subject, be a particular concept, §.262, and consequently can be neither universally affirmed nor universally denied of it, §.302. 2) These judgments cannot both be true. For if A and E were true, I and O would also be true, §.342. Consequently A and O, and likewise E and I, although they had the same subject and predicate, could be true at the same time, and that is impossible, §.343.",[]],"meier:346":["The alteration whereby the subject of a judgment is made the predicate, and the predicate the subject, is entitled the conversion of a judgment (*conversio iudicii*). The judgment which undergoes the alteration is entitled the converted judgment (*iudicium conversum*), and that which arises from it the converting judgment (*iudicium convertens*). In conversion the quantity of the judgment is either altered or not. In the first case the conversion takes place *per accidens* (*conversio per accidens*), in the second simply (*conversio simplex*). In so far as a true judgment remains true after conversion, it can be converted (*iudicium converti potest, iudicium reciprocabile*).",[]],"meier:347":["All affirmative judgments whose subject and predicate are reciprocal concepts can be converted simply, whether they are universal or singular judgments. For the subject also belongs to everything contained under the predicate, §.262, and can consequently be universally affirmed of the predicate, §.302. 346. Examples are the judgments which affirm the definition of the defined concept, and the members of the division, taken together in a disjunction, of the divided concept, §.270. 287.",[]],"meier:348":["All true particular affirmative judgments can be converted simply. For the abstract concept of the subject belongs to some of the things contained under it, §.260, and can therefore be particularly affirmed of them, §.295. And since the predicate belongs to some of the things contained under the subject, §.295, it can, as their higher concept, §.260, be taken as subject. Consequently the former subject can be particularly affirmed of the former predicate, §.346.",[]],"meier:349":["A universal negative judgment can be converted 1) simply. Otherwise the subject would not be opposed to everything contained under the predicate, §.302, and would consequently belong to some of it, §.343. Consequently the predicate would also belong to some of the things contained under the subject, §.348, and the universal negative judgment would be false, §.302, which is absurd. 2) *Per accidens*. For since it can be converted simply, the particular judgment contained in the universal converting judgment is also true, §.342, and through this particular judgment it is converted *per accidens*, §.346.",[]],"meier:350":["All universal affirmative judgments can be converted *per accidens*. For if they are true, the particular affirmative judgments contained in them are also true, §.342. These can be converted simply, §.348, and by that very means the universal judgments are converted *per accidens*, §.346.",[]],"meier:351":["If in particular negative judgments the negation is attached to the predicate, they become particular affirmative judgments, §.294, and can then be converted simply, §.348.",[]],"meier:352":["A universal affirmative judgment is contraposed (*contrapositio*) when its predicate is transformed into a negative concept and the former subject is universally denied of it. All true universal affirmative judgments can be contraposed; that is, to whatever their predicate does not belong, their subject does not belong either. Otherwise the predicate would be opposed to some things to which the subject belongs, §.343. The predicate could then be truly denied of some of the things contained under the subject, §.295, and the universal affirmative judgment would be false, §.302, which is absurd. Of learned syllogisms.",[]],"meier:353":["When certain true judgments contain the sufficient ground of the truth of another, they are connected with one another, §.15; and in this relation of true judgments consists the connection of truths (*nexus veritatum*).",[]],"meier:354":["A syllogism (*ratiocinium*) is a distinct representation of the connection of truths. When it is perfect in a higher degree, it is entitled a learned or logical syllogism (*ratiocinium logicum, eruditum*).",[]],"meier:355":["In a syllogism we derive one truth from other truths, §.353, 354. Since these other truths are the arguments of the first, §.191, we construct a syllogism when we distinctly derive a truth from its arguments. Consequently the distinct representation of the consequence of a proof is a syllogism, §.191.",[]],"meier:356":["That judgment which in a syllogism is derived from others is the conclusion (*conclusio, probandum, principiatum*). Those judgments, on the other hand, from which the conclusion is derived are the premisses (*praemissae, data, sumtiones, principia*).",[]],"meier:357":["The subjects and predicates of the judgments of which a syllogism consists are entitled the terms of the syllogism (*termini*). The subject of the conclusion is the minor term (*terminus minor*); its predicate is the major term (*terminus maior*). The term which, besides the minor and the major, is met with in the premisses is entitled the middle term (*terminus medius*).",[]],"meier:358":["The premisses contain the ground of proof [*Beweisthum*] of the conclusion (§§ 355, 356). But they contain nothing beyond the parts of the conclusion except the middle term (§ 357). The middle term is therefore the ground of proof (§ 191), and consequently the condition of the conclusion (§ 297). The middle term is thus found by following the directions of §§ 297 and 298; and a syllogism consists in our deriving a judgment from its condition in a distinct manner (§ 355).",[]],"meier:359":["The matter of the syllogism (*ratiocinii materia*) consists in its premisses; its form (*ratiocinii forma*) in the following of the conclusion from the premisses.",[]],"meier:360":["A correct syllogism (*ratiocinium verum*) must be correct both in matter and in form (§§ 359, 355, 193). If, therefore, either the matter is false, or the form, or both at once, it is a false, incorrect syllogism (*ratiocinium falsum*). An erroneous syllogism (*ratiocinium erroneum*) is a false syllogism in so far as it is held to be a correct one (§ 109). From §§ 193 and 194 it is evident when a syllogism has demonstrative certainty and when it has not.",[]],"meier:361":["In every syllogism the consequence must be distinct (§ 355), and no distinctness is possible without order (§ 142). The judgments of a syllogism must therefore be duly ordered together. Now since no order is possible without rules, certain rules of inference are required, in accordance with which a syllogism must be arranged if it is to have a correct and distinct consequence.",[]],"meier:362":["It is impossible that something should at the same time be and not be. Or: if one and the same [predicate] is at the same time affirmed and denied of a thing, that thing is nothing. This proposition is entitled the principle of contradiction (*principium contradictionis*). It is the first internal criterion of truth (§ 95), and all the consequences of syllogisms must rest upon it if they are to be true (§ 361).",[]],"meier:363":["Whatever can with truth be universally affirmed or denied of a concept can also with truth be affirmed or denied of every other concept which falls under it (§ 342). Otherwise two contradictory judgments would have to be true at the same time (§ 343), and that is contrary to the principle of contradiction (§ 362). This proposition is entitled the inference from the universal to the particular (*dictum de omni et nullo*).",[]],"meier:364":["If the sufficient ground is true, its consequence is also true. Otherwise the sufficient ground would be no sufficient ground (§ 15), which is contrary to § 362. Consequently, if the consequence is false, the sufficient ground is also false, since otherwise the sufficient ground could be without consequence. This inference is entitled the inference from the sufficient ground to its consequence (*a ratione sufficiente ad rationatum valet consequentia*).",[]],"meier:365":["If one of two contradictory judgments is true, the other is false; and if the one is false, the other is true (§§ 343, 362). This proposition is entitled the inference from the opposite (*ab uno oppositorum ad alterum valet consequentia*).",[]],"meier:366":["If a judgment is true, then that judgment must also be true which has arisen from a logical alteration of the first, provided the alteration is one that can be made without prejudice to truth. Otherwise the logical alteration would have to be at once prejudicial and not prejudicial to truth, which is impossible (§ 362). This inference is entitled the inference from the logical alteration of a true judgment.",[]],"meier:367":["An ordinary syllogism (*ratiocinium ordinarium*) is a syllogism in which the inference is from the universal to the particular (§ 363). For example: All virtues contribute something to my happiness; now all philosophical virtues are virtues; therefore all philosophical virtues contribute something to my happiness. All other syllogisms are extraordinary syllogisms (*ratiocinium extraordinarium*).",[]],"meier:368":["All ordinary syllogisms have two premisses, which have the middle term in common (§ 367). That premiss which contains the major term is entitled the major premiss of the ordinary syllogism (*propositio maior ratiociniorum ordinariorum*); that which contains the minor term, its minor premiss (*propositio minor*).",[]],"meier:369":["That species of ordinary syllogisms in which the middle term stands in the subject of the major premiss and in the predicate of the minor premiss is entitled the first figure (*figura prima ratiociniorum*). For example: All men can err; the learned are men; therefore the learned can err. In this figure the minor premiss and the conclusion have one [and the same] subject (§ 368). Consequently these two judgments always have the same quantity (§ 301).",[]],"meier:370":["That species of ordinary syllogisms in which the middle term stands in the predicate of both premisses is entitled the second figure (*figura secunda*). For example: No absurd thing is true; everything that stands in the Bible is true; therefore nothing that stands in the Bible is absurd.",[]],"meier:371":["That species of ordinary syllogisms in which the middle term stands in the subject of both premisses is entitled the third figure (*figura tertia*). For example: All learned men have a cultivated understanding; some learned men are vicious; therefore some vicious men have a cultivated understanding.",[]],"meier:372":["That species of ordinary syllogisms in which the middle term stands in the predicate of the major premiss and in the subject of the minor premiss is entitled the fourth, or Galenic, figure (*figura quarta, galenica*). For example: No stupid man is learned; some learned men are pious; therefore some pious men are not stupid people.",[]],"meier:373":["The figures of syllogisms (*figurae ratiociniorum*) are accordingly different species of ordinary syllogisms, which arise from the different arrangement of the middle term in relation to the other terms in the premisses; and there are no more than four figures (§§ 369–372).",[]],"meier:374":["From the inference from the universal to the particular (§ 363) the following rules of all ordinary syllogisms follow. 1) An ordinary syllogism can contain neither more nor fewer than three terms. Such a syllogism contains only three judgments, and consequently only six places for terms (§§ 368, 357). Now it contains, first, a term of which something is universally affirmed or denied, and of which it is said in the other premiss that another concept belongs to it. This term therefore occurs twice, since it is the middle term (§ 368). Secondly, the minor term occurs in the conclusion and in the minor premiss (§§ 357, 368); and thirdly, the major term in the conclusion and in the major premiss (§§ 357, 368).",[]],"meier:375":["2) The middle term must not be put into the conclusion. For in ordinary syllogisms the major term is affirmed or denied of the minor in the conclusion, because, as the premisses state, the minor term belongs to the middle term, of which the major can be affirmed or denied (§ 364). Consequently the conclusion can consist only of the minor and the major term.",[]],"meier:376":["3) The premisses may not all be negative. For one of them must affirm that the minor or the major term is connected with the middle term (§ 363). In other words, from purely negative premisses nothing follows, although from purely affirmative premisses something does follow. If the middle term is a negative concept, at least one premiss is merely infinite (§ 294), and in that case it merely seems as if all the premisses were negative.",[]],"meier:377":["4) The premisses may not all be particular judgments; otherwise the inference would not be from the universal to the particular (§ 363). In other words, from purely particular premisses nothing follows, although from purely universal premisses something can follow.",[]],"meier:378":["5) If one premiss is negative, the conclusion must also be negative. For in that case the syllogism conforms to the negative part of the inference from the universal to the particular (§ 363), and must therefore have a negative conclusion.",[]],"meier:379":["6) If one premiss is a particular judgment, the conclusion must also be particular. For in that case the inference runs: because some things of a kind belong to the concept of which something is universally affirmed or denied, this [something] can also be affirmed or denied of these some (§ 363).",[]],"meier:380":["7) The conclusion always follows the weaker part of the syllogism; for the negative and the particular premisses are entitled the weaker part of the syllogism (*pars ratiocinii debilior*) (§§ 378, 379).",[]],"meier:381":["8) The conclusion must contain no less than the premisses. For otherwise the minor and the major term would contain less in the conclusion than in the premisses, and more than three terms would then be met with in the syllogism (§ 374).",[]],"meier:382":["9) The conclusion must contain no more than the premisses. Otherwise the minor and the major term would contain more in the conclusion than in the premisses, and there would then be more than three terms in the syllogism (§ 374).",[]],"meier:383":["Besides these rules, two further rules must be observed in the first figure. 1) In the first figure the minor premiss must always be affirmative. For since it has for its subject the subject of the conclusion (§ 369), it affirms of that subject that it belongs to the middle term (§ 363). If the middle term is negative, the minor premiss is infinite (§ 376), and is thus nevertheless an affirmative judgment (§ 294).",[]],"meier:384":["2) In the first figure the major premiss must always be universal. For if the conclusion is universal, the major premiss can only be universal (§ 379). If, on the other hand, the conclusion is a particular judgment, the minor premiss is likewise particular (§ 369), and so the major premiss must once again be universal (§ 377). Since singular judgments are to be reckoned among the universal (§ 301), they form no exception to the rules which require universality in the judgments of a syllogism.",[]],"meier:385":["The moods of ordinary syllogisms (*modi ratiociniorum ordinariorum*) are different kinds of syllogisms within one figure, which arise from the different quality and quantity of the judgments of a syllogism.",[]],"meier:386":["If in the first figure the conclusion is universal affirmative, then the minor premiss is also affirmative (§§ 383, 378) and universal (§§ 379, 369), and the major premiss must likewise be affirmative (§ 378) and universal (§§ 384, 379). The mood of syllogisms in the first figure whose conclusion is universal affirmative is entitled Barbara. For example: All truths are useful; all philosophical truths are truths; therefore all philosophical truths are useful.",[]],"meier:387":["If in the first figure the conclusion is universal negative, the minor premiss must be universal (§§ 369, 379) and affirmative (§ 383), and the major premiss universal (§§ 384, 379) and negative (§ 380). The mood of syllogisms in the first figure which have a universal negative conclusion is entitled Celarent. For example: No vice makes me more perfect; all pride is a vice; therefore no pride makes me more perfect.",[]],"meier:388":["If in the first figure the conclusion is particular affirmative, the minor premiss must be particular (§§ 369, 380) and affirmative (§§ 383, 378), and the major premiss universal (§§ 384, 377) and affirmative (§ 378). The mood of syllogisms in the first figure whose conclusion is particular affirmative is entitled Darii. For example: All persistent unbelievers are damned; some learned men are persistent unbelievers; therefore some learned men are damned.",[]],"meier:389":["If in the first figure the conclusion is particular negative, the minor premiss must be particular (§ 369) and affirmative (§ 383), and the major premiss universal (§§ 384, 377) and negative (§ 380). The mood of syllogisms in the first figure whose conclusion is particular negative is entitled Ferio. For example: No good action is punished by God; some merely natural works of men are good actions; therefore some merely natural works of men are not punished by God. In the first figure only four moods of syllogisms are possible (§§ 385–388).",[]],"meier:390":["All true judgments which can be conclusions in ordinary syllogisms are either [*e[n]tweder*] A or E, or I or O (§ 301). Consequently all these judgments can be concluded in the first figure (§§ 386–389), and for this reason it is entitled a perfect figure. Hence the remaining figures are not necessary.",[]],"meier:391":["If, contrary to the rules of inference so far given, syllogisms are constructed whose premisses and conclusions are correct, the latter do not follow necessarily from the former, but are true only contingently. Such syllogisms are therefore no objections against the correctness of these rules.",[]],"meier:392":["A hypothetical syllogism (*ratiocinium hypotheticum, conditionale, connexum*) is a syllogism which infers from the sufficient ground to the consequence (§ 364). Now in no kind of judgment is the consequence of a judgment affirmed from its condition except in hypothetical judgments (§ 305). These syllogisms therefore have a hypothetical premiss, which is entitled their major premiss. This premiss must have a correct consequence (§ 306); otherwise the inference is from the stick in the corner (*argumentum a baculo ad angulum*).",[]],"meier:393":["In a hypothetical syllogism the inference is either 1) from the correctness of the antecedent [*des erstern*] to the correctness of the consequent [*des letztern*] (§§ 364, 305). In that case the minor premiss affirms that the antecedent is true, and the conclusion that the consequent is true (*modus ratiociniorum hypotheticorum ponens*). For example: If there is a providence of God, all anxious cares are vain; now the antecedent is true; therefore the consequent also. Or 2) from the incorrectness of the consequent to the incorrectness of the antecedent (§§ 364, 305). In that case the minor premiss affirms the incorrectness of the consequent, and the conclusion the incorrectness of the antecedent (*modus ratiociniorum hypotheticorum tollens*). For example: If there is a blind fate, there are no free actions; now the consequent is false; therefore the antecedent also.",[]],"meier:394":["Since a thing can have several sufficient grounds, one cannot in hypothetical syllogisms always infer either from the incorrectness of the antecedent to the incorrectness of the consequent, or from the correctness of the consequent to the correctness of the antecedent (§ 305).",[]],"meier:395":["Disjunctive syllogisms (*ratiocinium disiunctivum*) are syllogisms which infer from one opposite to the other (§ 365). Consequently they contain a disjunctive premiss, which is entitled their major premiss (§ 307). If this premiss is contrary to one of the rules proved in § 308, it has no consequence, and the disjunctive syllogism is incorrect in form (§ 360).",[]],"meier:396":["In disjunctive syllogisms the inference is either 1) from the correctness of one member of the disjunction to the incorrectness of the rest (§§ 395, 365). In that case the minor premiss must give out one member as true, and the conclusion the rest as false (*modus ratiociniorum disiunctivorum ponendo tollens*). For example: Matter either can think or cannot; now the second is true; therefore the first is false. Or 2) from the incorrectness of all members but one to the correctness of this one (§§ 395, 365). In that case the minor premiss must affirm of all members but one that they are false, and the conclusion must affirm of this one that it is true (*modus ratiociniorum disiunctivorum tollendo ponens*). For example: Matter either can think or cannot; now the first is false; therefore the second is true.",[]],"meier:397":["A dilemma (*ratiocinium cornutum, crocodillinum, dilemma, trilemma etc.*) is a hypothetical syllogism whose consequent is a disjunctive judgment in which all the members are false. The hypothetical judgment whose consequent is disjunctive is the major premiss; the minor premiss affirms that the consequent is false in all its members; and the conclusion affirms that the antecedent is false. A dilemma must therefore conform at once to the rules of hypothetical and of disjunctive syllogisms (§§ 392, 393, 395). For example: If this world were not the best, then either no best world would be possible, or God would have had no knowledge of it, or he could not have created it, or he would not have willed to create it; now the consequent is false in all its members; therefore the antecedent also.",[]],"meier:398":["Syllogisms which infer from the logical alteration of a judgment to the judgment which has arisen through the alteration are entitled immediate inferences (*consequentia immediata*) (§ 366). For example: All men can err; therefore some men can also err. Or: There is a God; therefore it is false that there is no God.",[]],"meier:399":["A syllogism is so constituted that either its correct form is evident or that form is concealed. The former is a formal syllogism (*ratiocinium formale*); the latter a concealed syllogism (*ratiocinium crypticum*), which may therefore seem to be incorrect in form. To conceal logical art and to avoid pedantry, concealed syllogisms are to be recommended.",[]],"meier:400":["To concealed syllogisms belong chiefly truncated syllogisms (*enthymema*), in which a judgment is omitted, that is, is not thought as distinctly as the others, because it is entirely certain and so familiar to someone that it occurs to him at once. For example: All men can err; therefore I also can err.",[]],"meier:401":["To truncated syllogisms belong: 1) Contracted syllogisms (*ratiocinium contractum*), when one simply adds the middle term to the conclusion, yet in such a way that it does not become a hypothetical judgment. For example: This world is the best, because it was chosen by God. 2) Inductive inferences [*Zergliederungsschlüsse*] (*inductio*), which take as their ground, but omit, the following major premiss: what can be affirmed or denied of each of the lower concepts can be universally affirmed or denied of their higher concept (§ 263). If all the lower concepts are cited in the minor premiss, it is a complete induction (*inductio completa*). For example: The first person of the Godhead is God, the second also, the third also; therefore all persons of the Godhead are God. 3) Inferences from example (*exemplum in ratiociniis*), when what one affirms or denies of a lower concept on account of its higher concept is affirmed or denied of another lower concept which belongs to the same higher concept. For example: Men can sin; therefore all holy angels can also sin.",[]],"meier:402":["A syllogism which is incorrect in form (*paralogismus*) is entitled, when its fault is concealed, a fallacy [*Betrugschluss*] (*sophisma, fallacia, captio*).",[]],"meier:403":["A fallacy can arise: 1) When we combine in an incorrect manner things that are separate, and separate in an incorrect manner things that are combined (*sophisma sensus compositi et divisi*). For example: Where there are three thalers, there are two thalers; now three and two thalers make five thalers; therefore where there are three thalers, there are five thalers. 2) When a term is taken in a twofold sense (*sophisma figurae dictionis*). For example: A philosopher is a species of the learned; Leibniz is a philosopher; therefore Leibniz is a species of the learned.",[]],"meier:404":["3) When a term is taken once with a restriction and the other time without restriction (*fallacia accidentis, seu a dicto secundum quid ad dictum simpliciter, aut vice versa*). For example: He who says that you are an animal speaks the truth; now he who says that you are an ass says that you are an animal; therefore he speaks the truth. 4) When a wrong middle term is assumed (*fallacia medii*). For example: Whoever looks pale studies diligently; now Caius looks pale; therefore he studies diligently.",[]],"meier:405":["5) When the judgment which one has proved is taken for that which one ought to have proved (*sophisma heterozeteseos*). For example: when one is to prove the immortality of the soul, and proves its incorruptibility. 6) When the judgment which one has refuted is taken for that which one ought to have refuted (*sophisma ignorationis elenchi*). For example: when, against someone who maintains that the soul can die, one proves that it lives eternally. 7) When a composite judgment is regarded in a syllogism merely as a simple one (*sophisma polyzeteseos*). For example: Either it is true that dogs alone among all four-footed animals have reason, or it is not true; if the former, then dogs have reason; if the latter, then all four-footed animals have reason.",[]],"meier:406":["A syllogism consists either of several syllogisms or of one only. The latter is a simple syllogism (*ratiocinium simplex, probatio simplex*); the former a composite syllogism (*ratiocinium compositum*). If a syllogism is composite, the several syllogisms of which it consists either hang together or do not (§ 353). In the latter case it is a *ratiocinium copulatum*. For example: All spirits are immortal; God and all human souls are spirits; therefore they are immortal. In the former case the premiss of the one is the conclusion of the other, and it is entitled a series of connected syllogisms (*ratiocinatio polysyllogistica, probatio composita*). For example: What accords with the laws of nature makes me more perfect; virtue accords with the laws of nature; therefore virtue makes me more perfect. That which makes me more perfect I am obligated to; virtue makes me more perfect; therefore I am obligated to virtue.",[]],"meier:407":["In a series of connected syllogisms there occur not only syllogisms whose conclusions are premisses of others, but also syllogisms whose premisses are conclusions of others (§ 406). The former are entitled prosyllogisms (*prosyllogismus*), the latter episyllogisms (*episyllogismus*). And some judgments occur twice in such a series (§ 406).",[]],"meier:408":["To avoid all confusion in a long series of connected syllogisms: 1) Divide a long proof into many parts, proving the chief premisses separately, as separate theorems. 2) Do not connect purely ordinary and formal syllogisms with one another. 3) Prove either only the major premisses of the prosyllogisms or only their minor premisses. 4) Derive the conclusions from contracted syllogisms (§ 401) (*epicherema*). 5) Omit all judgments which occur twice (§ 407); and this is entitled a heaped syllogism (*sorites*). For example: Youth [*Jugend*; doubtless a misprint for *Tugend*, virtue] accords with the laws of nature; what accords with the laws of nature makes me more perfect; that which makes me more perfect I am obligated to; therefore I am obligated to virtue.",[]],"meier:409":["If the syllogisms of which the sorites consists are of the first figure, and the minor premisses of the episyllogisms are omitted, it is a common sorites (*sorites communis*). For example, the instance cited above. If, however, it consists entirely of hypothetical premisses [*Vorsätzen*], it is entitled a hypothetical sorites (*sorites hypotheticus*). For example: If man has a limited understanding, he has confused representations; if he has confused representations, he can err; if he can err, he can sin; therefore, if man has a limited understanding, he can sin.",[]],"meier:410":["If in a series of connected syllogisms one or more judgments are omitted, a leap in proof (*saltus in probando*) is committed. The omitted judgments are either certain to him who is to be convinced by the proof, and readily occur to him, or they are not. In the first case the leap is legitimate (*saltus legitimus*); in the second, illegitimate (*saltus illegitimus*).",[]],"meier:411":["If a conclusion is derived from premisses which are just as uncertain as the conclusion itself, the grounds of proof are begged (*petitio principii seu quaesiti*). If, however, a conclusion is assumed as its own premiss, this fault is entitled a circle in proof (*circulus in probando*).",[]],"meier:412":["A proof proves too little (*probatio minus probans*) when through it the whole conclusion does not become certain. If, on the other hand, there follows from a proof, besides the conclusion, something which is false or altogether too different from the conclusion, it proves too much (*probatio plus probans*).",[]],"meier:413":["Through syllogisms one can 1) attain the most distinct certainty possible to a man (§ 355); 2) most easily withstand errors; 3) discover new truths; and 4) discover all the grounds of proof that are needed to erect a learned system, etc. Of the method of learned knowledge. [The closing words appear to be the heading of the following section, run into the text.]",[]],"meier:414":["Method (*methodus*) is a more noticeable or greater order of thoughts. In so far as several thoughts are either together or follow upon one another in one and the same manner, there is order among them; and this order arises when different thoughts are co-ordinated with one another according to the same rules.",[]],"meier:415":["Method is in knowledge (§§ 414, 11). It is therefore either an order of common knowledge, namely common method (*methodus vulgaris*), or of rational knowledge (§§ 17, 18), namely rational method (*methodus rationalis*). To the former belongs aesthetic method (§ 19); to the latter learned method (*methodus erudita, logica, philosophica*), the method of learned knowledge (§ 21).",[]],"meier:416":["Learned method promotes 1) the distinctness of learned knowledge (§ 142, no. 4); 2) truth in a system, and thoroughness (§ 105); 3) the unity and thoroughgoing connection of a system; 4) the perfection of learned knowledge, for all order is a perfection; 5) the retention of truths by memory (§ 414).",[]],"meier:417":["The more thoughts are connected with one another, and the more numerous and important the common rules according to which this connection takes place, the greater and more perfect is the method (§ 414). Consequently the greatest perfection of learned knowledge requires that it be as methodical as possible (§ 416).",[]],"meier:418":["A method connects thoughts either according to a single common rule only or according to several. The former is a simple method (*methodus simplex*), the latter a composite method (*methodus composita*). The latter is more perfect than the former (§ 417).",[]],"meier:419":["The diversity of methods arises from the diversity of the rules according to which thoughts are co-ordinated with one another (§ 414); and the diversity of the rules springs from the diversity of the cognitive powers [*Erkenntnisskräfte*], and from the differing character of the knowledge which is to be thought according to a method.",[]],"meier:420":["Since learned knowledge is a distinct knowledge from grounds (§§ 21, 17), in learned method the thoughts must follow upon one another in such a way that their connection is thereby rendered distinctly certain (§§ 415, 419). This is the fundamental law of learned method.",[]],"meier:421":["The method of dogmatic truths differs from the method of historical truths (§§ 419, 104). In the former the thoughts must be proved from one another, either by a demonstration or by an insufficient proof (§ 191). If the former is the case, it is entitled a demonstrative or scientific method (*methodus demonstrativa, scientifica, apodictica*).",[]],"meier:422":["In the learned method of dogmatic truths only such thoughts are co-ordinated with one another as are either the grounds of the truth, distinctness and certainty of others, or their consequences, or both at once (§§ 421, 420, 15). The grounds are therefore either placed before the consequences or after them. The former is the synthetic method (*methodus synthetica*), the latter the analytic method (*methodus analytica*). Consequently learned method is either synthetic or analytic, whether it be demonstrative or not (§ 421).",[]],"meier:423":["The analytic method is very inconvenient if one wishes to treat or to learn an extensive system by it. It is, however, very well suited 1) for drawing up the outline of a system, in order afterwards to treat it by the synthetic method; 2) for discovering the grounds and premisses from the consequences and conclusions; 3) for frequently reviewing a system already learned; 4) for thinking through properly the shorter separate parts of a system (§ 422).",[]],"meier:424":["The synthetic method is always attended with many inconveniences if it is employed in those cases where the analytic has been recommended (§ 423). It has, however, great advantages if it is employed 1) for thinking through and treating, considered as a whole, a system already discovered; 2) for learning such a system; 3) for discovering the consequences and conclusions from the grounds and premisses (§ 422).",[]],"meier:425":["In the synthetic method the grounds must always be thought before the consequences (§ 422). Consequently 1) concepts which are marks of other concepts must be explained before those others (§ 115), and thus the higher before the lower (§ 261). 2) Indemonstrable truths must precede demonstrable ones (§§ 314–317). 3) Premisses must be thought before conclusions (§ 356), and thus prosyllogisms before episyllogisms (§ 407). 4) Those demonstrable truths from which others follow must precede these others. In the analytic method everything is the reverse (§ 422).",[]],"meier:426":["The synthetic method whose sole aim is to promote the mathematical certainty of knowledge (§ 161) is entitled the mathematical method (*methodus mathematica*). It must therefore not only conform with the utmost exactness to the rules of the synthetic method (§ 425); by virtue of it one also either omits everything that is not indispensably required for mathematical certainty, or puts it into remarks (§ 340). In very difficult demonstrations, especially for beginners, it is of great use. But if one accustoms oneself to it alone, one acquires a merely learned knowledge (§ 161); it is accordingly not to be advised (§ 40).",[]],"meier:427":["Art (*ars*) is a sum of rules which are thought according to an order. Artificial method (*methodus artificialis, scholae*) is method in so far as it is learned and practised through art. Natural method (*methodus naturalis*) is method in so far as it is not artificial, but follows from the nature of the knowledge and of him who thinks. Mixed method (*methodus mixta*) is natural method in so far as artificial method comes to its aid.",[]],"meier:428":["Among the artificial learned methods the best are: 1) The syllogistic method (*methodus syllogistica*), when all proofs are analysed into nothing but formal syllogisms and no leaps are committed. It does a beginner good service; otherwise it is too prolix, too tedious, and not distinct enough. 2) The tabular method (*methodus tabellaris*), when all the members of the divisions and the parts of the whole are so arranged together that it is evident to what higher concept and whole each thought belongs. It promotes a distinct concept of a whole system, and the memory. But it often falls into what is forced; the attention is overburdened; and thoroughness cannot properly be attained through it. 3) The method that proceeds by a partition of a whole system, when the judgments are made to follow upon one another according to the diversity of their subjects; whence chapters, sections, etc. arise. If it is combined with the synthetic method, it does not hinder thoroughness, and it yields the benefit of the tabular method.",[]],"meier:429":["If one wishes to treat a system, this must be done according to the synthetic method, and in some cases according to the analytic (§§ 423, 424), for these are suited to the nature of a system (§ 420). Whatever, then, of all the other artificial methods can be combined with them, whether to promote distinctness and certainty, or memory, or to increase order, should be joined with the synthetic and analytic method. Consequently affectation in method (*affectatio in methodo*) is a fault: 1) when the artificial method is worthless, contrary to nature, and entirely arbitrary; 2) when one binds oneself too strictly to a simple method and will never make an exception to it; 3) when one observes the rules of method all too exactly and anxiously.",[]],"meier:430":["Learned method must be natural and unaffected (§ 429). It must therefore accord with the nature of learned knowledge, and be observed without anxious effort. Consequently it must suit the cast of mind [*Kopfe*] of him who would think according to it. He, then, to whom it is given can make use 1) of the Socratic method (*methodus socratica*), by which the thoughts follow upon one another as they give rise to one another, as questions and answers, in the conversation of several persons; 2) of the Platonic method (*methodus platonica*), by which the thoughts follow upon one another as in a free discourse, so that all kinds of learned thoughts are intermingled, without one's making formal and evident syllogisms.",[]],"meier:431":["If we are to treat a system of doctrine [Lehrgebäude] by the synthetic method, then (1) we must lay down only one truth as its foundation, and derive everything else from it. The system thereby acquires unity and a thoroughgoing connection, and contains neither more nor less than is necessary. (2) We lay down a division as foundation, and separate the system into a number of sections; this must be done, however, in such a way that no exception to the synthetic method is thereby made. (3) The learned thoughts themselves must follow upon one another in the following order: (a) first come the definitions; (b) then follow either the indemonstrable truths or the intuitive judgments; (c) then the demonstrable truths. (d) The corollaries and remarks are interspersed wherever they are necessary and useful. §§ 420, 425.",[]],"meier:432":["The method of historical truth (§ 421) must conform to the nature of historical truths (§ 419). We must therefore think them in the order in which their objects are connected with one another, either in space or in time. To this, then, belongs the chronological method (*methodus chronologica*), in which we think events in the very sequence in which they actually followed upon one another; and the geographical method (*methodus geographica*), by which we think, according to the chronological method, the events that take place, or have taken place, in one location. Now since the actual events of the world are also connected with one another, and a human being can often have insight into this connection, we can, at least often, employ the rational method also in the case of historical truths. § 415.",[]],"meier:433":["The best learned composite method may seem to be disorder to someone who does not know its rules, or who has accustomed himself to another method, or only to a simple one. But no well-grounded reproach can on that account be made against such a method.",[]],"meier:434":["A doctrine, a body of teaching (*doctrina*), is a sum of dogmatic truths that have one and the same object. A discipline (*disciplina*) is a doctrine in so far as it is known methodically. A demonstrated discipline is a science (*scientia obiective spectata*). Learned knowledge is always at the outset a doctrine; it is then given the form of a discipline, and finally the form of a science, and it has then attained its greatest perfection.",[]],"meier:435":["Whoever thinks, thinks either according to a method or according to none (§ 414). The former thinks methodically (*methodice cogitare, methodicum, acroamaticum, disciplinale in cognitione*); the latter, tumultuarily (*tumultuaria cognitio*). No tumultuary knowledge can be properly learned. § 416.",[]],"meier:436":["Meditation (*meditatio*) is that occupation of our cognitive powers through which we reflect upon a thing according to the rules of a method. If it takes place according to the rules of the learned method, it is learned meditation (*meditatio erudita*), which is of as many kinds as there are different kinds of learned method. Without this learned meditation we cannot attain any learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:437":["Whoever would meditate in a learned manner must (1) choose an object to which he directs his attention; (2) he must think, one after another, the marks of this object which constitute its definition, its indemonstrable predicates and, finally, its demonstrable predicates. These [marks] we have either already learned, and in that case we do well to write while meditating; or we wish to learn them, and in that case we must read in a book or listen to oral exposition; or we wish first to discover them. (3) We must investigate whether what we are thinking is a concept, a judgment, a theorem, etc., and consider it until we have satisfied all the rules which logic prescribes for each kind of learned thought.",[]],"meier:438":["It is a disgrace when, out of pedantry, we despise all ways of thinking that are not cut to the last of the method with which we have fallen in love, and when, like a charlatan, we make too much fuss about the method we love. Of learned exposition. Of the use of words.",[]],"meier:439":["The logical art of signification (*logica characteristica heuristica*) is the science of the rules which must be observed if we are to signify learned knowledge in a skilful manner. The logical art of interpretation (*hermeneutica logica*) is the science of the rules according to which we are to know, in a learned manner, the things signified from their signs.",[]],"meier:440":["A sign (*signum, symbolum*) is a means through the use of which the actuality of another thing can be known; this other thing is entitled the thing signified or the meaning (*signatum, significatus*). A term (*terminus*) is a sign of knowledge; the terms which ordinarily consist in a human voice are entitled words (*vocabulum*). The knowledge signified by terms and words is entitled their meaning (*significatus vocabuli et termini*).",[]],"meier:441":["The meaning which he who uses a word intends to signify by it is the true meaning (*significatus hermeneutice verus*). Every other meaning is the false meaning (*significatus hermeneutice falsus*). The true meaning may be a false concept, and the false meaning a true concept.",[]],"meier:442":["Discourse (*oratio*) is a series of words signifying representations that are connected with one another. The series of representations is entitled the sense of the discourse (*sensus orationis*). This is either a true sense (*sensus hermeneutice verus*), if the speaker intended to signify it, or a false sense (*sensus hermeneutice falsus*), if it is not the true one. Exposition [Vortrag] (*propositio*) is the producing of knowledge in others by means of discourse, whether the exposition be oral or written.",[]],"meier:443":["There are as many kinds of discourse and of exposition as there are kinds of knowledge (§ 442). There are thus common discourses, aesthetic discourses, rational discourses, and likewise corresponding kinds of exposition (§§ 17, 18, 19). A learned discourse (*oratio erudita*) is a discourse whose sense is learned knowledge (§ 21); and we expound something in a learned manner (*erudite proponere*) when, through a learned discourse, we produce learned knowledge in others.",[]],"meier:444":["Learned discourse, together with all its parts, must be so constituted that the highest perfection of learned knowledge is not hindered but furthered and signified (§ 442). In such discourse we must therefore (1) observe all the aesthetic rules of discourse which are not contrary to the logical perfection of knowledge (§ 34); (2) avoid all aesthetic perfections which are contrary to the logical perfection required of the discourse. § 34.",[]],"meier:445":["A discourse is either merely learned (*oratio mere erudita*), when it signifies nothing more than merely learned knowledge; or, in the opposite case, it is a discourse that is not merely learned (*oratio non mere erudita*). Learned exposition is distinguished in just the same way (§ 443). The latter are the best. §§ 40, 32.",[]],"meier:446":["The learned term [Ausdruck] must be copious (§§ 25, 444). Consequently (1) a learned man must know as many terms as are required to signify all his learned thoughts. (2) We must know all the necessary technical terms of our principal science, and in case of need it is permissible to invent new terms. A technical term (*terminus technicus*) is a term which may not be used at all outside an art or discipline. (3) We must know all terms of equal meaning (*synonyma*), that is, all terms that mean the same. (4) The learned term must signify everything that we think, and signify neither more nor less than we mean to say. (5) We must know all the meanings which a term has already acquired through the common and the learned usage of speech. Common usage (*usus loquendi*) is the agreement in common life by which people who speak a language connect one or more meanings with certain words. Learned usage (*receptus terminorum significatus*) is just such an agreement among those who apply themselves to a discipline.",[]],"meier:447":["The learned term must be sufficiently great, weighty and seemly for learned knowledge (§§ 444, 26). In learned exposition we must therefore avoid all base, ridiculous and vulgar terms, which are in use only among the rabble.",[]],"meier:448":["The learned term must be correct (§§ 444, 27). A true term (*terminus verus*) must correctly signify its true meaning (§ 441); a term that is not true is a false term (*terminus falsus*).",[]],"meier:449":["A term is false (1) when its meaning is a false concept and he who uses the term is convinced of the incorrectness of the meaning; (2) when we believe that we understand a term, although we do not in fact understand it. Such a false term is an empty term, or an empty word (*terminus inanis*). We understand a term (*terminum intelligere*) when we know its meaning from it. An empty word arises (a) when we take the concept of the word itself for its meaning (§ 440); (b) when the meaning of the word is an erroneous or deceptive concept (*conceptus deceptor*), that is, a concept which we believe we have, although we have none. (3) [A term is false] when it does not correctly signify a true concept. § 448.",[]],"meier:450":["A familiar term (*terminus familiaris*) is a term to which we have become accustomed, or which we believe we understand, although we do not understand it, because we pay no attention to its meaning. Such terms may be true terms; but in using them we are in the greatest danger of hatching empty terms, when we combine a couple of familiar terms whose meanings are impossible or contradict each other (§ 449). That is how people can talk about nothing and yet seem to understand one another, namely, when they use terms that are familiar to them.",[]],"meier:451":["Although in learned discourse we cannot avoid false terms of the first kind (§ 449) when we have to refute errors, we must nevertheless avoid all other false and empty terms, since thoughtlessness and errors arise from their use. To this end we must use no term (1) whose meaning we have not come to know, or (2) whose meaning we have not examined as to whether it is a true or a false concept.",[]],"meier:452":["A crude term (*terminus crassus*) is a term whose meaning is crude knowledge (§ 102). The use of empty terms in learned discourse is the word-peddling of the learned, in which words are sold for things. For example, when a learned man is to give the ground of a thing, and he has signified the thing by a term which he then adduces as the ground, he is selling words for things. All this must be avoided. § 451.",[]],"meier:453":["The learned term must be clear (§§ 444, 28). We must therefore not only be able to distinguish such terms [sie] properly from other terms, but their meaning must also be sufficiently clear. An obscure term (*terminus obscurus*) has an obscure meaning, a clear term (*terminus clarus*) a clear one, etc. An obscure term is not on that account empty (§ 449); and a term that is obscure to me is not on that account obscure absolutely and to other people. § 126.",[]],"meier:454":["From a term its meaning can either be clearly known or not. The former is an intelligible term (*terminus intelligibilis*), the latter an unintelligible term (*terminus non intelligibilis*). Now although in a learned discourse we cannot use only terms that are intelligible to everyone, we must nevertheless use only such terms as can be intelligible even to people of middling understanding and middling command of the language, provided they pay even middling attention to the discourse. § 453.",[]],"meier:455":["Whoever would employ the most intelligible terms in a learned discourse (§ 454) (1) must prefer the better known and more usual terms to the less known and less usual ones, when they are otherwise equal. (2) He must always connect with a term the meaning which usage, both in common life and in the disciplines, connects with it (§ 446), until necessity compels him to do otherwise. (3) Where usage is manifold, he must connect with terms their most usual meanings (*significatus famosissimus*), that is, those which most people connect with the term in most cases. (4) If in a particular case the three preceding rules do not suffice, we must define the meaning in which we take a term. § 268.",[]],"meier:456":["The learned term must be certain (§§ 444, 29). A certain term (*terminus certus*) is so constituted that we know with certainty not only that it has a meaning, but also that it has just this meaning and no other. A term is uncertain (*terminus incertus*) when it is not certain; and through the use of uncertain terms discourse is made equivocal [auf Schrauben gesetzt].",[]],"meier:457":["An ambiguous term (*terminus ambiguus, homonymus, vagus*) does not always have one meaning; as soon as it acquires one, its meaning is fixed (*terminus fixus*). The ambiguity of a term hinders its certainty (§ 456); we must therefore guard against all ambiguity. And if usage does not suffice for this (§ 446), we must fix the meanings by logical definitions.",[]],"meier:458":["The learned term must please to such a degree that we may thereby be stimulated to know from it the knowledge that lies concealed under it (§§ 444, 30). Consequently it must be adorned with all the perfections that are not contrary to learned knowledge, so that it may be read or heard willingly and with pleasure.",[]],"meier:459":["In the use of words in logical definitions four further rules are to be observed. (1) In definitions too we must observe usage, so long as this is possible (§ 455). (2) We must not put into the discourse which signifies the definition either the term for the concept defined or its equivalent terms; for the definition would then be no more distinct than the concept defined (§§ 275, 446). (3) A definition must be expressed in as few words as can be done without detriment to distinctness, so that the attention is not dispersed by too many words.",[]],"meier:460":["The meaning for the sake of which a word was invented and is used, even if it should otherwise have no meaning at all, is entitled the proper meaning (*significatus proprius*); the other meanings are improper (*significatus improprius*). If we use a word to signify the former, it is a proper word (*terminus proprius*); if we use it to signify the latter, it is an improper word (*terminus improprius*). With an improper word we always think the proper meaning in addition to the improper one, although we could have a complete concept of each without thinking of the other. Hence (4) in the expression of logical definitions we must guard against all improper terms, since otherwise the definition would be too copious (§ 270). And if at times we can find no proper terms in the language, we must first turn an improper word into a proper one, by defining its improper meaning, before we use it in another definition.",[]],"meier:461":["When we are to define the meaning of a word that has several meanings, we may define whichever of its several meanings pleases us, provided the several meanings are equally usual. And in this respect definitions are often arbitrary.",[]],"meier:462":["A judgment signified by terms is entitled a proposition (*propositio, enunciatio*). A proposition in which not all the concepts are signified by special terms is entitled a cryptic proposition (*propositio cryptica*). The terms that signify the quantity of the judgment are entitled the signs of quantity. In a proposition the signs of quantity are either present or not (*propositio definita, et indefinita*). The latter are, by the usage of speech, understood as universal; and whoever, therefore, would not be wrongly understood must not omit the signs of quantity from those propositions which he does not wish to be understood as universal. A proposition is understood when all the terms contained in it are understood. § 449.",[]],"meier:463":["An inference of reason, when it is signified by terms, is entitled a syllogism [Schlussrede] (*syllogismus*). We should take care not always to present syllogisms in one order and with the same technical terms. And when we present syllogisms in cryptic or truncated form, we should take our guidance from the hearer, so that he is not confused and can call to mind all the omitted propositions. Those fallacies which rest merely on the ambiguity of terms (*sophisma ambiguitatis, amphiboliae*) are scarcely worth the trouble of mentioning. Of learned style.",[]],"meier:464":["Learned style (*stilus eruditus, philosophicus*) is the agreement or similarity of the manner in which one speaks in a learned way.",[]],"meier:465":["Since learned style is a part of learned discourse (§§ 464, 443), it has the same aim as that discourse, namely, to further learned knowledge in oneself and in others. A good learned style (*stilus eruditus perfectus*) must therefore further learned knowledge and its perfections; a style that hinders them is a bad learned style (*stilus eruditus imperfectus*).",[]],"meier:466":["To the perfections of learned style belong (1) all those of its properties without which the perfections of learned knowledge cannot be attained; (2) all other beauties of style, if they are not contrary to the perfection of learned knowledge. Without these the style would be merely learned. To the imperfections of learned style belong (1) all those of its properties by which the perfection of learned knowledge is hindered, even should they be beauties; (2) all properties by which imperfections are caused in learned knowledge; (3) all imperfections which can be tolerated in no style whatsoever. § 465.",[]],"meier:467":["Learned style must be so ordered that through it the distinctness of the knowledge of truth from grounds is furthered in the best possible way (§ 465). This is the fundamental law of learned style.",[]],"meier:468":["(1) Learned style must be distinct (§ 467). The distinctness of learned style (*perspicuitas stili eruditi*) is that perfection of it by which it does not hinder, but furthers, the distinctness of the knowledge signified. The opposite imperfection is its obscurity (*obscuritas stili eruditi*). To make style distinct, (1) we must use only clear terms (§§ 453–455); (2) we must speak not too briefly, but with an abundance of words; (3) we must so connect the parts of the discourse that the connection of the thoughts can be recognised from it most distinctly and easily. Consequently (a) we must avoid all rare and difficult constructions; (b) we must not misplace [verwerfen] the order of the construction; (c) we must not too frequently omit the terms which signify the connections of thoughts; (d) we must not make too many insertions; (e) we must not make our sentences [Punkte] too long. A proposition which is not part of another proposition is entitled a sentence [Punkt] (*punctum*). We must therefore intermingle shorter and longer sentences.",[]],"meier:469":["(2) Learned style must be pure, so far as is possible (§ 467). The purity of learned style (*puritas stili eruditi*) consists in its agreement with the improved nature of the language. The opposite imperfection is its impurity (*impuritas stili eruditi*). Since a pure style pleases, it stimulates the attention of the reader and hearer, and thus furthers the distinctness of learned knowledge. § 142.",[]],"meier:470":["Should the purity of style hinder its distinctness, an exception must be made to the former (§§ 35, 21). Such necessary impurity is only an apparent imperfection of learned style. § 466.",[]],"meier:471":["For the sake of the purity of learned style we must avoid (1) all hybrid words (*vox hybrida*), whose parts are taken from different languages; (2) all grammatical errors; (3) all barbarous terms; (4) all terms from foreign languages, and foreign constructions, unless they have already acquired the rights of citizenship; (5) all obsolete terms and constructions; (6) all new words and technical terms; (7) all multiplicity of terms, where we can instead employ a single term, even though it is impure (§ 469). For the reason given in § 470, exceptions may be made to all these rules.",[]],"meier:472":["With regard to the purity of learned style we must avoid (1) the careless and inattentive barbarism of pedantic scholars, by which they wholly neglect the purity of style; (2) an excessive love of purity of style, when we seek to preserve purity in a thoroughly forced manner, even to the detriment of the perfection of learned knowledge. § 466.",[]],"meier:473":["(3) Learned style must be fitting [füglich], so far as is possible. The fittingness of style (*concinnitas stili*) consists in the proportioned agreement of the parts of a discourse, as, for example, when we construct periods. In a merely learned discourse this perfection cannot be attained; but in a learned discourse that is not merely learned we must, in those passages where we are at the same time thinking beautifully, take the utmost care to secure this perfection also (§§ 467, 445), since it makes the expression more agreeable. § 458.",[]],"meier:474":["(4) Learned style must be elegant, so far as is possible (§ 467). The elegance of style (*ornatus stili eruditi*) consists in using, in place of certain terms, others by which the same concepts are signified, but in a more beautiful manner. In those parts where the discourse is merely learned, no elegant style is possible; but when the learned discourse must at the same time be beautiful, the learned style must also be elegant. § 445.",[]],"meier:475":["(5) Learned style must be euphonious, so far as is possible (§ 467). The euphony of style (*sonoritas stili*) consists in that perfection by which it pleases the ear (§ 458). This perfection must be sought especially in those passages of a learned discourse where the discourse is not merely learned. § 445.",[]],"meier:476":["Nothing but simple propositions, and excessively long sentences, render the style obscure (§ 468). In a learned discourse, therefore, for the sake also of the fittingness and euphony of the style (§§ 473, 475), simple and compound sentences must alternate with moderately beautiful periods, according as the thoughts signified are merely learned or at the same time more or less beautiful.",[]],"meier:477":["(6) Learned style must be appropriate, so far as is possible (§ 467). The appropriateness of style (*congruentia stili*) is that perfection by which the style befits, and is in conformity with, the knowledge signified, the person speaking and the hearers. Consequently the learned style must be sufficiently weighty for learned knowledge (§ 447), and thus not ridiculous, vulgar or base. And it must be natural to him who employs it, and thus not forced.",[]],"meier:478":["For the sake of the appropriateness of learned style, it must (1) in those passages where merely learned thoughts are expressed, not be so pure, fitting, elegant and euphonious as (2) in those passages where thoughts are expressed which are at the same time beautiful. § 477. Of a learned discourse.",[]],"meier:479":["Learned exposition is either oral or written, that is, a book or a writing (*liber, scriptum*). Whoever gives a learned exposition is a teacher; and whoever acquires his learned knowledge from the exposition of another learns it, and is a pupil, whether he be a reader or a hearer.",[]],"meier:480":["The sense of a learned discourse must be learned knowledge which conforms to all the rules of its perfection (§§ 10–438), and the expression must be so constituted as we have hitherto shown (§§ 439–478). In a learned discourse we must therefore expound all the parts of learned knowledge, and indeed in the order in which they must follow upon one another if learned knowledge is to arise.",[]],"meier:481":["A learned discourse must be copious in a complete manner (§§ 480, 41–65). It must therefore be neither too brief (*oratio erudita nimis brevis*), when it says less of the object than suffices for the most perfect learned knowledge, nor excessively prolix (*oratio erudita nimis prolixa*), when it says more than is necessary for the attainment of perfect learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:482":["A learned discourse may be too prolix with respect to one person, too brief with respect to another, and complete with respect to a third (§ 481). Consequently, the less capacity a pupil possesses, the less he has already learned, and the less he applies himself to an object, the briefer must the learned exposition be for him. A pupil of the opposite kind must be instructed through a more copious exposition.",[]],"meier:483":["A learned discourse must be sufficiently weighty (§§ 480, 66–91, 447, 477). Its object must therefore not be a worthless trifle.",[]],"meier:484":["A learned discourse must be as correct as possible (§§ 480, 92–114, 448–452). A learned man who himself errs, and on top of that spreads his errors, is a seducer of other people, and doubly imperfect. We must therefore first examine our own knowledge with the utmost care before we expound it.",[]],"meier:485":["A learned discourse must be distinct and intelligible (§§ 480, 114–154, 468). It is therefore a fault (1) when the teacher speaks without understanding, or when his discourse has no sense at all; (2) when his words and constructions are obscure and too difficult; (3) when he himself cannot be understood. A speaker is understood (*proponens intelligitur*) when we connect with his words the very series of representations which he intended to signify by them. We can therefore often understand the discourse without understanding the speaker (§ 449). A teacher must therefore either himself define the meanings in which he takes his words, or connect with them no concept other than those which he knows the pupil already has and connects with the words.",[]],"meier:486":["When a teacher expounds confused knowledge, his pupil must either already possess it, the teacher merely recalling it to his memory through the exposition, or the teacher must impart it to him in some other way than through exposition. Distinct knowledge can be produced in others only through exposition (§ 14). A teacher must therefore possess very distinct knowledge.",[]],"meier:487":["Learned exposition becomes distinct (1) through a distinct style (§ 468); (2) through the observance of a suitable method (§ 416); (3) when abstract knowledge is illustrated by beautiful examples; (4) when a thing, if its importance warrants it, is expounded in a varied and manifold way, and aesthetic illustrative arguments are employed. (5) What is agreeable and lively in exposition furthers attention, and thus also distinctness.",[]],"meier:488":["A learned discourse must be convincing and thorough (§§ 480, 155–215). It is therefore a fault (1) when a teacher offers no proofs at all, in a case where he is expounding demonstrable knowledge; (2) when his proofs are not certain in the degree, and with the kind of certainty, that the knowledge to be expounded demands; (3) when he throws dust in his pupils' eyes (*fucus logicus*), that is, when instead of conviction he produces in them mere persuasion. § 184.",[]],"meier:489":["A learned discourse must be practical (§§ 480, 216–248). Everything that we have hitherto remarked of learned discourse must also be applied to learned exposition. § 443.",[]],"meier:490":["The gift of learned exposition (*donum didacticum*) is the sum of all those skills of soul and body without which no perfect learned exposition is possible. Without this gift no one can be a teacher. § 479.",[]],"meier:491":["A teacher must, in his learned exposition, adapt himself to his pupils (§ 479). Consequently there are to be commended to him (1) condescension (*condescendentia*), when he has before him pupils of lesser capacity and so orders his exposition that it is not too high for them; (2) elevation (*coadscendentia*) to the greater capacity of his pupils, so that the exposition is not too poor for them.",[]],"meier:492":["A pupil of learned knowledge (1) must by nature be disposed to learned knowledge; (2) he must already have exercised his understanding; (3) he must often already possess a store of learned knowledge; (4) he must apply the necessary diligence to the learning of learned knowledge. A learned man must therefore expound learned knowledge to no one who is not an apt pupil of it. And he must not become angry when he sees that many people learn nothing from his exposition, unless he turns his anger against himself, seeing that he has spent his pains on the wrong persons.",[]],"meier:493":["In a learned exposition we either have principally the aim of refuting someone and his error, in which case it is a refutation of another (*refutatio alterius*), or we do not. In the latter case we regard the reader or hearer as a pupil who is ignorant, not as one who errs, and we therefore seek to produce perfect learned knowledge in him; such an exposition is entitled instruction (*docere*).",[]],"meier:494":["In learned instruction the following rules must be observed. (1) We must begin with the definition or description of all the concepts that occur in the object, unless they are already sufficiently clear to the pupil; and we must convince him of the correctness of these concepts according to the rules of §§ 258, 265, 267 and 278.",[]],"meier:495":["(2) In instruction concerning an indemonstrable proposition, nothing more is needed than to analyse it and, where this can be done, to illustrate it by an example. §§ 313–318, 300, 487.",[]],"meier:496":["(3) In instruction concerning an intuitive judgment, we must either recall to the pupil's memory all the sensations of which this judgment consists, or impart them to him in the course of the instruction (§§ 319–321, 202), or convince him by probable testimony that others have had these sensations. §§ 206–215.",[]],"meier:497":["(4) In instruction concerning demonstrable propositions, (a) we must analyse the proposition, and the solution of a problem, so that they are rightly understood; (b) we must present the proof according to the analytic method and, if it is long, bring it into one or a couple of formal syllogisms (§§ 423, 203, 204, 206–215); (c) we must carry the whole proof on until we arrive either at indemonstrable and intuitive judgments, or at such demonstrable judgments as the other person is already sufficiently convinced of. Hence arises the citation of sections in writings.",[]],"meier:498":["(5) We must seal the conviction of the truth by a practical representation of its practical character; for what the heart loves, the understanding believes. §§ 216–248.",[]],"meier:499":["A correct proof that an error is an error is a true refutation (*refutatio vera*); but an incorrect proof, either that an error is an error or that a truth is an error, is an apparent refutation (*impugnatio*). Only errors can be correctly refuted. He who undertakes the refutation is the attacking party (*adversarius opponens*), and he who refutes the refutation is the defending party (*adversarius defendens*). The defence is therefore either correct or false. A learned controversy (*controversia*) consists of the refutation and defence of an opinion.",[]],"meier:500":["The proposition which the attacking party refutes and the party attacked defends is entitled the disputed proposition (*thesis controversa*); and the proposition of the attacking party, which cannot be true at the same time as the disputed proposition, is entitled the counter-proposition (*antithesis*). It is therefore opposed to the disputed proposition either contradictorily (§ 343) or in a general way (§ 345). The question at issue is determined (*status controversiae, seu quaestionis formatur*) when the attacking party shows distinctly which is the disputed proposition and which the counter-proposition.",[]],"meier:501":["When a question at issue is not correctly determined, the opponents either carry on no controversy at all, or they fall into a verbal dispute (*logomachia*), that is, a controversy which arises because they do not understand one another (§ 500); and that is always a foolish and futile affair. Distinct from this, however, are controversies about words (*controversia philologica*), which are genuine controversies, but whose object is words or other terms.",[]],"meier:502":["Learned controversies must be conducted according to the following rules. 1) The attacking party should look for an error in his opponent's proof (§ 194), or, more generally, for some departure of his opponent's thoughts from the rules of logic. He has not thereby refuted the matter itself, but he has refuted his opponent's way of thinking. If such an attack is sound, no defence against it is possible. Otherwise one must show that one has not violated the rules of logic whose transgression has been laid to one's charge.",[]],"meier:503":["2) The attacking party may demonstrate the opposite [of the disputed proposition]. It then follows that the disputed proposition is false, and that the opponent is in error (§ 500). Against this attack one can defend oneself only if the opponent has committed an error in his demonstration, and this error must be pointed out according to the first rule (§ 502). If one party can show the other that it accepts without proof propositions which admit of proof, this objection remains unanswered until the proof has been given.",[]],"meier:504":["3) The attacking party may refute the disputed proposition by an apagogic proof (§ 196). Here belongs also the case in which one shows that the disputed proposition contradicts another opinion held by the opponent, even though we ourselves do not accept that opinion (*argumentatio ad hominem*). Against such an attack one can defend oneself only if there is an error in the apagogic proof, and this error must be pointed out to the opponent (§ 502).",[]],"meier:505":["4) If one of the opponents abandons the question in dispute, and brings forward matters by which the contested proposition is not refuted and its opposite is not proved, one must not enter upon such matters. One should rather concede them for the purposes of the present controversy, lest it become altogether too prolix (§ 481). In this case the other party can defend itself only if the first has conceded something over-hastily; it must then show that what has been conceded does in fact bear upon the question in dispute.",[]],"meier:506":["5) The attacking party may, by a restriction (§ 299) which he adds to the contested proposition, show that it is not so universally true as his opponent represents it to be. Against this attack we can defend ourselves only by showing either that this restriction has no bearing upon the question now in dispute, or that it is false; or else by proving soundly that our proposition is true without restriction.",[]],"meier:507":["6) The attacking party may bring a distinction (*distinctio*) against his opponent; that is, he may show the difference between things which the opponent has confused, and through the confusion of which he has been led to enter into a controversy or to fall into error. Against a distinction one can defend oneself in the same way as against a restriction (§ 506).",[]],"meier:508":["7) The attacking party may refute the universality of the contested propositions by instances. An instance (*instantia*) is an exception to a proposition, from which it is sufficiently evident that the proposition is not universally true. Against an instance one can defend oneself only in one of the following ways: by accepting it; by showing that it does not apply; by so restricting the contested proposition that it does not apply; or by establishing the universality of the contested proposition without regard to the instance.",[]],"meier:509":["8) The party attacked employs retaliation (*retorsio*) when he shows that what the opponent has derived from the contested proposition by apagogic proof (§ 504) follows rather from its opposite. The attacking party can then defend himself only by showing that his opponent has committed an error in his proof.",[]],"meier:510":["9) When the refutation is complete, one should seek to instil in the opponent an aversion to the error, by showing that it is dangerous, harmful and ridiculous, though without violating the duties one owes to the opponent. Against this attack one can defend oneself only by showing that the opponent views our opinion from the wrong side. Through a correct observance of these rules refutations become at the same time practical (§ 489).",[]],"meier:511":["If one derives evil and absurd consequences from the contested proposition, not in order to convince the opponent of his error, but in order to injure him in some way or other, one is a consequence-monger [Folgenmacher, Consequenzenmacher] (*consequentiarius*). Consequence-mongering is not necessarily an offence against logic; but it is always a sin, and therefore deserves to be abhorred by all honest people.",[]],"meier:512":["The defence against a consequence-monger is entitled the apology [Verantwortung] (*apologia*). It may be conducted with logical weapons, if the consequence-monger has committed offences against logic (§ 511); and one must then at the same time show him his want of understanding [Unverstand] and his evil heart, though without oneself resorting to consequence-mongering. Otherwise one must avail oneself of the weapons of the law, and logic then has nothing further to observe in the matter.",[]],"meier:513":["Although learned controversies have their use (§ 178), a learned man must nevertheless 1) not make a trade of refuting others; 2) not start a controversy of his own over any trifle; 3) conduct important and necessary refutations with complete thoroughness; and 4) not defend himself against every attack.",[]],"meier:514":["Two contending parties dispute with one another (*disputatio formaliter sumta*) when the opposite [of a proposition] is set forth and judged in a learned manner, and when both these things take place orally and in the presence of both parties. He who sets forth the opposite in a learned manner is the opponent (*opponens*). He who judges it, that is, who detects what is false and uncertain in it and in its proof, is the respondent (*respondens*). In order, then, that the disputation may not become disorderly and altogether too prolix, the opponent alone must bring proofs, and the respondent must do nothing more than judge the proofs.",[]],"meier:515":["Since the speeches delivered in the course of a disputation do not fall within the province of logic, it is clear that the opponent opens the disputation. He must then 1) determine the question in dispute (§§ 500, 501). If he does not understand the respondent's opinion, he must have it explained to him by the respondent. 2) He should attack the respondent according to one of the rules set forth in §§ 502-510. 3) For the sake of brevity and distinctness he should present his proofs in formal syllogisms (§ 399); and 4) for the same reason he should employ the analytic method (§ 423). The Socratic and Platonic method renders disputation too prolix and burdensome (§ 430), unless indeed one should find oneself compelled by the former to keep the other party to the question in dispute.",[]],"meier:516":["The respondent takes up the argument (*assumere argumentum*) when he repeats the opponent's objection. This is useful in order that he may consider it properly, and so be able to answer it soundly. For the sake of brevity the respondent must do nothing further than show where the opponent has committed an error, either in the matter or in the form of his proof (§ 502). He must then demand that the opponent either alter his syllogism, or prove the premiss which seems to the respondent false or uncertain.",[]],"meier:517":["The respondent's supporter (*praeses*) is to prevent the respondent from conceding the opposite [of his thesis] before it has been sufficiently proved. If, therefore, the respondent can find nothing more, or nothing apt, to answer, the *praeses* must take over his office (§ 516). Of learned writings.",[]],"meier:518":["The account of past events is history (*historia*). Learned writings present either history or dogmatic truths (§ 104). The former are historical writings (*scripta erudita historica*), the latter dogmatic writings (*scripta dogmatica*). These last present either a common or a learned knowledge of dogmatic truths (§§ 18, 21). The former are historico-dogmatic writings (*scriptum historico-dogmaticum*); the latter are systematic writings (*scriptum systematicum*), which are scientific writings (*scriptum scientificum*) when they present a science (§ 434). Every writing is, in respect of its content, either very extensive or very brief. The former is a large work (*systema*), the latter an abridgement (*compendium*).",[]],"meier:519":["History narrates either things belonging to nature and art, in so far as neither the free actions of men nor their more immediate effects are taken into consideration; or it narrates the free actions of men, together with everything that stands in closer connection with them. The former is the history of nature and art (*historia naturae et artis*), the latter history simply so called (*historia stricte dicta*). To the latter belong: 1) political history (*historia politica*), which narrates the civil and political actions of men; 2) ecclesiastical history (*historia ecclesiastica*), which narrates the fortunes of the churches, and those actions of men which have a notable influence upon them; 3) private history (*historia privata*), which narrates the course of life of individual persons, in so far as it has no notable influence upon the state, the church or learning; 4) learned history (*historia litteraria*), which narrates the events of the learned world, and is a history either of the disciplines, or of the learned, or of learned writings.",[]],"meier:520":["A historical writing must be 1) exhaustive [ausführlich] (§§ 481, 482), neither too extensive nor too brief. It may therefore be exhaustive for one purpose and for one reader, too extensive for another purpose and another reader, and too brief for a third purpose and a third reader. In the course of time every history becomes too brief. 2) Important, both as regards its whole content and as regards what is narrated of that content (§ 483). 3) Correct (§ 484). 4) Distinct (§ 485); hence pictures and engravings are often necessary. 5) Thorough (§ 488); it must consequently prove everything, according to the rules of §§ 206-215. 6) Practical or pragmatic (*historia pragmatica*) (§ 489). 7) Methodical (§§ 487, 432).",[]],"meier:521":["If history is to be at once pragmatic and exhaustive, it must be so presented that a sufficiently practical purpose is attained (§ 520). Now everything actual in this world, in so far as it can be known by us men, aims not only at the glory of God but also at our happiness. The historian must therefore seek to attain both these purposes through history. In natural history in particular one must have a twofold purpose (§ 519): 1) to come to know better the perfections of God from natural things; and 2) to impart to readers distinct concepts of natural things, and thereby to prepare the way to a learned knowledge of them.",[]],"meier:522":["History in the narrower sense (§ 519) redounds to the glory of God when from the events of men one comes to know the divine government of the human race, and the perfections which God reveals therein. To the happiness of men there redound: 1) civil history, when one can learn from it political prudence in the affairs of war and peace; 2) ecclesiastical history, when one can learn from it the prudence needed to govern the church, and when it sets forth the marks of the true and the false church; 3) learned history, when it advances learning; 4) private history, when one can learn from it human prudence in private life.",[]],"meier:523":["Historico-dogmatic writings are not learned writings (§ 518). If, however, they are exhaustive, important, intelligible, correct and practical, they are very useful to people who cannot or will not become learned, and also to children who are to be introduced to the sciences, in order to give them a foretaste of learning.",[]],"meier:524":["A systematic book must be 1) exhaustive (§§ 481, 482). It must therefore contain either everything known of a doctrine at the time the book was written, or as much of it as the author's particular purpose requires. The most exhaustive writings may in the course of time cease to be exhaustive. One must therefore carefully distinguish the exhaustiveness of a dogmatic book in respect of the time, the author and the reader. It must be 2) important (§ 483); 3) correct (§ 484), in that not only are all the thoughts it contains true, but each of them also conforms to the rules of logic; 4) distinct and intelligible (§ 485); 5) thorough (§ 488), and it must therefore be written according to the learned method and in a connected manner; 6) practical (§ 489); 7) methodical (§§ 414-438).",[]],"meier:525":["Compilation [Zusammenschmieren] (*compilatio*) consists in an author's writing down together everything he finds here and there on the subject of which he intends to write, without properly connecting it. Although a compiled work may be useful to other learned men, it remains, as regards its author, a wretched work. A writing is plagiarised [zusammengestohlen] (*plagiarius*) when the author gives himself out as the inventor of thoughts which he has learned from others.",[]],"meier:526":["He who makes an abridgement of a large work (*epitomator*), by omitting from it everything superfluous for his own smaller purpose, may write a very useful and necessary book, provided only that he has chosen his purpose rationally (§ 518). Of the character of a learned man.",[]],"meier:527":["The character of a learned man (*character eruditi*) consists in the sum of those of his marks [Merkmale] which are the proximate grounds and causes of learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:528":["The general character of a learned man (*generalis character eruditi*) is the character of a learned man in so far as it contains the proximate grounds of learning in general; the special character (*character eruditi specialis*), in so far as it contains the proximate grounds of this or that kind, and of this or that part, of learning.",[]],"meier:529":["The first element of the general character of a learned man consists in the learned natural disposition [Naturell] (*natura erudita*), that is, in that proportion of all the powers of the soul by virtue of which a man is fitted and inclined for learned knowledge (§ 528).",[]],"meier:530":["To the learned natural disposition belongs 1) mother wit [Mutterwitz], the learned head (*ingenium eruditum*), that is, the proportion of the cognitive powers by which a man is fitted for learned knowledge. To this belong: a) reason (*ratio*), the faculty of distinctly discerning the connection of things (§ 21), which must by nature be disposed to become sound, wide-ranging, strong, thorough and beautiful; b) the understanding (*intellectus*), the faculty of distinct knowledge (§§ 21, 17), which must by nature be disposed to become wide-ranging, profound, pure and beautiful. Consequently mother wit requires great attention, reflection [Nachdenken], deliberation [Überlegung] and abstraction (§ 142). 2) A beautiful mind [schöner Geist] (*ingenium pulchrum*), the proportion of the cognitive powers by virtue of which a man is disposed to beautiful thinking (§ 32).",[]],"meier:531":["To the learned natural disposition belongs 2) the learned temperament (*temperamentum eruditum*), that is, the proportion of the appetitive powers and their agreement with mother wit, by virtue of which a man is not only inclined to acquire learned knowledge, but also exerts his cognitive powers as he ought, so as to produce the most perfect learned knowledge (§ 529).",[]],"meier:532":["The learned natural disposition is a mere gift of fortune, which must be inborn in a man; but without it it is impossible, by any art whatsoever, to attain learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:533":["Mother wit is entitled innate natural logic (*logica naturalis connata*); the science of learned knowledge and of learned exposition, on the other hand, is entitled school wit [Schulwitz] or artificial logic (*logica artificialis*). The indistinct knowledge of the rules of mother wit, together with the facility in observing them, which is acquired through the mere use of mother wit, is entitled acquired natural logic (*logica naturalis acquisita theoretica et practica*), and is reckoned as mother wit in the wider sense. Mother wit does not contradict school wit; it is indispensable both to the theory and to the practice of the latter. Outside the disciplines it is also sufficient by itself. Without school wit, however, no learned knowledge can be attained. School wit improves mother wit, and when great school wit is combined with great mother wit, a man can attain much greater heights than through the best mother wit taken by itself.",[]],"meier:534":["He who wishes to become a great scholar must follow nature. He should devote himself to learned knowledge only if he possesses the learned natural disposition, and only in so far as he possesses it (§ 532).",[]],"meier:535":["One can know whether one possesses the learned natural disposition: 1) if one has a rational inclination towards learned knowledge, because its perfections give us pleasure; 2) if things go easily with us whenever we think in a learned manner; 3) if one finds by trial that one can actually think in a learned manner.",[]],"meier:536":["The second element of the general character of a learned man consists in the facilities [Fertigkeiten] which he gradually acquires through exercises (§ 527). To the facilities of a learned man belong: 1) The extension of understanding and reason (*extensio intellectus et rationis*), the facility of knowing many things distinctly and in a manifold connection, and of knowing many clear marks of each thing (§ 25). 2) The strength of reason and understanding (*intensio intellectus et rationis*), the facility of knowing things with a high degree of completeness and mathematical certainty (§§ 27, 28, 29). 3) The facility of thinking beautifully (§ 32). 4) Greatness of heart (§ 91) (*magnitudo pectoris*), the facility of thinking and acting in conformity with the learned temperament (§ 531), which presupposes the facility of thinking only in a great and practical manner (§§ 26, 30).",[]],"meier:537":["The learned exercises are A) the more general (*exercitia generaliora*), that is, frequent repetitions of learned meditation in general. To these belongs 1) learned naturalising [Naturalisiren], that is, the exercises undertaken without art, which constitute acquired natural logic (§ 433).",[]],"meier:538":["2) The more perfect, regular and artificial exercises (*exercitia oculatiora, logica artificialis practica*), that is, frequent observance of the rules of artificial logic. To this end: a) one should learn a good artificial logic; b) one should seek to practise all its rules; c) when one has produced and written down a series of thoughts according to the logical rules, one should hold them against these rules, and examine where one has observed them and where not. Through these exercises one must at last reach the point of observing the rules of artificial logic in thinking and speaking without being conscious of them.",[]],"meier:539":["B) The special learned exercises, or study [Studiren] (*exercitia erudita specialiora*), consist in all the activities by which learned knowledge arises, and is improved, in him who undertakes them. Six exercises belong here.",[]],"meier:540":["I) Learning from oral instruction. Since this exercise is the easiest and the safest, one must begin with it. He who would become learned through this exercise 1) must have prepared his head for learning through the lower and the fine sciences [schönen Wissenschaften]. 2) He must choose a teacher at least moderately capable, from whose oral instruction he can hope to acquire good learned knowledge. 3) He must pay due attention to the oral instruction, so that he may rightly understand the teacher and his exposition. 4) He must meditate thoroughly upon everything the teacher presents, or at least upon the chief points of his exposition (§§ 436, 437). 5) He must judge logically (*logice diiudicare*) everything he has heard, that is, seek to know what logical perfections or imperfections are to be found in it. He must therefore examine to what species and genus of learned knowledge that belongs which he intends to judge. He must call to mind the logical rules of this species or genus, and, by comparing the object with these rules, seek to know whether or not it conforms to them.",[]],"meier:541":["II) The reading of learned writings. To this end a) one must set before oneself a rational end for the sake of which one intends to read learned writings. A student must therefore read books: 1) in order to repeat a discipline already learned; 2) in order to improve our learned knowledge more and more; 3) in order to learn a part of learning not yet understood; 4) in order to exercise oneself in observing the rules of logic. b) One must choose a book fitted to further this purpose. Consequently 1) one must not read all books just as they come to hand. 2) At the outset one should read only such books as, after logical judgment, one has found fitted to further our purpose. 3) When one already possesses a facility in learned thinking, one may read with profit even books full of defects and errors. c) One must seek rightly to understand the book and its author. d) One must meditate thoroughly upon each thought presented in the book, and judge it logically, as in § 540, nos. 4 and 5, in order that one may learn rightly to discern what is presented.",[]],"meier:542":["The profitable reading of learned writings is furthered by observing the following rules. 1) One should read with the greatest attention, admitting no extraneous thoughts. 2) One should read the book through from the beginning, if it is written systematically. 3) One should never interrupt one's reading in a closely connected subject. 4) One should read the book through making haste slowly, neither too quickly nor too slowly. 5) One must not grow weary of reading before one has come to the end. 6) One must not read too many books at once, especially books of differing content. 7) One should begin with the shorter abridgements, proceed to the larger works, and finally read writings which treat particular subjects exhaustively. 8) One should read the writings belonging to a principal part of learning according to the synthetic method.",[]],"meier:543":["The learning of learned knowledge is greatly furthered, partly if at the outset one learns from one teacher only the first principles of the branch of learning to which one has devoted oneself, and partly if one at once uses and puts into practice the truths one has learned.",[]],"meier:544":["III) Frequent repetition (*repetitio*), or recollection of what one has learned. This requires the observance of the following rules. 1) One must not turn this repetition into mere learning by heart. 2) In repeating, one must meditate upon and judge each thought just as if one were learning it for the first time, although this always proceeds more quickly and easily. 3) One should repeat the truths learned according to the synthetic method according to the analytic and inferential method (§§ 423, 428). 4) One should repeat the repetition often, with some variation. 5) One should keep on learning more, and put the truths learned into practice. 6) If in repeating one writes down one's meditations, this exercise is greatly furthered. 7) One should set forth the truths learned, in writing or orally, in a learned manner. 8) One should converse with others about the truths learned.",[]],"meier:545":["The repetition and learning of learned knowledge is greatly furthered if one writes down briefly, and with rational selection, what one fears one may forget, and what will at the same time bring the rest back to memory.",[]],"meier:546":["IV) Investigation (*investigatio*), or the rational imitation of other learned men in their learned thinking and exposition. To this end 1) one should choose a great scholar as one's model. 2) One should examine how he manages to think so learnedly, so thoroughly, so orderly, etc. 3) One should examine how he discovered the truths, or how they could be discovered if they had not yet been found. 4) One should seek to think and speak in the same way in similar cases.",[]],"meier:547":["V) One should present the disciplines to others in a learned manner, either orally or in writing. In other words, learned exposition is an exercise in learned knowledge; for by teaching we learn.",[]],"meier:548":["VI) The invention of new truths (*inventio*): when we know something in a learned manner without learning it from other men, whether we are the first to know it or others have known it before us. This exercise is the most difficult, and must be reserved till last.",[]],"meier:549":["An autodidact [Autodidaktus] is a learned man who has become learned without a teacher, whether he has invented his whole learning (§ 548) or has merely enjoyed no oral instruction. A rational man chooses the easiest and safest way, provided all this is otherwise possible for him: he first seeks to learn from oral instruction, then reads books, and employs his inventive power rather in discovering entirely new truths than truths long since known.",[]],"meier:550":["Just as it is a fault to seek to become learned without mother wit (§ 534), so it is a negligent manner of learned thinking (*negligens eruditionis genus et impolitum*) when one does not properly combine art with mother wit (§ 533).",[]],"meier:551":["The affected and forced manner of learned thinking (*affectatum et coactum eruditionis genus*) arises when school wit is badly combined with mother wit: 1) when the rules of artificial logic are false and groundless; 2) when its correct rules are badly applied, being suited either not to the truths or not to the natural disposition of the learned man.",[]],"meier:552":["Knowledge and exposition which are merely learned constitute the dry, jejune, rough, scholastic and pedantic manner of learning (*macilentum, ieiunum, pedanticum, scholasticum eruditionis genus*). To avoid it, a student must daily devote some time, in his hours of recreation, to the fine sciences.",[]],"meier:553":["The third element of the general character of a learned man consists in learned diligence (*diligentia erudita*), that is, in such exertion of all acquired learned facilities as is required on each occasion if one is to think and expound in a learned manner with a certain degree of perfection (§ 527). To this end ten rules must be observed.",[]],"meier:554":["1) A student must above all mark out the horizon of his learned knowledge as correctly as possible, so that he may always know beforehand to what he is to direct his learned diligence. And in order that this horizon be taken neither too narrow nor too wide, one must extend it gradually as our powers and skills increase.",[]],"meier:555":["2) A student must judge his powers as exactly as possible, [to determine] whether they suffice for learning in general, for the part of it to which he applies himself, and for the knowledge of this or that truth. To this end a) he must test himself as to whether he possesses the learned natural disposition (§§ 529-535). b) One should turn that with respect to which one wishes to test one's powers into a problem (§§ 328-339), and examine whether one has power enough actually to effect the solution. c) In judging one's powers one should guard against the prejudice of excessive confidence and of excessive diffidence (§ 170). d) One should test one's powers by an honest trial. e) One should let oneself be judged by others.",[]],"meier:556":["3) In studying one must not apply too much diligence, nor direct one's learned diligence to things found above, below or outside the horizon of our learned knowledge (§ 554).",[]],"meier:557":["4) Nor must one apply too little diligence, but as much as is required to carry out all the learned exercises properly, and gradually to study through the whole horizon of our learned knowledge.",[]],"meier:558":["5) One must begin to study as early as possible: what one intends to do, one should do soon.",[]],"meier:559":["6) One must, if it is otherwise possible for us, study and think in a learned manner daily. He who goes no further forward goes backward.",[]],"meier:560":["7) In the midst of the course of study one must always make haste slowly. That one may not hasten too much, one must not leave a learned occupation until one has satisfied all the rules of logic in it. But that one may not be too slow, one must duly kindle the desire to study.",[]],"meier:561":["8) One must never cease to learn, either always learning something new or learning to know better what one has already learned.",[]],"meier:562":["9) One must seek constantly to improve the learning already acquired, and to remove its remaining defects and errors.",[]],"meier:563":["10) With study one must combine daily experience, intercourse with the respectable world, and the use of the very things upon which one meditates in a learned manner, lest one live like a mere bookworm [gelehrter Wurm] on the dust of the schools and dream of Platonic republics.",[]]};
